(window.QUIZ_CHUNKS=window.QUIZ_CHUNKS||{})["e2a.ad4aff005b.js"]={"13":[{"id":"sdl13_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Evaluate the passive permeability differences of the thin limbs of the loop of Henle, explaining why the thin descending limb is highly water-permeable (concentrating segment) while the thin ascending limb is water-impermeable but highly permeable to NaCl.","batch":1,"stem":"Which property of the thin descending limb makes it a concentrating segment of the loop of Henle?","choices":{"A":"Active Na+ reabsorption raises the luminal solute concentration directly","B":"Active ATP-dependent solute secretion into the lumen raises its osmolarity along the segment","C":"High water permeability lets water leave the lumen as fluid moves toward the papilla","D":"Impermeability to NaCl traps solute within the lumen as fluid descends","E":"Basolateral Na+-K+-ATPase drives active solute uptake from the interstitium"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The thin descending limb is highly permeable to water but has little active transport capacity. As tubular fluid descends into the increasingly hyperosmotic medullary interstitium, water passively exits, concentrating the remaining luminal fluid.","boardPrep":"Step 1: thin descending limb = water OUT, passive; thin ascending limb = NaCl OUT, passive, water-impermeable. Board vignettes test this permeability split directly.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 -> Obj 1 — the thin-limb permeability split that sets up the countercurrent multiplier","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl13_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Evaluate the passive permeability differences of the thin limbs of the loop of Henle, explaining why the thin descending limb is highly water-permeable (concentrating segment) while the thin ascending limb is water-impermeable but highly permeable to NaCl.","batch":1,"stem":"How does the thin ascending limb differ from the thin descending limb in its transport properties?","choices":{"A":"It is impermeable to both water and NaCl, serving as a structural conduit","B":"It is impermeable to water but highly permeable to NaCl, which diffuses out passively","C":"It actively secretes NaCl into the lumen using a basolateral cotransporter","D":"It is permeable to water but impermeable to NaCl, mirroring the descending limb in reverse","E":"It reabsorbs water and NaCl together in a fixed isosmotic ratio"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The thin ascending limb is a passive segment: it lacks water permeability but allows NaCl to diffuse out of the lumen down its concentration gradient, established as fluid concentrated on the way down the descending limb now dilutes on the way back up.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: this passive NaCl exit (no ATP required) contrasts sharply with the thick ascending limb's active, energy-dependent NKCC2 transport tested in Objective 2.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 -> Obj 1 — the thin-limb permeability split that sets up the countercurrent multiplier","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl13_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Evaluate the passive permeability differences of the thin limbs of the loop of Henle, explaining why the thin descending limb is highly water-permeable (concentrating segment) while the thin ascending limb is water-impermeable but highly permeable to NaCl.","batch":1,"stem":"A short loop of Henle nephron has an unusually short thin descending limb. What direct consequence follows for the tubular fluid reaching the bend of the loop?","choices":{"A":"The fluid is less concentrated than with more segment length available for water loss","B":"The fluid is more concentrated because less time allows less solute back-diffusion","C":"Urea secretion into the lumen increases to compensate for the shorter segment","D":"NaCl reabsorption in the segment increases to offset the reduced water loss","E":"The fluid osmolarity becomes equal to plasma whatever the segment length"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Concentration in the descending limb depends on progressive water loss into the increasingly hypertonic interstitium, so a shorter segment gives water less distance and less time to exit and the fluid at the bend is less concentrated. This fits the Costanzo statement that the long loops of juxtamedullary nephrons are essential for concentrating urine.","boardPrep":"Step 1: nephron heterogeneity (short-looped vs. long-looped) is a classic distractor topic — long juxtamedullary loops are essential for concentrating urine, while short loops contribute less.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 -> Obj 1 — the thin-limb permeability split that sets up the countercurrent multiplier","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl13_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Deconstruct the active, load-dependent Na+ reabsorption mechanism of the thick ascending limb (luminal NKCC2, basolateral Na+-K+ ATPase), and analyze how electrogenic K+ recycling via apical ROMK channels generates a +7 mV lumen-positive potential to drive paracellular divalent cation (Ca2+ and Mg2+) reabsorption.","batch":1,"stem":"What ions are transported into thick ascending limb cells by the luminal NKCC2 cotransporter?","choices":{"A":"One Na+ and one Cl- ion in a simple electroneutral pair","B":"Two Na+ ions and one Cl- ion in a fixed ratio","C":"One Na+, one K+, and two Cl- ions together","D":"One K+ ion and two Cl- ions, with Na+ entering separately","E":"One Na+, one K+, and one Cl- ion in a 1:1:1 ratio"},"correct":"C","explanation":"NKCC2 (the Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransporter) moves one sodium, one potassium, and two chloride ions together across the luminal membrane, powered by the low intracellular sodium concentration that basolateral Na+-K+-ATPase maintains. A one-to-one sodium-chloride pair is the Na+-Cl- cotransporter of the early distal tubule, and a 1:1:1 ratio undercounts chloride, which NKCC2 carries two of.","boardPrep":"Step 1: NKCC2 stoichiometry (1 Na+ : 1 K+ : 2 Cl-) is directly testable and is the molecular target of loop diuretics in Objective 3.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 -> Obj 2 — the TAL's NKCC2/ROMK machinery and the +7 mV potential it creates","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Deconstruct the active, load-dependent Na+ reabsorption mechanism of the thick ascending limb (luminal NKCC2, basolateral Na+-K+ ATPase), and analyze how electrogenic K+ recycling via apical ROMK channels generates a +7 mV lumen-positive potential to drive paracellular divalent cation (Ca2+ and Mg2+) reabsorption.","batch":1,"stem":"What generates the +7 mV lumen-positive transepithelial potential difference across the thick ascending limb?","choices":{"A":"Basolateral Na+-K+-ATPase directly pumps positive charge into the lumen","B":"NKCC2 itself carries net positive charge across the apical membrane into the lumen","C":"A dedicated apical proton pump acidifies and electropositively charges the lumen","D":"K+ that entered via NKCC2 recycles back into the lumen through apical ROMK channels","E":"Paracellular calcium movement establishes the potential before transport begins"},"correct":"D","explanation":"A portion of the potassium that enters the cell via NKCC2 diffuses back out through apical ROMK channels into the lumen. Because this recycled K+ carries positive charge back into the tubular fluid without a matching anion, it establishes the lumen-positive potential.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: this +7 mV potential is the driving force for paracellular Ca2+ and Mg2+ reabsorption — losing it (loop diuretics) wastes both cations.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 -> Obj 2 — the TAL's NKCC2/ROMK machinery and the +7 mV potential it creates","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Deconstruct the active, load-dependent Na+ reabsorption mechanism of the thick ascending limb (luminal NKCC2, basolateral Na+-K+ ATPase), and analyze how electrogenic K+ recycling via apical ROMK channels generates a +7 mV lumen-positive potential to drive paracellular divalent cation (Ca2+ and Mg2+) reabsorption.","batch":1,"stem":"By what route are Ca2+ and Mg2+ reabsorbed across the thick ascending limb?","choices":{"A":"Paracellularly, driven by the lumen-positive potential repelling the positively charged cations","B":"Transcellularly through a dedicated apical calcium channel, mirroring divalent transport in the distal tubule","C":"By active cotransport with sodium across the apical NKCC2 transporter, using the same carrier that moves monovalent ions","D":"Through basolateral calcium-sensing receptors, pulling the ions directly from the lumen into the cell","E":"By facilitated diffusion coupled to urea movement, following the same channel used for urea recycling"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The lumen-positive potential generated by ROMK-mediated K+ recycling repels the positively charged divalent cations, pushing Ca2+ and Mg2+ through the paracellular tight-junction pathway into the interstitium and blood. NKCC2 carries only Na+, K+ and Cl-, not divalent cations; the lumen-positive potential it helps create is what pushes Ca2+ and Mg2+ between the cells.","boardPrep":"Step 1: paracellular, potential-driven divalent cation reabsorption explains why any drug or condition that abolishes the +7 mV potential wastes both calcium and magnesium together.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 -> Obj 2 — the TAL's NKCC2/ROMK machinery and the +7 mV potential it creates","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Analyze the pharmacological mechanism of loop diuretics (furosemide, bumetanide, ethacrynic acid) binding to the Cl- site of the NKCC2 cotransporter, predicting their downstream impact on sodium excretion, divalent cation wasting (hypercalciuria/hypermagnesuria), and why water-impermeability designates the TAL as the \"diluting segment\" of the nephron.","batch":1,"stem":"At what site on the NKCC2 cotransporter do loop diuretics such as furosemide bind?","choices":{"A":"The sodium-binding site of the cotransporter","B":"The chloride-binding site of the cotransporter","C":"An allosteric site on the basolateral Na+-K+-ATPase pump","D":"The apical ROMK channel pore directly","E":"A regulatory site restricted to the potassium-binding domain"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Loop diuretics bind the Cl- site of NKCC2, blocking the cotransporter and preventing Na+, K+, and Cl- entry together, which shuts down thick ascending limb reabsorption at its source. ROMK is a separate K+ channel that recycles potassium into the lumen; loop diuretics act on the cotransporter, not on that channel pore.","boardPrep":"Step 1: furosemide, bumetanide, and ethacrynic acid all act at this same chloride site — a frequently tested pharmacology fact paired with their divalent-cation-wasting side effects.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 -> Obj 3 — loop diuretic pharmacology built on that same NKCC2 mechanism","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Analyze the pharmacological mechanism of loop diuretics (furosemide, bumetanide, ethacrynic acid) binding to the Cl- site of the NKCC2 cotransporter, predicting their downstream impact on sodium excretion, divalent cation wasting (hypercalciuria/hypermagnesuria), and why water-impermeability designates the TAL as the \"diluting segment\" of the nephron.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient on chronic furosemide therapy is found to have both hypercalciuria and hypermagnesuria. What single mechanism links both findings?","choices":{"A":"Furosemide blocks a shared calcium-magnesium cotransporter on the apical membrane","B":"Increased distal sodium delivery dilutes urinary calcium and magnesium","C":"Furosemide raises parathyroid hormone secretion independent of any electrical effect","D":"Loss of the lumen-positive potential removes the driving force for paracellular cations","E":"The drug increases glomerular filtration of both cations without any tubular effect"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Because NKCC2 blockade eliminates the K+ recycling that generates the +7 mV lumen-positive potential, the paracellular driving force for both Ca2+ and Mg2+ reabsorption is lost together, producing combined hypercalciuria and hypermagnesuria. There is no shared apical calcium-magnesium cotransporter in this segment, since both cations move paracellularly; dilution by extra urine flow would lower their concentration, not raise the total amount excreted; and the losses follow from the lost lumen-positive voltage, not from a parathyroid hormone effect.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: loop diuretics wasting BOTH divalent cations (vs. thiazides sparing calcium) is a classic diuretic-comparison board question.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 -> Obj 3 — loop diuretic pharmacology built on that same NKCC2 mechanism","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Analyze the pharmacological mechanism of loop diuretics (furosemide, bumetanide, ethacrynic acid) binding to the Cl- site of the NKCC2 cotransporter, predicting their downstream impact on sodium excretion, divalent cation wasting (hypercalciuria/hypermagnesuria), and why water-impermeability designates the TAL as the \"diluting segment\" of the nephron.","batch":1,"stem":"Why is the thick ascending limb referred to as the \"diluting segment\" of the nephron?","choices":{"A":"It secretes additional free water into the lumen faster than any other segment","B":"It reabsorbs equal fractions of solute and water, keeping the fluid isosmotic along its length","C":"It is a segment whose reabsorption is set by ADH levels","D":"It reabsorbs water preferentially over solute, concentrating the fluid as it moves along","E":"It reabsorbs a large fraction of filtered solute while remaining impermeable to water"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Because the thick ascending limb actively reabsorbs NaCl but cannot reabsorb water alongside it, the tubular fluid leaving the segment has a lower solute concentration and lower osmolarity than plasma, which is the definition of dilution.","boardPrep":"Step 1: \"diluting segment\" pairs with the early distal tubule (Obj 1 of SDL 14) as the two water-impermeable, solute-reabsorbing segments that generate dilute tubular fluid before the collecting duct.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 -> Obj 3 — loop diuretic pharmacology built on that same NKCC2 mechanism","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Correlate active solute transport and high oxygen consumption in the TAL with its susceptibility to ischemic Acute Tubular Necrosis (ATN), evaluating the protective vasodilatory role of local renal prostaglandins (PGE2 and PGI2) and the clinical danger of administering NSAIDs in hypoperfused states.","batch":1,"stem":"Why is the thick ascending limb especially vulnerable to ischemic acute tubular necrosis during renal hypoperfusion?","choices":{"A":"Its active NKCC2 transport creates high oxygen demand amid low medullary oxygen tension","B":"It lacks any local vasodilatory mechanism that could protect it during hypoperfusion","C":"It lacks mitochondria and so relies on anaerobic glycolysis to meet its substantial energy needs","D":"Its epithelium regenerates more slowly than most other nephron segments after injury","E":"It receives essentially no blood supply under normal physiologic conditions"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Active Na+ reabsorption via NKCC2 requires substantial ATP, giving the thick ascending limb a high oxygen demand. Combined with the medulla's naturally low baseline oxygen tension, this makes the segment particularly susceptible to ischemic injury when renal perfusion falls. The segment does have local protection, since prostaglandins dilate the afferent arteriole and inhibit its Na+-K+-2Cl- transport, and the medulla is perfused by the vasa recta, just at low flow.","boardPrep":"Step 1: TAL vulnerability to ischemic ATN is a recurring theme linking renal hemodynamics (SDL 8) to tubular injury — a classic two-objective bridge question.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 -> Obj 4 — the TAL's oxygen cost and its prostaglandin-dependent protection","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Correlate active solute transport and high oxygen consumption in the TAL with its susceptibility to ischemic Acute Tubular Necrosis (ATN), evaluating the protective vasodilatory role of local renal prostaglandins (PGE2 and PGI2) and the clinical danger of administering NSAIDs in hypoperfused states.","batch":1,"stem":"How do local renal prostaglandins such as PGE2 and PGI2 protect the thick ascending limb during states of reduced renal perfusion?","choices":{"A":"They directly increase mitochondrial ATP production, supplying extra energy for transport","B":"They constrict the efferent arteriole, protecting filtration independent of tubular transport","C":"They stimulate additional Na+ reabsorption, which raises the segment's oxygen demand further","D":"They block ROMK channels, removing the paracellular pathway for divalent cations","E":"They inhibit NKCC2-mediated Na+ reabsorption, lowering the segment's own oxygen consumption"},"correct":"E","explanation":"By inhibiting Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransport, local prostaglandins reduce the amount of active transport the thick ascending limb must perform, which lowers its oxygen demand at a time when oxygen delivery is already compromised, protecting the segment from ischemic injury. Prostaglandins do not block ROMK; their protective tubular action is inhibition of Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransport.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: prostaglandins also dilate afferent arterioles to protect GFR during hemorrhage — the same renal protective system operates at both the vascular and tubular level.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 -> Obj 4 — the TAL's oxygen cost and its prostaglandin-dependent protection","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Correlate active solute transport and high oxygen consumption in the TAL with its susceptibility to ischemic Acute Tubular Necrosis (ATN), evaluating the protective vasodilatory role of local renal prostaglandins (PGE2 and PGI2) and the clinical danger of administering NSAIDs in hypoperfused states.","batch":1,"stem":"Why are NSAIDs particularly dangerous in a patient with hypoperfusion from hemorrhage or dehydration?","choices":{"A":"They directly constrict the thick ascending limb epithelium and cause mechanical obstruction","B":"They block the prostaglandin synthesis that protects renal flow and lowers oxygen demand","C":"They stimulate excessive NKCC2 activity and worsen solute reabsorption beyond normal limits","D":"They increase renal blood flow beyond what the kidney can safely filter","E":"They block aldosterone receptors and cause acute hyperkalemia independent of perfusion"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In a hypoperfused kidney, protective vasodilatory prostaglandins are maintaining renal blood flow and reducing the thick ascending limb's oxygen consumption. NSAIDs inhibit prostaglandin synthesis, removing both protections at once and precipitating acute kidney injury. NSAIDs remove the prostaglandin brake on Na+-K+-2Cl- transport, restoring normal Na+ reabsorption and O2 use rather than driving the carrier beyond its normal limits.","boardPrep":"Step 1: NSAID-induced AKI in volume-depleted patients is one of the highest-yield pharmacology-physiology crossover questions on the exam.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 -> Obj 4 — the TAL's oxygen cost and its prostaglandin-dependent protection","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q13","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Evaluate the passive permeability differences of the thin limbs of the loop of Henle, explaining why the thin descending limb is highly water-permeable (concentrating segment) while the thin ascending limb is water-impermeable but highly permeable to NaCl.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old man undergoes nephrectomy for a small renal tumor and consents to a micropuncture study of the normal pole. Tubular fluid is collected at the end of the proximal tubule and at the start of the distal tubule, and segmental water reabsorption is calculated from [TF/P]inulin. Electron microscopy of the thin loop segments shows flat cells without a brush border and with few mitochondria. Which result is expected for water reabsorption across the loop of Henle?","choices":{"A":"About 65%, mostly in the thin descending limb via AQP1","B":"About 5%, mostly in the thick ascending limb via AQP2","C":"About 20%, mostly in the thin descending limb via AQP1","D":"About 35%, mostly in the thin ascending limb via AQP2","E":"About 10%, mostly in the thick ascending limb via AQP2"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Guyton states that about 20% of the filtered water is reabsorbed in the loop of Henle and that almost all of it occurs in the thin descending limb, passively through AQP-1 water channels; the thin limbs have no brush border, few mitochondria, and minimal metabolic activity, so their transport is passive. About two-thirds of filtered water is reabsorbed upstream in the proximal tubule, so 65% describes the proximal tubule rather than the loop. The ascending limb, both thin and thick portions, is virtually impermeable to water, so neither the thin ascending limb nor the thick ascending limb accounts for loop water reabsorption. AQP2 is the ADH-regulated channel of principal cells in the late distal tubule and collecting duct, not a loop channel.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Loop of Henle: about 20% of filtered water (thin descending limb, AQP1) and about 25% of filtered Na+, Cl-, and K+ (mostly the thick ascending limb); the thin limbs lack brush borders and have few mitochondria, so their transport is passive. COMLEX: The water-impermeable ascending limb is what lets the loop dilute tubular fluid and build the medullary gradient.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 1 — the thin limb permeabilities feed countercurrent multiplication of the corticopapillary gradient","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl13_q14","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Evaluate the passive permeability differences of the thin limbs of the loop of Henle, explaining why the thin descending limb is highly water-permeable (concentrating segment) while the thin ascending limb is water-impermeable but highly permeable to NaCl.","batch":2,"stem":"A 24-year-old man is evaluated for polyuria. Blood pressure is 120/76 mm Hg. Serum sodium is 141 mEq/L (reference range: 136–146 mEq/L), and glucose is 88 mg/dL (reference range: 70–100 mg/dL). After overnight water deprivation, his urine osmolality reaches 1,150 mOsm/kg (reference range: greater than 800 mOsm/kg after deprivation), which is reassuring. While explaining the result, his physician mentions a comparative study of two desert mammals: Species A has proportionally much longer loops of Henle extending deep into the medulla than Species B. Assuming similar tubular transport machinery otherwise, which prediction about Species A follows directly from thin-limb physiology?","choices":{"A":"Species A will have a lower maximum urine osmolarity because of greater water loss along the descending limb","B":"Species A and Species B will have the same maximum urine osmolarity, since loop length does not matter","C":"Species A will secrete more urea in its ascending limb, offsetting any concentrating advantage","D":"Species A will require more ADH to achieve any given urine osmolarity than Species B","E":"Species A can achieve a higher maximum urine osmolarity than Species B"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A longer loop allows more repeats of countercurrent multiplication, so a larger corticopapillary gradient is built and fluid at the bend, which equilibrates with that interstitium, reaches a higher osmolarity; the maximum urine osmolarity rises with it. Descending-limb water loss does not lower the maximum, urea is not secreted by the ascending limb to offset the gain, loop length does change the maximum, and a longer loop does not raise the ADH requirement.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: desert mammals (e.g., kangaroo rats) with very long loops of Henle achieve extraordinarily concentrated urine — a classic comparative-physiology teaching example.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 1 — thin limb permeability underlies the corticopapillary gradient built further in SDL 19","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl13_q15","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Evaluate the passive permeability differences of the thin limbs of the loop of Henle, explaining why the thin descending limb is highly water-permeable (concentrating segment) while the thin ascending limb is water-impermeable but highly permeable to NaCl.","batch":2,"stem":"A 31-year-old woman is found to have the rare Colton-null blood group during pretransfusion testing. Genetic studies show that she lacks the water channel responsible for the water permeability of the thin descending limb of the loop of Henle. She has no symptoms. Blood pressure is 116/72 mm Hg. Serum sodium is 140 mEq/L (reference range: 136–146 mEq/L), and creatinine is 0.7 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL). Assuming her other transport pathways in the loop are intact, what is the most direct expected consequence for tubular fluid reaching the bend of her loops of Henle?","choices":{"A":"Tubular fluid at the bend would be far less concentrated than normal","B":"Tubular fluid at the bend would become more concentrated, since less water dilutes the remaining solute","C":"Tubular fluid osmolarity at the bend would be unaffected, since the descending limb does not help concentrate the fluid","D":"She would develop obligate glucosuria as a compensatory response","E":"NaCl reabsorption in the ascending limb would increase enough to compensate for the lost concentrating step"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Without water permeability, the descending limb loses its ability to lose water into the hypertonic interstitium, so tubular fluid arriving at the bend would remain close to its original osmolarity instead of becoming progressively concentrated.","boardPrep":"Step 1: this mirrors the physiology behind aquaporin-1 knockout models, which show markedly impaired urine-concentrating ability despite an intact ascending limb.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 1 — thin limb permeability underlies the corticopapillary gradient built further in SDL 19","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl13_q16","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Deconstruct the active, load-dependent Na+ reabsorption mechanism of the thick ascending limb (luminal NKCC2, basolateral Na+-K+ ATPase), and analyze how electrogenic K+ recycling via apical ROMK channels generates a +7 mV lumen-positive potential to drive paracellular divalent cation (Ca2+ and Mg2+) reabsorption.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man with hypercalcemia from a parathyroid adenoma has a high serum calcium level that activates the calcium-sensing receptor in the thick ascending limb. Through what downstream mechanism would activation of this receptor be expected to reduce paracellular calcium reabsorption in this segment?","choices":{"A":"It directly opens a transcellular calcium channel that bypasses the paracellular pathway","B":"It increases basolateral Na+-K+-ATPase activity, indirectly raising calcium reabsorption","C":"It stimulates additional ROMK channel insertion, strengthening the lumen-positive potential further","D":"It reduces NKCC2 activity, weakening the potential for calcium movement across tight junctions","E":"It has no effect on thick ascending limb transport and instead acts in the distal tubule"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Calcium-sensing receptor activation in the thick ascending limb inhibits NKCC2 activity, which weakens K+ recycling through ROMK and reduces the lumen-positive potential. Since paracellular Ca2+ reabsorption depends on that potential, its reduction lowers calcium reabsorption, providing a physiologic brake against worsening hypercalcemia.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: this calcium-sensing receptor feedback is the physiologic basis for why hypercalcemia itself limits further calcium reabsorption — a protective mechanism distinct from PTH.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 2 — this ROMK-dependent potential is examined further in SDL 18's renal calcium handling objective","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q17","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Deconstruct the active, load-dependent Na+ reabsorption mechanism of the thick ascending limb (luminal NKCC2, basolateral Na+-K+ ATPase), and analyze how electrogenic K+ recycling via apical ROMK channels generates a +7 mV lumen-positive potential to drive paracellular divalent cation (Ca2+ and Mg2+) reabsorption.","batch":2,"stem":"A 26-year-old woman with Bartter syndrome, a genetic disorder caused by loss-of-function mutations in NKCC2, presents with hypokalemia, metabolic alkalosis, and hypercalciuria despite normal dietary intake. Which mechanism directly explains her hypercalciuria?","choices":{"A":"NKCC2 loss increases distal tubule calcium channel expression and actively secretes calcium into the urine","B":"Chronic metabolic alkalosis directly increases intestinal calcium absorption that is then excreted unchanged","C":"A dysfunctional NKCC2 no longer sustains the potential that drives paracellular calcium reabsorption","D":"Hypokalemia independently stimulates parathyroid hormone release and causes bone calcium mobilization","E":"The mutation disrupts basolateral calcium-ATPase activity that is confined to the proximal tubule"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Bartter syndrome is essentially a genetic loop-diuretic state: without functional NKCC2, K+ recycling through ROMK cannot generate the lumen-positive potential, so the paracellular driving force for calcium (and magnesium) reabsorption is lost, producing hypercalciuria that mirrors chronic loop diuretic use.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bartter syndrome is essentially \"genetic furosemide\" — presenting with hypokalemia, metabolic alkalosis, and hypercalciuria; a frequently tested electrolyte-genetics vignette.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 2 — this ROMK-dependent potential is examined further in SDL 18's renal calcium handling objective","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q18","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Deconstruct the active, load-dependent Na+ reabsorption mechanism of the thick ascending limb (luminal NKCC2, basolateral Na+-K+ ATPase), and analyze how electrogenic K+ recycling via apical ROMK channels generates a +7 mV lumen-positive potential to drive paracellular divalent cation (Ca2+ and Mg2+) reabsorption.","batch":2,"stem":"A 38-year-old man with resistant hypertension enrolls in a phase 1 trial of an investigational diuretic. Blood pressure is 158/96 mm Hg despite three medications. Serum potassium is 4.2 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L), and creatinine is 1.0 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL). The drug selectively blocks apical ROMK channels in the thick ascending limb without affecting NKCC2 or the basolateral Na+-K+-ATPase. Which of the following best describes its most direct effect on Na+ reabsorption and on the lumen-positive potential in this segment, compared with baseline?","choices":{"A":"Na+ reabsorption would increase to compensate for the blocked K+ channel, preserving the potential","B":"Na+ reabsorption would be unaffected, since NKCC2 does not depend on ROMK-mediated K+ recycling","C":"The lumen-positive potential would increase, since blocking K+ efflux traps more positive charge intracellularly","D":"Na+ reabsorption would fall from lost K+ recycling, and the lumen-positive potential would be largely lost too","E":"Both Na+ reabsorption and the potential would be unaffected, since ROMK is not involved in either process"},"correct":"D","explanation":"NKCC2 requires a continuous supply of luminal K+ to keep transporting, and luminal K+ concentration is low relative to what NKCC2 needs, so ROMK-mediated recycling of K+ back into the lumen is what sustains ongoing cotransporter turnover. Blocking ROMK starves NKCC2 of luminal K+, reducing Na+ reabsorption and largely abolishing the +7 mV lumen-positive potential that recycling itself generates.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: ROMK is functionally coupled to NKCC2 turnover, not just potential generation — this is why ROMK loss-of-function mutations also cause a Bartter-like phenotype.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 2 — this ROMK-dependent potential is examined further in SDL 18's renal calcium handling objective","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q19","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Analyze the pharmacological mechanism of loop diuretics (furosemide, bumetanide, ethacrynic acid) binding to the Cl- site of the NKCC2 cotransporter, predicting their downstream impact on sodium excretion, divalent cation wasting (hypercalciuria/hypermagnesuria), and why water-impermeability designates the TAL as the \"diluting segment\" of the nephron.","batch":2,"stem":"A 67-year-old man with heart failure and chronic diuretic resistance is switched from furosemide to a combination of furosemide plus a thiazide diuretic, achieving substantially greater diuresis than either drug alone. Which mechanism best explains this synergy?","choices":{"A":"The thiazide directly potentiates furosemide binding at the NKCC2 chloride site itself","B":"Blocking NKCC2 raises distal sodium delivery, which the thiazide then blocks from being reabsorbed","C":"Furosemide increases renal blood flow, which independently enhances thiazide delivery to the distal tubule","D":"Both drugs act at the identical transporter, producing an additive but mechanistically redundant effect","E":"The thiazide blocks aldosterone receptors, preventing any distal compensation for the lost TAL sodium"},"correct":"B","explanation":"With chronic loop diuretic use, the terminal nephron reabsorbs more of the sodium that escapes the blocked thick ascending limb, because distal reabsorption is load-dependent, which blunts the diuretic effect over time. Adding a thiazide blocks that compensatory distal NCC-mediated reabsorption, unmasking the full natriuretic effect of the loop diuretic.","boardPrep":"Step 1: sequential nephron blockade (loop + thiazide) is a classic clinical pharmacology teaching point for diuretic-resistant edema.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 3 — this sequential-blockade principle depends on the early distal tubule mechanism covered next in SDL 14","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q20","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Analyze the pharmacological mechanism of loop diuretics (furosemide, bumetanide, ethacrynic acid) binding to the Cl- site of the NKCC2 cotransporter, predicting their downstream impact on sodium excretion, divalent cation wasting (hypercalciuria/hypermagnesuria), and why water-impermeability designates the TAL as the \"diluting segment\" of the nephron.","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman is prescribed a loop diuretic for resistant hypertension. Several weeks later, laboratory studies show a serum magnesium of 1.2 mg/dL (low) and a 24-hour urine calcium that is elevated above her pretreatment baseline. Which single upstream event best accounts for both findings occurring together?","choices":{"A":"NKCC2 blockade abolished the lumen-positive potential driving paracellular reabsorption of both cations","B":"The drug independently blocked a magnesium-specific channel and a separate calcium-specific channel","C":"Increased urine flow rate by itself diluted both cations without any change in their total reabsorption","D":"The drug stimulated parathyroid hormone release, which increased renal calcium reabsorption while wasting magnesium","E":"Reduced glomerular filtration from volume depletion decreased the filtered load of both cations"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Because Ca2+ and Mg2+ reabsorption in the thick ascending limb share the same paracellular, potential-driven mechanism, a single upstream event — loss of the lumen-positive potential from NKCC2 blockade — explains both the hypomagnesemia and the hypercalciuria appearing together. Loop diuretics do not block separate magnesium and calcium channels; both cations share the one paracellular route driven by the lumen-positive voltage.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: combined hypomagnesemia and hypercalciuria on a loop diuretic should immediately suggest this shared TAL mechanism rather than two independent processes.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 3 — this sequential-blockade principle depends on the early distal tubule mechanism covered next in SDL 14","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q21","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Analyze the pharmacological mechanism of loop diuretics (furosemide, bumetanide, ethacrynic acid) binding to the Cl- site of the NKCC2 cotransporter, predicting their downstream impact on sodium excretion, divalent cation wasting (hypercalciuria/hypermagnesuria), and why water-impermeability designates the TAL as the \"diluting segment\" of the nephron.","batch":2,"stem":"A 72-year-old man with chronic heart failure is admitted because of worsening dyspnea and leg swelling. Blood pressure is 108/70 mm Hg, and pulse is 96/min. He has jugular venous distention, bibasilar crackles, and 3+ pitting edema of both legs. Serum sodium is 122 mEq/L (reference range: 136–146 mEq/L), and serum osmolality is 256 mOsm/kg (reference range: 275–295 mOsm/kg). He is treated with an intravenous diuretic that blocks NKCC2 in the thick ascending limb. What does loss of this diluting segment do to the kidney's ability to excrete dilute urine?","choices":{"A":"Blocking NKCC2 causes the segment to actively secrete additional water into the lumen","B":"Blocking NKCC2 does not change urine dilution, since dilution takes place in the collecting duct","C":"Blocking NKCC2 increases ADH secretion directly, independent of any nephron segment effect","D":"Blocking NKCC2 increases water permeability in the thick ascending limb itself","E":"Blocking NKCC2 stops the segment from forming hypotonic fluid needed for free-water excretion"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The thick ascending limb normally dilutes tubular fluid by reabsorbing solute without water. When a loop diuretic blocks this reabsorption, the segment can no longer generate dilute fluid, so the kidney loses its ability to make maximally dilute urine; because the drug also abolishes concentration, urine becomes nearly isotonic, which is why loop diuretics generally do not worsen hyponatremia, unlike thiazides. Because the thick ascending limb also builds the medullary gradient through countercurrent multiplication, loop diuretics impair both urine dilution and urine concentration.","boardPrep":"Step 1: loop diuretics impair both dilution and concentration, so urine becomes nearly isotonic, whereas thiazides are the classic diuretic cause of hyponatremia.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 3 — the thick ascending limb's diluting function sets up the early distal tubule mechanism covered next in SDL 14","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q22","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Correlate active solute transport and high oxygen consumption in the TAL with its susceptibility to ischemic Acute Tubular Necrosis (ATN), evaluating the protective vasodilatory role of local renal prostaglandins (PGE2 and PGI2) and the clinical danger of administering NSAIDs in hypoperfused states.","batch":2,"stem":"A 72-year-old man with chronic NSAID use for osteoarthritis develops acute gastroenteritis with several days of vomiting and poor oral intake. He is admitted with a rise in creatinine from his baseline. Through what combined mechanism did his chronic NSAID use predispose him to this acute kidney injury during volume depletion?","choices":{"A":"NSAIDs directly damaged the glomerular basement membrane independent of any hemodynamic effect","B":"NSAIDs increased renal blood flow beyond the kidney's autoregulatory capacity and caused barotrauma","C":"NSAIDs stimulated excessive aldosterone secretion, producing volume overload during the illness","D":"NSAIDs blocked the vasodilation and oxygen-sparing that protect the kidney during hypoperfusion","E":"NSAIDs blocked ADH release and prevented any renal water conservation response to the vomiting"},"correct":"D","explanation":"During volume depletion, renal prostaglandins normally dilate afferent arterioles to preserve glomerular filtration and reduce thick ascending limb oxygen consumption to protect against ischemia. Chronic NSAID use had already suppressed this protective prostaglandin production, so when true volume depletion from vomiting occurred, the kidney had no reserve mechanism left to prevent acute injury.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: this NSAID-plus-volume-depletion combination is one of the most frequently tested causes of prerenal-to-intrinsic AKI progression in clinical medicine.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 4 — the same afferent-arteriole prostaglandin mechanism connects directly to the renal hemodynamics covered in SDL 8","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q23","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Correlate active solute transport and high oxygen consumption in the TAL with its susceptibility to ischemic Acute Tubular Necrosis (ATN), evaluating the protective vasodilatory role of local renal prostaglandins (PGE2 and PGI2) and the clinical danger of administering NSAIDs in hypoperfused states.","batch":2,"stem":"A 67-year-old man develops acute kidney injury after prolonged hypotension during cardiac surgery. On postoperative day 2, blood pressure is 112/68 mm Hg. Serum creatinine has tripled from his baseline to 3.1 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL), and urine microscopy shows muddy brown granular casts. A kidney biopsy shows that tubular injury is most prominent in the medullary thick ascending limbs, a segment whose oxygen consumption is disproportionately high for its size compared with the proximal tubule. What best explains this segment's high oxygen consumption?","choices":{"A":"The thick ascending limb has a larger surface area than the proximal tubule that raises its total oxygen demand","B":"The thick ascending limb runs continuous ATP-dependent Na+-K+-ATPase activity to sustain NKCC2-mediated Na+ reabsorption","C":"The thick ascending limb performs glucose reabsorption in addition to sodium transport that doubles its energy needs","D":"The thick ascending limb generates heat as its primary function, more than other reabsorptive segments","E":"The thick ascending limb has fewer mitochondria than the proximal tubule that forces inefficient metabolism"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The thick ascending limb reabsorbs a large fraction of filtered sodium through active, ATP-dependent transport (NKCC2 driven by basolateral Na+-K+-ATPase). The heavy Na+-K+-ATPase workload gives the segment high O2 use per gram of tissue, and it operates in the medulla at low PO2, so its oxygen supply-demand margin is thin.","boardPrep":"Step 1: recognizing which nephron segments rely on active vs. passive transport directly predicts their relative ischemic vulnerability — a recurring integrative exam theme.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 4 — the same afferent-arteriole prostaglandin mechanism connects directly to the renal hemodynamics covered in SDL 8","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q24","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Correlate active solute transport and high oxygen consumption in the TAL with its susceptibility to ischemic Acute Tubular Necrosis (ATN), evaluating the protective vasodilatory role of local renal prostaglandins (PGE2 and PGI2) and the clinical danger of administering NSAIDs in hypoperfused states.","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old woman is admitted to the intensive care unit with sepsis from pneumonia. Blood pressure is 86/50 mm Hg, and pulse is 118/min. Before the renal risk is recognized, she receives one dose of a nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drug for fever, and the medication is then stopped. Over the next 24 hours, her serum creatinine doubles from her baseline to 1.8 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL), and her urine output falls to 20 mL/h. Which finding would most strongly support that transient loss of prostaglandin-dependent vasodilation was the mechanism of this decline?","choices":{"A":"Creatinine recovers promptly after drug withdrawal, with a bland sediment and a low FENa","B":"A rise in serum creatinine occurring several weeks after the single dose, well outside the expected hemodynamic window","C":"Persistent proteinuria on urinalysis, consistent with glomerular basement membrane injury","D":"A finding of red blood cell casts on urine microscopy, suggesting an inflammatory glomerular process","E":"An elevated eosinophil count, suggesting an allergic interstitial nephritis reaction"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Prompt recovery of creatinine after the single dose is withdrawn, with a bland urine sediment and a low fractional excretion of sodium, is the expected signature of acutely losing protective prostaglandin-mediated vasodilation, distinguishing it from delayed or structural causes of NSAID-associated kidney injury. Eosinophilia points to acute interstitial nephritis, a hypersensitivity reaction to drugs such as NSAIDs, which is a different, immune mechanism from loss of prostaglandin vasodilation.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: distinguishing hemodynamic (prostaglandin-mediated) NSAID nephrotoxicity from allergic interstitial nephritis (delayed, eosinophils present) is a common differentiation question.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 4 — the same afferent-arteriole prostaglandin mechanism connects directly to the renal hemodynamics covered in SDL 8","isHighYield":true},{"id":"ex2t_sdl13_q1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Evaluate the passive permeability differences of the thin limbs of the loop of Henle, explaining why the thin descending limb is highly water-permeable (concentrating segment) while the thin ascending limb is water-impermeable but highly permeable to NaCl.","batch":3,"stem":"A 58-year-old man is brought in after hiking in the desert without water for 10 hours. He has dry mucous membranes, and his urine osmolality is 1150 mOsmol/kg H2O (reference range: 50–1200 mOsmol/kg H2O). Tubular fluid in his long loops of Henle reaches its highest osmolality at the hairpin turn. Which property of the thin descending limb accounts for this?","choices":{"A":"High sodium permeability, low water permeability","B":"Active chloride transport by NKCC2 cotransporters","C":"Facilitated urea secretion via UT-A1 transporters","D":"Active water transport driven by Na+-K+-ATPase","E":"High water permeability through aquaporin-1 channels"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The thin descending limb has high water permeability through aquaporin-1 channels but low NaCl permeability, so water leaves osmotically into the hypertonic medullary interstitium and tubular fluid becomes most concentrated at the hairpin turn. High sodium permeability with low water permeability describes the thin ascending limb, which lets NaCl diffuse out and begins diluting the fluid. NKCC2-mediated chloride transport is the active step of the thick ascending limb, a water-impermeable segment. The descending limb allows only small passive urea entry through UT-A2; UT-A1 is the ADH-regulated urea transporter of the inner medullary collecting duct and does not concentrate fluid in the descending limb. Water moves osmotically along gradients and is not pumped by the Na+-K+-ATPase. Sources: Exam 2 lecture notes (SDL 13, biophysics of the thin limbs); Costanzo Physiology Ch. 6 (loop of Henle); Guyton and Hall Ch. 29 (urine concentration, water permeability of the descending limb).","boardPrep":"Step 1: thin descending limb = water permeable with low solute permeability; thin ascending limb = solute permeable and water impermeable. COMLEX: a dehydration vignette asking where osmolality peaks within the loop of Henle points to the bend.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 -> Obj 1 — osmotic water exit from the descending limb is the passive half of countercurrent multiplication","isHighYield":false},{"id":"ex2t_sdl13_q2","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Deconstruct the active, load-dependent Na+ reabsorption mechanism of the thick ascending limb (luminal NKCC2, basolateral Na+-K+ ATPase), and analyze how electrogenic K+ recycling via apical ROMK channels generates a +7 mV lumen-positive potential to drive paracellular divalent cation (Ca2+ and Mg2+) reabsorption.","batch":3,"stem":"A 7-year-old boy has polyuria, growth failure, and nephrocalcinosis, with a serum potassium of 3.0 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L) and a urine calcium of 18 mg/kg/day (reference range: <4 mg/kg/day). Genetic testing shows a nonfunctional apical potassium channel in the thick ascending limb. Loss of this channel most directly impairs which process?","choices":{"A":"Basolateral chloride exit through CLC-Kb channels","B":"Apical sodium entry through epithelial sodium channels","C":"Water reabsorption through apical aquaporin-2 water channels","D":"Generation of the lumen-positive transepithelial voltage","E":"Paracellular reabsorption of urea in the inner medulla"},"correct":"D","explanation":"ROMK recycles K+ that entered on NKCC2 back into the lumen. This K+ back-leak keeps NKCC2 supplied with K+ and, with basolateral Cl- exit, generates the lumen-positive voltage of the thick ascending limb that drives paracellular Ca2+ and Mg2+ reabsorption; losing ROMK collapses that voltage, producing the hypercalciuria and nephrocalcinosis of Bartter syndrome. CLC-Kb is the basolateral chloride channel whose loss causes a different Bartter subtype, and it is not the apical potassium channel described. Epithelial sodium channels mediate Na+ entry in collecting duct principal cells. The thick ascending limb is water impermeable and lacks aquaporin-2. Urea reabsorption occurs through UT-A transporters in the inner medullary collecting duct. Sources: Exam 2 lecture notes (SDL 13, thick ascending limb transport and electrogenesis); Costanzo Physiology Ch. 6 (lumen-positive potential and Ca2+ and Mg2+ reabsorption); Guyton and Hall Ch. 32 (Bartter syndrome).","boardPrep":"Step 1: K+ recycling through ROMK makes the thick ascending limb lumen positive and drives Ca2+ and Mg2+ reabsorption. COMLEX: Bartter syndrome resembles chronic loop diuretic use, with hypokalemic metabolic alkalosis and hypercalciuria.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 -> Obj 3 — the same lumen-positive voltage drives paracellular magnesium reabsorption","isHighYield":true},{"id":"ex2t_sdl13_q3","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Correlate active solute transport and high oxygen consumption in the TAL with its susceptibility to ischemic Acute Tubular Necrosis (ATN), evaluating the protective vasodilatory role of local renal prostaglandins (PGE2 and PGI2) and the clinical danger of administering NSAIDs in hypoperfused states.","batch":3,"stem":"A 74-year-old man with 3 days of vomiting has been taking ibuprofen for back pain. His blood pressure is 92/58 mm Hg, serum creatinine has risen to 2.8 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL), and urine sediment shows muddy brown granular casts. Loss of which mediators best explains his medullary tubular injury?","choices":{"A":"Angiotensin II and endothelin 1","B":"Atrial natriuretic peptide and urodilatin","C":"Prostaglandin E2 and prostacyclin","D":"Norepinephrine and vasopressin","E":"Thromboxane A2 and leukotriene C4"},"correct":"C","explanation":"In volume depletion, prostaglandin E2 and prostacyclin act as safety buffers: they dilate renal vessels against angiotensin II and sympathetic vasoconstriction, and prostaglandin E2 blunts NKCC2 transport in the thick ascending limb, lowering oxygen demand in the hypoxic outer medulla. Cyclooxygenase inhibition by ibuprofen removes both protections, so the thick ascending limb and straight proximal tubule become ischemic and slough into muddy brown casts of acute tubular necrosis. Angiotensin II, endothelin 1, norepinephrine, and vasopressin are vasoconstrictors whose activity is increased, not lost, in this setting. Atrial natriuretic peptide and urodilatin are suppressed by volume depletion and are not blocked by NSAIDs. Thromboxane A2 is a cyclooxygenase-derived vasoconstrictor, so losing it would not cause ischemia, and leukotriene C4 comes from the lipoxygenase pathway, which NSAIDs do not block. Sources: Exam 2 lecture notes (SDL 13, medullary oxygen dynamics and prostaglandin safety buffers); Katzung Ch. 15 (prostaglandin E2 and thick ascending limb Na+ reabsorption, NSAID interference); Robbins Ch. 20 (ischemic acute tubular necrosis of the thick ascending limb and straight proximal tubule).","boardPrep":"Step 1: NSAIDs precipitate acute kidney injury in prostaglandin-dependent states such as volume depletion, heart failure, and cirrhosis. COMLEX: muddy brown granular casts indicate acute tubular necrosis; the outer medulla is most vulnerable because of its high oxygen use and low oxygen supply.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 -> Obj 1 — low effective arterial volume activates the vasoconstrictor systems that prostaglandins counterbalance","isHighYield":true}],"14":[{"id":"sdl14_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Deconstruct active, electroneutral NaCl reabsorption via the NCC cotransporter in the early distal convoluted tubule, and analyze why its absolute water impermeability designates it as the \"cortical diluting segment,\" explaining the pharmacological mechanism of thiazide diuretics as competitive NCC inhibitors","batch":1,"stem":"What ions does the NCC cotransporter move across the luminal membrane of the early distal convoluted tubule?","choices":{"A":"Two Na+ ions move together with one Cl-, producing an electrogenic current","B":"One Na+, one K+, and two Cl- ions move together, mirroring the thick ascending limb transporter stoichiometry","C":"One Na+ and one Cl- move together, making the process electroneutral","D":"A single Cl- ion crosses independently without any accompanying cation","E":"One Na+ moves independently through the channel, while Cl- follows a separate paracellular route"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The early distal tubule NCC cotransporter moves one Na+ and one Cl- together, making the process electroneutral; the thick ascending limb NKCC2 moves four ions (one Na+, one K+, and two Cl-) and the sources differ on its charge balance: the course notes call it electroneutral, with the segment's lumen-positive potential arising from ROMK-mediated K+ recycling, whereas the Costanzo reading calls it electrogenic, bringing slightly more negative than positive charge into the cell and so creating the lumen-positive potential itself. NCC carries Na+ and Cl- together; chloride does not cross on it alone.","boardPrep":"Step 1: NCC stoichiometry (1 Na+:1 Cl-) is the key distinguishing feature from NKCC2 (1 Na+:1 K+:2 Cl-); both are electroneutral, and the thick ascending limb's lumen-positive potential comes from ROMK K+ recycling. A frequently tested transporter-comparison point.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 -> Obj 1 — the NCC cotransporter that makes the early DCT the cortical diluting segment","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Deconstruct active, electroneutral NaCl reabsorption via the NCC cotransporter in the early distal convoluted tubule, and analyze why its absolute water impermeability designates it as the \"cortical diluting segment,\" explaining the pharmacological mechanism of thiazide diuretics as competitive NCC inhibitors","batch":1,"stem":"Why is the early distal convoluted tubule called the \"cortical diluting segment\"?","choices":{"A":"It reabsorbs NaCl while staying water-impermeable, lowering fluid osmolarity further","B":"It secretes free water into the lumen to actively dilute the tubular fluid","C":"It is located in the medulla, where the corticopapillary gradient dilutes the fluid passively","D":"It reabsorbs water in excess of solute, which paradoxically lowers osmolarity","E":"It has no active transport, and dilution occurs by passive solute back-diffusion"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Fluid entering the early distal tubule is already dilute from the thick ascending limb. Because the early distal tubule reabsorbs NaCl via NCC but stays water-impermeable, it dilutes the fluid further, earning its designation as the cortical diluting segment (cortical because distal tubules sit in the renal cortex).","boardPrep":"COMLEX: \"cortical\" distinguishes this segment's location from the medullary thick ascending limb, even though both are solute-reabsorbing, water-impermeable diluting segments.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 -> Obj 1 — the NCC cotransporter that makes the early DCT the cortical diluting segment","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Deconstruct active, electroneutral NaCl reabsorption via the NCC cotransporter in the early distal convoluted tubule, and analyze why its absolute water impermeability designates it as the \"cortical diluting segment,\" explaining the pharmacological mechanism of thiazide diuretics as competitive NCC inhibitors","batch":1,"stem":"At which site do thiazide diuretics bind to inhibit the NCC cotransporter?","choices":{"A":"An extracellular Na+-binding regulatory domain, distinct from the transport pore itself","B":"An ATP-binding site, supplying the energy for active NCC cycling","C":"A basolateral receptor, triggering internalization of the transporter","D":"A calcium-binding regulatory loop, located on the intracellular tail of NCC","E":"The Cl- binding site, preventing NCC from completing its transport cycle"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Like loop diuretics at NKCC2, thiazide diuretics are organic acid anions that bind the Cl- site of the NCC cotransporter, preventing it from cycling and thereby blocking NaCl reabsorption in the early distal tubule. Thiazides act at the Cl- site of the transporter in the luminal membrane, not at a separate Na+-binding regulatory domain or a basolateral receptor that internalizes NCC.","boardPrep":"Step 1: thiazides and loop diuretics share a Cl--site binding mechanism on their respective cotransporters — a useful mnemonic link between the two drug classes.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 -> Obj 1 — the NCC cotransporter that makes the early DCT the cortical diluting segment","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Analyze the electrogenic transport mechanics of principal cells in the late distal tubule and collecting duct (ENaC, Na+-K+ ATPase, ROMK), and deconstruct the genomic mechanism of aldosterone action via intracellular mineralocorticoid receptors","batch":1,"stem":"How does Na+ cross the luminal membrane of principal cells in the late distal tubule and collecting duct?","choices":{"A":"Via a Na+-Cl- cotransporter, like the one in the early distal tubule","B":"Via a Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransporter, shared with the thick ascending limb","C":"Actively via a luminal Na+-ATPase, distinct from the basolateral pump","D":"Passively through ENaC channels, diffusing down its electrochemical gradient","E":"Via a Na+-glucose cotransporter, recycling residual filtered glucose"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Unlike the coupled cotransporters of upstream segments, principal cells use ENaC, a dedicated Na+ channel, allowing Na+ to diffuse into the cell down its electrochemical gradient before being extruded basolaterally by Na+-K+-ATPase.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: ENaC is a channel, not a cotransporter — recognizing this distinguishes principal cell Na+ entry from every upstream nephron segment discussed so far.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 -> Obj 2 — principal cell ENaC transport and aldosterone's genomic mechanism","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Analyze the electrogenic transport mechanics of principal cells in the late distal tubule and collecting duct (ENaC, Na+-K+ ATPase, ROMK), and deconstruct the genomic mechanism of aldosterone action via intracellular mineralocorticoid receptors","batch":1,"stem":"Where does aldosterone bind after diffusing into a principal cell?","choices":{"A":"A luminal membrane receptor, coupled to a G protein and adenylyl cyclase","B":"An intracellular receptor, which then translocates to the nucleus","C":"A basolateral tyrosine kinase receptor, phosphorylating ENaC directly","D":"An extracellular binding protein, shuttling it to a cell-surface channel","E":"A cytoplasmic calcium-binding protein, activating protein kinase C"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Aldosterone, a steroid hormone, diffuses across the basolateral membrane and binds an intracellular mineralocorticoid receptor. The hormone-receptor complex then moves to the nucleus, where it directs transcription of new mRNAs and proteins.","boardPrep":"Step 1: steroid hormones act through intracellular/nuclear receptors and gene transcription, in contrast to peptide hormones like ADH, which use surface receptors and second messengers.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 -> Obj 2 — principal cell ENaC transport and aldosterone's genomic mechanism","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Analyze the electrogenic transport mechanics of principal cells in the late distal tubule and collecting duct (ENaC, Na+-K+ ATPase, ROMK), and deconstruct the genomic mechanism of aldosterone action via intracellular mineralocorticoid receptors","batch":1,"stem":"Which proteins are among those that aldosterone-directed transcription increases in principal cells?","choices":{"A":"ENaC channels and Na+-K+-ATPase pumps together with citrate synthase","B":"NKCC2 cotransporters and ROMK channels of the thick ascending limb","C":"Aquaporin-2 water channels, leaving Na+ transport proteins unaffected","D":"NCC cotransporters located in the early distal tubule, upstream of the principal cells","E":"V2 receptors for ADH located on the principal cell basolateral membrane"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Aldosterone-induced gene transcription increases synthesis of the luminal ENaC channel, the basolateral Na+-K+-ATPase, and mitochondrial citric acid cycle enzymes such as citrate synthase, which supply the ATP needed to sustain the increased pump activity. Aldosterone also raises K+ secretion through apical ROMK channels. NCC is the early distal tubule transporter, upstream of the principal cells, and is not one of the principal cell proteins the course lists as aldosterone-induced.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: citrate synthase induction is a less commonly recalled but board-relevant detail — aldosterone increases the metabolic machinery supporting transport, not just the transporters themselves.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 -> Obj 2 — principal cell ENaC transport and aldosterone's genomic mechanism","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare and contrast potassium-sparing diuretics in the late distal nephron, distinguishing direct ENaC blockers from competitive mineralocorticoid receptor antagonists","batch":1,"stem":"By what mechanism does spironolactone reduce Na+ reabsorption by principal cells?","choices":{"A":"It binds directly to ENaC channels, physically occluding the pore","B":"It inhibits basolateral Na+-K+-ATPase activity, independent of aldosterone levels","C":"It degrades circulating aldosterone before it reaches the principal cell","D":"It competitively blocks the mineralocorticoid receptor, preventing hormone binding","E":"It blocks the V2 receptor for ADH, located on the basolateral membrane"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Spironolactone is a steroid structurally related to aldosterone that competitively occupies the mineralocorticoid receptor, blocking aldosterone from entering the nucleus and preventing the transcription-dependent increase in ENaC, Na+-K+-ATPase, and metabolic enzymes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: spironolactone works upstream at the receptor/transcription level, while amiloride and triamterene work downstream at the channel itself — a key mechanistic distinction between K+-sparing diuretic subclasses.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 -> Obj 3 — potassium-sparing diuretic pharmacology built on that same ENaC/aldosterone axis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare and contrast potassium-sparing diuretics in the late distal nephron, distinguishing direct ENaC blockers from competitive mineralocorticoid receptor antagonists","batch":1,"stem":"By what mechanism does amiloride reduce Na+ reabsorption by principal cells?","choices":{"A":"It competitively displaces aldosterone from the mineralocorticoid receptor","B":"It prevents aldosterone synthesis in the adrenal zona glomerulosa","C":"It blocks basolateral Na+-K+-ATPase, indirectly reducing the driving force for ENaC entry","D":"It stimulates internalization of ENaC channels from the luminal membrane","E":"It binds luminal ENaC channels, without involving the mineralocorticoid receptor"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Amiloride and triamterene act directly on the luminal ENaC channel, physically blocking Na+ entry regardless of aldosterone levels, in contrast to spironolactone, which works entirely through receptor antagonism and gene transcription.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: amiloride is useful even when aldosterone is already suppressed, since its action does not depend on the hormone-receptor pathway at all.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 -> Obj 3 — potassium-sparing diuretic pharmacology built on that same ENaC/aldosterone axis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare and contrast potassium-sparing diuretics in the late distal nephron, distinguishing direct ENaC blockers from competitive mineralocorticoid receptor antagonists","batch":1,"stem":"Why are K+-sparing diuretics typically combined with loop or thiazide diuretics rather than used as monotherapy for diuresis?","choices":{"A":"They are rapidly inactivated by hepatic metabolism, reducing their overall effect","B":"They inhibit a modest fraction of Na+ reabsorption, limiting their effect","C":"They require a functioning thick ascending limb, delaying their onset of action","D":"They cause severe hypokalemia, unless combined with a second diuretic","E":"They have no natriuretic effect, acting on potassium transport"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Because the late distal tubule and collecting duct reabsorb only about 3% of filtered Na+, blocking that small fraction produces only mild diuresis. K+-sparing diuretics are combined with more potent loop or thiazide diuretics mainly to offset the kaliuresis those drugs cause. Their limit is the small share of sodium reabsorbed at their site, not hepatic inactivation; they act on principal cells downstream of the thick ascending limb, so they do not depend on it; and they are natriuretic, since blocking ENaC or aldosterone reduces Na+ reabsorption, just mildly.","boardPrep":"Step 1: matching each diuretic class to the fraction of filtered Na+ reabsorbed at its nephron site directly predicts its natriuretic potency — a recurring pharmacology integration point.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 -> Obj 3 — potassium-sparing diuretic pharmacology built on that same ENaC/aldosterone axis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Contrast the variable, ADH-dependent water permeability of the late distal tubule and collecting duct (V2-receptor, Gs-adenylyl cyclase, AQP2 translocation) with the fixed water impermeability of the ascending limb and early distal tubule","batch":1,"stem":"What determines whether the principal cells of the late distal tubule and collecting duct are permeable to water?","choices":{"A":"The magnitude of the lumen-positive electrical potential, present in that segment","B":"The local concentration of Na+-Cl- cotransporter activity, occurring in that segment","C":"Circulating ADH levels, which control AQP2 channel insertion","D":"Fixed anatomic water channels, present whatever the hormonal status","E":"The rate of active solute reabsorption, occurring in the same segment"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Unlike the ascending limb of Henle and early distal tubule, which are always water-impermeable regardless of hormonal state, principal cell water permeability is variable and controlled by ADH-driven aquaporin-2 insertion. The late distal tubule lumen is negative, about −50 mV, not positive; Na+-Cl- cotransport belongs to the early distal tubule; and solute reabsorption does not open water channels, which is why the diluting segments reabsorb solute without water.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: this is the physiologic basis for calling the ascending limb and early distal tubule \"fixed impermeable\" versus the late distal tubule/collecting duct \"variable, hormone-controlled\" permeability segments.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 -> Obj 4 — ADH-controlled water permeability contrasted with the fixed-impermeable segments upstream","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Contrast the variable, ADH-dependent water permeability of the late distal tubule and collecting duct (V2-receptor, Gs-adenylyl cyclase, AQP2 translocation) with the fixed water impermeability of the ascending limb and early distal tubule","batch":1,"stem":"What second messenger is generated when ADH binds its basolateral V2 receptor on principal cells?","choices":{"A":"Cyclic AMP, produced by Gs-protein-coupled activation of adenylyl cyclase","B":"Cyclic GMP, produced by activation of a membrane-bound guanylyl cyclase","C":"Inositol trisphosphate, produced by phospholipase C activation","D":"Diacylglycerol, produced alongside IP3 by phospholipase C","E":"Calcium released from intracellular stores by receptor activation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"ADH binding to the V2 receptor activates a stimulatory G protein (Gs), which activates adenylyl cyclase to convert ATP to cAMP. cAMP then activates protein kinase A, which triggers phosphorylation events leading to AQP2 vesicle insertion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: V2 receptor signaling (Gs/cAMP/PKA) is a classic second-messenger pathway question, often paired with pseudohypoparathyroidism-style Gs-defect vignettes for other hormones.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 -> Obj 4 — ADH-controlled water permeability contrasted with the fixed-impermeable segments upstream","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Contrast the variable, ADH-dependent water permeability of the late distal tubule and collecting duct (V2-receptor, Gs-adenylyl cyclase, AQP2 translocation) with the fixed water impermeability of the ascending limb and early distal tubule","batch":1,"stem":"What is the direct functional consequence of protein kinase A activation in a principal cell exposed to high ADH levels?","choices":{"A":"Existing luminal Na+ channels are phosphorylated and directly gated open","B":"Vesicles containing AQP2 channels are shuttled into the luminal membrane","C":"Basolateral Na+-K+-ATPase pumps are synthesized de novo within minutes","D":"Mineralocorticoid receptors are translocated into the nucleus immediately","E":"Cyclic AMP is converted back to ATP, terminating the signaling cascade"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Protein kinase A phosphorylates intracellular structures involved in vesicle trafficking, causing AQP2-containing vesicles to be shuttled to and inserted into the luminal membrane, which is the step that directly increases the cell's water permeability. New Na+-K+-ATPase synthesis and mineralocorticoid receptor movement into the nucleus are aldosterone effects, not ADH effects; and breaking down cAMP would end the signal rather than carry out its effect.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: AQP2 trafficking (not new channel synthesis) is the acute mechanism; chronic ADH exposure additionally upregulates AQP2 gene expression, a distinct slower process sometimes tested separately.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 -> Obj 4 — ADH-controlled water permeability contrasted with the fixed-impermeable segments upstream","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q13","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Deconstruct active, electroneutral NaCl reabsorption via the NCC cotransporter in the early distal convoluted tubule, and analyze why its absolute water impermeability designates it as the \"cortical diluting segment,\" explaining the pharmacological mechanism of thiazide diuretics as competitive NCC inhibitors","batch":2,"stem":"A 54-year-old woman with idiopathic hypercalciuria and recurrent calcium oxalate kidney stones is started on chlorthalidone. Over several weeks, her urinary calcium excretion falls substantially even though her Na+ excretion increases. Why does inhibiting NCC in the early distal tubule paradoxically increase Ca2+ reabsorption rather than decrease it?","choices":{"A":"Thiazides directly open a luminal calcium channel independent of any change in intracellular Na+","B":"Thiazides increase PTH secretion directly, and the PTH rise drives the observed calcium effect","C":"Thiazides reduce glomerular filtration of calcium, lowering the filtered load reaching the tubule","D":"Lower intracellular Na+ from NCC blockade drives more Na+-Ca2+ exchange and luminal Ca2+ entry","E":"Thiazides upregulate a distal calcium-binding protein that has no relationship to sodium transport"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Distal Ca2+ reabsorption is uncoupled from Na+ reabsorption, so thiazides block NaCl reabsorption without reducing Ca2+ reabsorption, and urinary Ca2+ falls. Lower intracellular Na+ from NCC blockade enhances basolateral Na+-Ca2+ exchange, which lowers intracellular Ca2+ and favors luminal Ca2+ entry.","boardPrep":"Step 1: thiazide-induced hypocalciuria (via enhanced distal Ca2+ reabsorption) is the mechanistic basis for using thiazides in idiopathic hypercalciuria and recurrent calcium stone disease.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 1 — this same distal calcium-handling mechanism is examined in depth in SDL 18's renal calcium objective","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q14","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Deconstruct active, electroneutral NaCl reabsorption via the NCC cotransporter in the early distal convoluted tubule, and analyze why its absolute water impermeability designates it as the \"cortical diluting segment,\" explaining the pharmacological mechanism of thiazide diuretics as competitive NCC inhibitors","batch":2,"stem":"A 19-year-old woman with congenital nephrogenic diabetes insipidus comes to the clinic because of constant thirst and passing about 8 L of urine daily. Blood pressure is 112/70 mm Hg. Serum sodium is 146 mEq/L (reference range: 136–146 mEq/L), and urine osmolality is 90 mOsm/kg (reference range: 50–1,200 mOsm/kg). Her urine output did not change after a trial of an ADH analog, because her collecting duct principal cells fail to respond to ADH. Her physician prescribes a thiazide diuretic. Which mechanism explains why the thiazide paradoxically reduces her urine output?","choices":{"A":"The thiazide directly restores V2 receptor sensitivity to circulating ADH","B":"The thiazide increases aquaporin-2 insertion independent of any ADH signaling","C":"The thiazide causes volume contraction that raises proximal reabsorption and cuts distal delivery","D":"The thiazide blocks the countercurrent multiplier in the loop of Henle, abolishing free-water clearance","E":"The thiazide converts nephrogenic diabetes insipidus into central diabetes insipidus pharmacologically"},"correct":"C","explanation":"An ADH analog would be futile because the defect is in the principal cell response to ADH, not in ADH secretion. Thiazides work mainly indirectly: by causing mild volume contraction, they increase proximal tubule Na+ and water reabsorption, which reduces the volume of fluid delivered to the water-impermeable diluting segments and ultimately lowers overall urine output.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: recognizing that thiazides treat nephrogenic DI through a volume-contraction, proximal-reabsorption mechanism (not by fixing the ADH defect) is a classic clinical pharmacology teaching point.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 4 — ADH-dependent water permeability of the late distal tubule and collecting duct, in contrast to the fixed-impermeable diluting segments","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q15","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Deconstruct active, electroneutral NaCl reabsorption via the NCC cotransporter in the early distal convoluted tubule, and analyze why its absolute water impermeability designates it as the \"cortical diluting segment,\" explaining the pharmacological mechanism of thiazide diuretics as competitive NCC inhibitors","batch":2,"stem":"A 66-year-old man with hypertension and stage 4 chronic kidney disease has had little response to a thiazide diuretic. Blood pressure is 162/94 mm Hg. Serum creatinine is 3.4 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL), and potassium is 4.8 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L). His plasma drug concentration is in the expected range, but accumulated organic anions compete with the drug for proximal tubule secretion, so very little of it reaches the tubular fluid of the early distal tubule. Across several dose changes, his NaCl excretion tracks the rate at which the drug appears in his urine rather than its plasma concentration. What does this observation indicate about the drug's site of action on NCC?","choices":{"A":"The Cl- binding site targeted by thiazides is reachable from the lumen but not the basolateral membrane","B":"NCC has two Cl- binding sites that function identically on the luminal and basolateral sides","C":"The thiazide is degraded before it can cross the basolateral membrane and produce any effect","D":"Thiazides bind the Na+ site of NCC, which faces the blood side of the cell","E":"The basolateral membrane lacks the ATP supply needed for thiazide binding to have any effect"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Thiazides reach NCC from the tubular fluid: they are secreted into the proximal tubule by the organic acid secretory pathway and then bind the Cl- site of NCC on the luminal membrane of the early distal tubule. In this patient, natriuresis tracked the amount of drug reaching the urine rather than the plasma level, which fits a binding site reached from the lumen rather than from the blood side. Thiazides bind the Cl- site, not the Na+ site; NCC does not have a second, basolateral binding site; and drug degradation or basolateral ATP supply does not explain the observation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: furosemide and thiazides act from the tubular fluid side of their luminal cotransporters, and protein binding in the lumen (as in nephrotic syndrome) impairs furosemide delivery and effect.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 1 — the Cl--site binding mechanism mirrors the loop diuretic pharmacology covered in SDL 13's NKCC2 objective","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q16","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Analyze the electrogenic transport mechanics of principal cells in the late distal tubule and collecting duct (ENaC, Na+-K+ ATPase, ROMK), and deconstruct the genomic mechanism of aldosterone action via intracellular mineralocorticoid receptors","batch":2,"stem":"A 62-year-old man with a rare genetic mutation has principal cells with normal mineralocorticoid receptors but constitutively active ENaC channels that no longer require aldosterone stimulation. Which transport abnormality would be expected even if his serum aldosterone level is measured as very low?","choices":{"A":"Normal Na+ and K+ handling because ENaC activity would still require aldosterone-driven gene transcription to function","B":"Excessive renal Na+ reabsorption and K+ secretion driven by unregulated ENaC activity","C":"Severe hyponatremia from excessive Na+ loss due to loss of aldosterone-driven ENaC synthesis","D":"Hyperkalemia from reduced K+ secretion because ENaC activity determines the driving force for ROMK","E":"No abnormality because basolateral Na+-K+-ATPase activity is what determines the transport rate"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Because this mutation makes ENaC constitutively active independent of aldosterone, Na+ reabsorption and the resulting lumen-negative potential that drives K+ secretion through ROMK proceed at a high rate regardless of aldosterone level, producing a picture resembling aldosterone excess (Na+ retention, K+ wasting) despite genuinely low aldosterone (this describes the physiology of Liddle syndrome).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Liddle syndrome (constitutively active ENaC) produces hypertension and hypokalemia with LOW aldosterone and renin, distinguishing it from primary hyperaldosteronism on labs alone.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 2 — the ENaC-ROMK electrical coupling described here is the same principle tested in SDL 17's principal cell secretion objective","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q17","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Analyze the electrogenic transport mechanics of principal cells in the late distal tubule and collecting duct (ENaC, Na+-K+ ATPase, ROMK), and deconstruct the genomic mechanism of aldosterone action via intracellular mineralocorticoid receptors","batch":2,"stem":"A 48-year-old woman is found to have a mutation that prevents aldosterone-bound mineralocorticoid receptor complexes from entering the nucleus, while receptor binding to aldosterone itself remains intact. What downstream consequence would be expected for the core aldosterone-induced proteins?","choices":{"A":"Normal Na+ reabsorption, since receptor binding is sufficient by itself to activate ENaC directly","B":"Increased Na+ reabsorption, since the trapped receptor complex acts as a constitutive activator in the cytoplasm","C":"Selective loss of ENaC transcription, with Na+-K+-ATPase and citrate synthase unaffected","D":"Compensatory upregulation of amiloride-sensitive channels independent of the mineralocorticoid pathway","E":"Failure of ENaC, Na+-K+-ATPase, and citrate synthase transcription despite normal hormone-receptor binding"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Aldosterone's genomic mechanism requires the hormone-receptor complex to translocate to the nucleus to direct mRNA synthesis. If nuclear translocation is blocked, none of the core aldosterone-induced proteins (ENaC, Na+-K+-ATPase, and citrate synthase, along with K+ secretion channels such as ROMK) are transcribed, functionally mimicking mineralocorticoid receptor antagonism even though the receptor itself binds hormone normally.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: this vignette isolates the nuclear-translocation step of steroid hormone signaling, distinct from ligand binding — a mechanism-dissection style question favored on integrated exams.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 2 — the aldosterone receptor and nuclear transcription pathway that spironolactone blocks pharmacologically","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q18","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Analyze the electrogenic transport mechanics of principal cells in the late distal tubule and collecting duct (ENaC, Na+-K+ ATPase, ROMK), and deconstruct the genomic mechanism of aldosterone action via intracellular mineralocorticoid receptors","batch":2,"stem":"A 46-year-old woman with an aldosterone-secreting adrenal adenoma has hypertension and hypokalemia. Her physician explains that the hormone acts on the principal cells of the late distal tubule and collecting duct, the final segments to adjust how much Na+ is excreted. Which description of Na+ reabsorption by these principal cells is most accurate?","choices":{"A":"About 3% of filtered Na+, entering through ENaC channels","B":"About 25% of filtered Na+, entering through NKCC2","C":"About 67% of filtered Na+, entering through NHE3","D":"About 5% of filtered Na+, entering through NCC","E":"About 15% of filtered Na+, entering through ENaC channels"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The late distal tubule and collecting duct reabsorb only the final 3% of the filtered Na+, but as the last segments to act they make the fine adjustments to Na+ excretion; Na+ diffuses into principal cells through luminal ENaC channels down its electrochemical gradient and is pumped out by the basolateral Na+-K+-ATPase, with a lumen-negative transepithelial potential of about −50 mV. About 25% of filtered Na+ is reabsorbed by the thick ascending limb through NKCC2, and about 67% by the proximal tubule, where NHE3 is a major luminal entry step. About 5% is reabsorbed in the early distal tubule by the NCC cotransporter. ENaC is the correct channel, but 15% overstates the share handled by these segments.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Segmental Na+ reabsorption: proximal tubule 67%, thick ascending limb 25%, early distal tubule 5% (NCC), late distal tubule and collecting duct 3% (ENaC, aldosterone-regulated). COMLEX: Although the share is small, aldosterone excess at this site is enough to cause hypertension with hypokalemia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 3 — amiloride and spironolactone act on these same principal cells","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q19","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare and contrast potassium-sparing diuretics in the late distal nephron, distinguishing direct ENaC blockers from competitive mineralocorticoid receptor antagonists","batch":2,"stem":"A 66-year-old man with resistant hypertension and hyperaldosteronism from an aldosterone-producing adenoma is treated with spironolactone, but develops breast tenderness and gynecomastia over several months. His physician switches him to amiloride instead. Which property specific to spironolactone, absent with amiloride, best explains this side-effect profile?","choices":{"A":"Spironolactone causes a much greater degree of ENaC blockade than amiloride at equivalent doses","B":"Amiloride has no effect on K+ secretion, while spironolactone directly increases it","C":"Spironolactone, as a steroid, carries off-target antiandrogen activity that amiloride lacks","D":"Spironolactone is metabolized to a compound that independently raises H+ secretion in the kidney","E":"Amiloride works in the proximal tubule, so it does not cause the same distal side effects"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Both drugs reduce distal K+ secretion by blunting Na+ reabsorption and the associated lumen-negative potential, so hyperkalemia risk is a class effect shared by both, not something specific to spironolactone. What distinguishes spironolactone as a steroid molecule is its off-target binding to androgen and progesterone receptors, producing side effects like gynecomastia that amiloride, a non-steroidal ENaC blocker, does not share. This structural difference, rather than any difference in K+-sparing potency, accounts for the side-effect profile.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: spironolactone's antiandrogen activity (gynecomastia, menstrual irregularity) is a distinguishing clinical feature versus amiloride/triamterene, which lack steroid-receptor cross-reactivity.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 3 — spironolactone blocks the mineralocorticoid receptor while amiloride blocks ENaC directly","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q20","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare and contrast potassium-sparing diuretics in the late distal nephron, distinguishing direct ENaC blockers from competitive mineralocorticoid receptor antagonists","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old woman with cirrhosis and ascites is treated with furosemide plus spironolactone. In addition to enhancing diuresis, the combination limits a furosemide-induced side effect. Which furosemide-induced process does spironolactone most directly offset?","choices":{"A":"Furosemide-induced direct inhibition of the mineralocorticoid receptor itself","B":"Increased distal Na+ delivery driving greater ENaC-mediated reabsorption and K+ secretion","C":"Furosemide-induced destruction of principal cells requiring replacement by aldosterone-driven regeneration","D":"Furosemide-induced suppression of ADH secretion that spironolactone directly restores","E":"Furosemide-induced hypercalcemia that spironolactone corrects through calcium-sparing action"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Loop diuretics increase Na+ delivery to the distal nephron, where increased ENaC-mediated reabsorption in principal cells drives greater K+ secretion (kaliuresis). Spironolactone blunts this compensatory ENaC-driven Na+ reabsorption by blocking the mineralocorticoid receptor, thereby reducing the associated K+ loss while contributing natriuresis of its own.","boardPrep":"Step 1: combining loop/thiazide diuretics with K+-sparing agents specifically to offset kaliuresis is one of the most frequently tested diuretic-combination rationales.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 3 — increased distal Na+ delivery from loop diuretic blockade is the same downstream effect discussed in SDL 13's sequential nephron blockade vignette","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q21","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare and contrast potassium-sparing diuretics in the late distal nephron, distinguishing direct ENaC blockers from competitive mineralocorticoid receptor antagonists","batch":2,"stem":"A 40-year-old man with Bartter syndrome (nonfunctional NKCC2) has persistent hypokalemia despite adequate oral potassium supplementation. His physician considers adding a K+-sparing diuretic to reduce ongoing distal K+ secretion. Which agent would remain effective independent of circulating aldosterone levels, given that the primary defect is upstream in the thick ascending limb, not in ENaC itself?","choices":{"A":"Amiloride, since it blocks ENaC directly regardless of what caused the increased downstream Na+ delivery","B":"Furosemide, since it would directly correct the K+ wasting at its NKCC2 site of action","C":"A thiazide diuretic, since blocking NCC in the distal tubule would compensate for the nonfunctional NKCC2 upstream","D":"Acetazolamide, since inhibiting proximal carbonic anhydrase would reduce distal K+ secretion","E":"No K+-sparing diuretic would have any effect, since the primary defect lies upstream in the thick ascending limb"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Regardless of the primary defect's location, amiloride acts locally on ENaC in the distal nephron and reduces K+ secretion driven by whatever Na+ delivery reaches that segment, making it effective even in a Bartter syndrome patient whose distal Na+ load is elevated because of an upstream NKCC2 defect. Furosemide, thiazides, and acetazolamide increase K+ excretion rather than reduce it, and no K+-sparing agent restores NKCC2 function; it only reduces distal K+ secretion.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: amiloride's site-specific mechanism (direct ENaC block) makes it broadly useful for distal K+ wasting regardless of the upstream cause; K+-sparing agents help by reducing distal K+ secretion, not by correcting the NKCC2 defect.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 3 — amiloride blocks ENaC directly regardless of the upstream cause of distal Na+ delivery","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q22","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Contrast the variable, ADH-dependent water permeability of the late distal tubule and collecting duct (V2-receptor, Gs-adenylyl cyclase, AQP2 translocation) with the fixed water impermeability of the ascending limb and early distal tubule","batch":2,"stem":"A 30-year-old man with central diabetes insipidus is given desmopressin (a synthetic ADH analog) and his urine osmolarity rises substantially within an hour. Which cellular event most directly accounts for this rapid response, given that new AQP2 protein synthesis would take much longer to occur?","choices":{"A":"Desmopressin directly opens existing luminal AQP2 channels without any signaling cascade","B":"Desmopressin triggers immediate transcription of new AQP2 mRNA that is translated within minutes","C":"Desmopressin increases the permeability of the ascending limb of Henle to compensate acutely","D":"Desmopressin acts on V1 receptors to vasoconstrict the vasa recta, indirectly concentrating urine","E":"Existing AQP2 vesicles are shuttled to and fused with the luminal membrane once PKA is activated"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The rapid rise in urine osmolarity reflects the fast, non-transcriptional arm of ADH action: V2-receptor-triggered cAMP/protein kinase A activation causes pre-formed AQP2-containing vesicles already present in the cytoplasm to translocate to and fuse with the luminal membrane within minutes, increasing water permeability immediately. Transcriptional upregulation of AQP2 expression is a separate, slower process that reinforces the effect over hours to days.","boardPrep":"Step 1: distinguishing the acute vesicle-trafficking mechanism of ADH action from its chronic transcriptional upregulation of AQP2 is a subtle but testable point on integrated renal physiology exams.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 4 — ADH acts through V2 receptors, cAMP, and PKA to insert AQP2 vesicles into the luminal membrane","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q23","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Contrast the variable, ADH-dependent water permeability of the late distal tubule and collecting duct (V2-receptor, Gs-adenylyl cyclase, AQP2 translocation) with the fixed water impermeability of the ascending limb and early distal tubule","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old man with small cell carcinoma of the lung is evaluated for confusion. He appears euvolemic. Blood pressure is 128/80 mm Hg. Serum sodium is 122 mEq/L (reference range: 136–146 mEq/L), serum osmolality is 252 mOsm/kg (reference range: 275–295 mOsm/kg), and urine osmolality is 610 mOsm/kg (reference range: 50–1,200 mOsm/kg). His plasma ADH concentration is markedly elevated. Despite this, his thick ascending limbs and early distal tubules remain impermeable to water, whereas his late distal tubules and collecting ducts are highly permeable. Which structural difference between these segment types best explains this contrast?","choices":{"A":"The upstream segments express AQP2 constitutively but at a much lower density than principal cells","B":"Principal cells hold regulatable AQP2 channels; upstream segments carry no water channels under any condition","C":"ADH receptors are present throughout the nephron, but upstream segments fail to couple to adenylyl cyclase","D":"The upstream segments actively pump water out of the lumen, masking any passive permeability","E":"The tight junctions of the upstream segments become more permeable to water under high ADH exposure"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The ascending limb of Henle and early distal tubule are impermeable to water because they lack water channels of any kind, regardless of ADH level — this impermeability is fixed and structural. Principal cells, by contrast, express AQP2 channels that are held in cytoplasmic vesicles until ADH-driven signaling triggers their insertion into the luminal membrane, making their permeability variable and hormone-dependent. The upstream thick ascending limb does respond to ADH, which stimulates its Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransport, so the difference is the lack of water channels, not failed receptor coupling.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the fixed-versus-variable water permeability distinction along the nephron is essential for predicting how ADH changes urine concentration only at specific segments, not throughout the entire tubule.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 4 — this fixed-impermeability property of the thick ascending limb is the same one established in SDL 13's \"diluting segment\" objective","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q24","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Contrast the variable, ADH-dependent water permeability of the late distal tubule and collecting duct (V2-receptor, Gs-adenylyl cyclase, AQP2 translocation) with the fixed water impermeability of the ascending limb and early distal tubule","batch":2,"stem":"A 27-year-old woman with primary polydipsia has ADH secretion suppressed for a few days of heavy water intake. After that short period of low ADH, when she is water-deprived, her principal cells still respond appropriately by inserting AQP2 and concentrating her urine. What does this preserved response indicate about the ADH-AQP2 signaling pathway?","choices":{"A":"AQP2 channels had remained inserted in her luminal membrane throughout the period of low ADH","B":"Her principal cells developed a compensatory ADH-independent water permeability mechanism during the overhydration period","C":"Low ADH exposure downregulated V2 receptors, and the observed response reflects a nonspecific stress reaction","D":"The V2 receptor-Gs-adenylyl cyclase complex stays intact and functional after transient suppression","E":"Her collecting ducts secreted their own ADH locally, bypassing the need for pituitary-derived hormone"},"correct":"D","explanation":"A preserved concentrating response after water deprivation demonstrates that the entire V2 receptor/Gs/adenylyl cyclase/PKA signaling cascade remains intact and functional; the pathway simply had not been actively engaged during the short period of low circulating ADH (chronic suppression would be expected to down-regulate AQP2), distinguishing this from an inherited defect such as nephrogenic diabetes insipidus, where the pathway itself is broken regardless of ADH level or history. ADH is secreted by the posterior pituitary and reaches the kidney through the circulation; the collecting ducts do not make it.","boardPrep":"Step 1: this vignette tests the conceptual difference between a pathway being unstimulated versus a pathway being broken — a distinction central to differentiating primary polydipsia from nephrogenic diabetes insipidus on water-deprivation testing.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 1 — central versus nephrogenic DI is distinguished by ADH level and renal responsiveness","isHighYield":true},{"id":"ex2t_sdl14_q1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Deconstruct active, electroneutral NaCl reabsorption via the NCC cotransporter in the early distal convoluted tubule, and analyze why its absolute water impermeability designates it as the \"cortical diluting segment,\" explaining the pharmacological mechanism of thiazide diuretics as competitive NCC inhibitors","batch":3,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman with recurrent kidney stones and idiopathic hypercalciuria starts an oral antihypertensive that increases urinary sodium excretion while lowering urinary calcium excretion. Two weeks later her serum sodium is 131 mEq/L (reference range: 136–146 mEq/L) and serum potassium is 3.2 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L). Which transporter does this drug inhibit?","choices":{"A":"The Na+-Cl- cotransporter of the early distal tubule","B":"The Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransporter of the thick ascending limb","C":"The epithelial sodium channel of the principal cell","D":"The Na+-H+ exchanger of the proximal tubule","E":"The Na+-phosphate cotransporter of the proximal tubule"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A natriuretic drug that lowers urinary calcium is a thiazide acting on the electroneutral Na+-Cl- cotransporter (NCC) of the early distal convoluted tubule. Blocking NCC lowers intracellular Na+, which enhances basolateral Na+-Ca2+ exchange and Ca2+ entry through apical channels, and mild volume contraction raises proximal Ca2+ reabsorption; the result is hypocalciuria. Greater Na+ and flow delivery to principal cells causes hypokalemia, and impaired cortical dilution causes hyponatremia. Blocking the Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransporter raises urinary calcium because it collapses the lumen-positive voltage. Blocking the epithelial sodium channel spares potassium, which does not fit her hypokalemia. Inhibiting the proximal Na+-H+ exchanger mainly causes bicarbonaturia, and inhibiting Na+-phosphate cotransport causes phosphaturia. Sources: Exam 2 lecture notes (SDL 14, NCC transport in the early distal convoluted tubule); Costanzo Physiology Ch. 6 (distal tubule Ca2+ reabsorption and the hypocalciuric action of thiazides); Katzung Ch. 15 (thiazide diuretics).","boardPrep":"Step 1: thiazides cause hypokalemia, hyponatremia, metabolic alkalosis, hyperuricemia, hyperglycemia, and hypercalcemia. COMLEX: thiazides are used to prevent calcium stones in idiopathic hypercalciuria, whereas loop diuretics raise urinary calcium.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 -> Obj 1 — the distal convoluted tubule is also where PTH regulates transcellular calcium reabsorption","isHighYield":true},{"id":"ex2t_sdl14_q2","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare and contrast potassium-sparing diuretics in the late distal nephron, distinguishing direct ENaC blockers from competitive mineralocorticoid receptor antagonists","batch":3,"stem":"A 48-year-old man with cirrhosis and ascites is treated with a drug that competes with aldosterone at the cytoplasmic mineralocorticoid receptor. One week later his serum potassium is 5.7 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L), and he has developed tender gynecomastia. Which change in the cortical collecting duct explains his rise in serum potassium?","choices":{"A":"Greater sodium delivery to the distal nephron","B":"Increased apical ROMK channel density in principal cells","C":"A less negative luminal voltage from reduced sodium entry","D":"Direct blockade of apical potassium channels","E":"Stimulated basolateral Na+-K+-ATPase activity in principal cells"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Spironolactone blocks the mineralocorticoid receptor, so principal cells synthesize fewer epithelial sodium channels and Na+-K+-ATPase pumps. Less electrogenic Na+ entry leaves a less negative luminal voltage, which lowers the electrical driving force for K+ secretion through ROMK and raises serum potassium; its antiandrogen action explains the gynecomastia. Greater sodium delivery to the distal nephron increases K+ secretion and is the hypokalemic mechanism of loop and thiazide diuretics. Aldosterone blockade reduces ROMK density and Na+-K+-ATPase activity rather than increasing them. Potassium-sparing drugs act on Na+ entry, not by directly blocking apical potassium channels. Sources: Exam 2 lecture notes (SDL 14, potassium-sparing diuretics and principal cell mechanics); Katzung Ch. 15 (aldosterone antagonists and ENaC blockers); Costanzo Physiology Ch. 6 (aldosterone and K+ secretion by principal cells).","boardPrep":"Step 1: spironolactone and eplerenone block the mineralocorticoid receptor, while amiloride and triamterene block ENaC directly; all can cause hyperkalemia. COMLEX: gynecomastia with spironolactone is a reason to switch to eplerenone.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 -> Obj 2 — the lumen-negative voltage created by ENaC drives ROMK potassium secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"ex2t_sdl14_q3","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Contrast the variable, ADH-dependent water permeability of the late distal tubule and collecting duct (V2-receptor, Gs-adenylyl cyclase, AQP2 translocation) with the fixed water impermeability of the ascending limb and early distal tubule","batch":3,"stem":"A 29-year-old woman has polyuria of 7 L daily beginning 2 days after transsphenoidal pituitary surgery. Her serum sodium is 149 mEq/L (reference range: 136–146 mEq/L) and urine osmolality is 90 mOsmol/kg H2O (reference range: 50–1200 mOsmol/kg H2O), which rises to 620 mOsmol/kg H2O (reference range: 50–1200 mOsmol/kg H2O) after desmopressin. Desmopressin produced this change through which cellular event?","choices":{"A":"Basolateral insertion of aquaporin-1 water channels","B":"Apical insertion of aquaporin-2 water channels","C":"Apical opening of epithelial sodium channels","D":"Apical insertion of urea transporters in the thick limb","E":"Inhibition of the Na+-Cl- cotransporter in the distal tubule"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Central diabetes insipidus after pituitary surgery removes ADH, and desmopressin replaces it at V2 receptors on principal cells of the late distal tubule and collecting duct. V2 signaling through Gs, cAMP, and protein kinase A moves aquaporin-2 water channels from cytoplasmic vesicles into the apical membrane, so water exits through basolateral aquaporin-3 and aquaporin-4 into the hypertonic interstitium and the urine concentrates. Aquaporin-1 is constitutive in the proximal tubule and thin descending limb and is not regulated by ADH. Epithelial sodium channels are regulated mainly by aldosterone and do not change urine osmolality this much. ADH-regulated urea transporters are in the inner medullary collecting duct, and the thick ascending limb is water impermeable. Inhibiting the distal Na+-Cl- cotransporter is the action of thiazides, not of desmopressin, and would not concentrate the urine. Sources: Exam 2 lecture notes (SDL 14, ADH-dependent water permeability of the collecting duct); Costanzo Physiology Ch. 6 (ADH, V2 receptors, and aquaporin-2); Guyton and Hall Ch. 29 (ADH control of collecting duct water permeability).","boardPrep":"Step 1: a large rise in urine osmolality after desmopressin indicates central diabetes insipidus, while little change indicates nephrogenic diabetes insipidus. COMLEX: watch for transient or triphasic diabetes insipidus after pituitary surgery.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 -> Obj 1 — water deprivation and desmopressin testing separates central from nephrogenic diabetes insipidus","isHighYield":true}],"15":[{"id":"sdl15_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Synthesize the integrated endocrine and neural effector pathways (SNS, RAAS, peritubular Starling forces) that coordinate the renal response to high versus low dietary Na+ intake, and evaluate the clinical distinction between ECF volume and Effective Arterial Blood Volume (EABV) in edematous disease states","batch":1,"stem":"What is effective arterial blood volume (EABV)?","choices":{"A":"The total extracellular fluid volume measured across the entire body","B":"The plasma volume remaining after subtracting interstitial fluid volume","C":"The venous blood volume returning to the heart per minute","D":"The portion of ECF volume within arteries that perfuses tissues","E":"The fraction of cardiac output distributed toward the kidneys"},"correct":"D","explanation":"EABV refers specifically to the arterial portion of ECF volume that effectively perfuses tissues, a concept the kidneys sense and respond to by adjusting Na+ excretion, even when it diverges from total ECF volume.","boardPrep":"Step 1: EABV is a functional, perfusion-based concept distinct from measured ECF volume — the distinction becomes clinically important in edematous states covered later in this objective.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 -> Obj 1 — the integrated SNS/RAAS/Starling-force response to dietary Na+ and the ECF-versus-EABV distinction","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Synthesize the integrated endocrine and neural effector pathways (SNS, RAAS, peritubular Starling forces) that coordinate the renal response to high versus low dietary Na+ intake, and evaluate the clinical distinction between ECF volume and Effective Arterial Blood Volume (EABV) in edematous disease states","batch":1,"stem":"How does increased sympathetic nerve activity change renal Na+ handling in response to a fall in arterial pressure?","choices":{"A":"It dilates afferent arterioles and decreases proximal tubule Na+ reabsorption","B":"It constricts afferent arterioles and increases proximal tubule Na+ reabsorption","C":"It constricts efferent arterioles selectively, sparing the afferent arteriole from any change","D":"It has no direct renal effect, acting instead on cardiac output","E":"It increases distal tubule Na+ reabsorption without any vascular effect"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Baroreceptor-triggered sympathetic activation constricts renal afferent arterioles and directly stimulates proximal tubule Na+ reabsorption, both actions that help restore arterial pressure at the expense of renal blood flow.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: this is the same baroreceptor-sympathetic pathway tested in cardiovascular physiology, applied here to its renal Na+-conserving consequence.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 -> Obj 1 — the integrated SNS/RAAS/Starling-force response to dietary Na+ and the ECF-versus-EABV distinction","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Synthesize the integrated endocrine and neural effector pathways (SNS, RAAS, peritubular Starling forces) that coordinate the renal response to high versus low dietary Na+ intake, and evaluate the clinical distinction between ECF volume and Effective Arterial Blood Volume (EABV) in edematous disease states","batch":1,"stem":"How do peritubular capillary Starling forces change when ECF volume expands?","choices":{"A":"Peritubular oncotic pressure rises, which stimulates proximal tubule Na+ reabsorption","B":"Peritubular hydrostatic pressure falls, which stimulates proximal tubule Na+ reabsorption","C":"Peritubular oncotic pressure falls, which inhibits proximal tubule Na+ reabsorption","D":"Peritubular oncotic pressure stays fixed as ECF volume changes","E":"Peritubular capillary flow reverses direction, pulling fluid back into the tubule"},"correct":"C","explanation":"ECF volume expansion dilutes plasma proteins in peritubular capillary blood, lowering oncotic pressure and reducing the force favoring fluid uptake from the interstitium, which in turn inhibits proximal tubule Na+ reabsorption and promotes natriuresis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: this Starling-force mechanism is the same one governing glomerulotubular balance, applied here to whole-body Na+ regulation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 -> Obj 1 — the integrated SNS/RAAS/Starling-force response to dietary Na+ and the ECF-versus-EABV distinction","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Deconstruct the molecular and cellular mechanisms of ANP and related vasoactive peptides (BNP, urodilatin), analyzing how their G-protein/cGMP signaling pathway regulates afferent and efferent arteriolar resistance to increase GFR while directly inhibiting distal tubular Na+ reabsorption","batch":1,"stem":"Besides its actions on the renal arterioles and on collecting duct Na+ reabsorption, atrial natriuretic peptide also alters the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system. Which combination of effects does it produce?","choices":{"A":"Lower renin release and lower aldosterone secretion","B":"Higher renin release and higher aldosterone secretion","C":"Lower renin release with higher aldosterone secretion","D":"Higher renin release with lower aldosterone secretion","E":"Unchanged renin release with higher aldosterone output"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Guyton describes ANP as decreasing renin secretion, and therefore angiotensin II formation, and decreasing aldosterone secretion both by direct action on zona glomerulosa cells and by reducing angiotensin II; together with afferent dilation and direct inhibition of collecting duct Na+ reabsorption, these effects increase Na+ and water excretion. Higher renin and aldosterone are the response to a low effective arterial blood volume, the opposite of the ECF expansion that releases ANP. Lower renin with higher aldosterone, or higher renin with lower aldosterone, splits two outputs that ANP moves in the same direction. ANP does not leave renin unchanged or raise aldosterone; any rise in aldosterone would oppose the natriuresis it produces.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ANP and BNP act on the same guanylyl cyclase natriuretic peptide receptors (cGMP): afferent dilation with efferent constriction (higher GFR), less Na+ reabsorption in the late distal tubule and collecting duct, and lower renin and aldosterone. COMLEX: ANP and BNP are greatly elevated in heart failure, where they partly blunt Na+ and water retention.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 3 — ANP release from ECF expansion underlies escape from mineralocorticoid excess","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Deconstruct the molecular and cellular mechanisms of ANP and related vasoactive peptides (BNP, urodilatin), analyzing how their G-protein/cGMP signaling pathway regulates afferent and efferent arteriolar resistance to increase GFR while directly inhibiting distal tubular Na+ reabsorption","batch":1,"stem":"Where does ANP act directly to inhibit tubular Na+ reabsorption?","choices":{"A":"The proximal convoluted tubule","B":"The thick ascending limb of the loop of Henle","C":"The early distal convoluted tubule via NCC inhibition","D":"The thin descending limb of the loop of Henle","E":"The late distal tubule and collecting ducts"},"correct":"E","explanation":"ANP directly decreases Na+ reabsorption in the late distal tubule and collecting ducts, acting on the same principal cells targeted by aldosterone but in the opposite direction, which is central to its natriuretic effect. ANP does not act on the thick ascending limb, the Na+-K+-2Cl- site that loop diuretics target, or on the thin descending limb, which is permeable to water but not to sodium.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ANP and aldosterone act on the same nephron segment with opposite effects on Na+ reabsorption, a useful pairing for board questions on collecting duct regulation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 -> Obj 2 — ANP's hemodynamic and tubular actions built on cGMP signaling","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Deconstruct the molecular and cellular mechanisms of ANP and related vasoactive peptides (BNP, urodilatin), analyzing how their G-protein/cGMP signaling pathway regulates afferent and efferent arteriolar resistance to increase GFR while directly inhibiting distal tubular Na+ reabsorption","batch":1,"stem":"What second messenger mediates ANP's cellular actions after binding its receptor?","choices":{"A":"Cyclic AMP, generated by Gs-coupled adenylyl cyclase","B":"Cyclic GMP, generated via intrinsic guanylyl cyclase activity","C":"Inositol trisphosphate, generated by phospholipase C","D":"Calcium released directly from the sarcoplasmic reticulum","E":"Diacylglycerol, generated alongside IP3 by phospholipase C"},"correct":"B","explanation":"ANP receptors possess intrinsic guanylyl cyclase activity, so ligand binding directly generates cyclic GMP rather than requiring a separate G-protein/adenylyl cyclase step, distinguishing ANP signaling from the cAMP-based pathway used by ADH.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: ANP's cGMP mechanism is shared with nitric oxide signaling and is a frequently tested contrast against ADH's cAMP-based V2 receptor pathway.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 -> Obj 2 — ANP's hemodynamic and tubular actions built on cGMP signaling","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Analyze the clinical and physiological compensatory mechanisms of \"mineralocorticoid escape,\" evaluating how hemodynamics, subclinical ECF volume expansion, and ANP release act in concert to prevent progressive hypervolemic edema in primary hyperaldosteronism","batch":1,"stem":"What triggers ANP release in a patient with sustained mineralocorticoid excess?","choices":{"A":"A direct stimulatory effect of aldosterone on atrial ANP-secreting cells","B":"Hyperkalemia resulting from excessive principal cell K+ secretion","C":"The ECF volume expansion produced by aldosterone-driven Na+ retention","D":"A compensatory fall in renin release from the juxtaglomerular apparatus","E":"Direct stimulation of the atria by angiotensin II independent of volume status"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Excess aldosterone increases Na+ reabsorption, expanding ECF volume; this volume expansion itself, sensed by atrial stretch receptors, is what triggers ANP secretion, not a direct hormonal action of aldosterone on the atria. A fall in renin is itself a consequence of the volume expansion, not a signal to the atria, which secrete ANP when ECF volume rises.","boardPrep":"Step 1: recognizing that ANP release is a volume-sensing response, not a direct aldosterone effect, is essential for understanding mineralocorticoid escape as a hemodynamic feedback loop.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 -> Obj 3 — mineralocorticoid escape, the clinical payoff of Obj 1 and Obj 2 acting together","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Analyze the clinical and physiological compensatory mechanisms of \"mineralocorticoid escape,\" evaluating how hemodynamics, subclinical ECF volume expansion, and ANP release act in concert to prevent progressive hypervolemic edema in primary hyperaldosteronism","batch":1,"stem":"What is the net effect of ANP release during mineralocorticoid escape on collecting duct Na+ handling?","choices":{"A":"It partially offsets aldosterone's Na+-retaining effect, limiting further ECF expansion","B":"It reverses aldosterone's Na+ retention, returning ECF volume to baseline","C":"It has no measurable effect on collecting duct Na+ transport during mineralocorticoid excess","D":"It potentiates aldosterone's Na+-retaining action, worsening ECF expansion further","E":"It acts on the proximal tubule instead, leaving the collecting duct unaffected"},"correct":"A","explanation":"ANP inhibits Na+ reabsorption in the collecting duct, partially but not completely counteracting the ongoing Na+-retaining effect of excess aldosterone, which is why patients with primary hyperaldosteronism plateau at a new, higher ECF volume rather than developing progressively worsening edema.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: mineralocorticoid escape explains why primary hyperaldosteronism classically presents with hypertension but not overt edema, unlike secondary hyperaldosteronism in heart failure or cirrhosis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 -> Obj 3 — mineralocorticoid escape, the clinical payoff of Obj 1 and Obj 2 acting together","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Analyze the clinical and physiological compensatory mechanisms of \"mineralocorticoid escape,\" evaluating how hemodynamics, subclinical ECF volume expansion, and ANP release act in concert to prevent progressive hypervolemic edema in primary hyperaldosteronism","batch":1,"stem":"Why does mineralocorticoid escape occur in primary hyperaldosteronism but classically fail to prevent edema in conditions like nephrotic syndrome or heart failure?","choices":{"A":"ANP secretion is blunted in nephrotic syndrome and heart failure compared with hyperaldosteronism","B":"The kidneys stop responding to ANP signaling once ECF volume is expanded","C":"Aldosterone levels are typically lower in nephrotic syndrome than in primary hyperaldosteronism","D":"Persistently low EABV keeps driving Na+ retention despite ongoing ANP release","E":"ANP receptors are downregulated after the first exposure to volume expansion"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In nephrotic syndrome and heart failure, EABV remains chronically low (from protein loss or pump failure) even as ECF volume rises, so the baroreceptor-sensed signal for Na+ retention persists and continues to override ANP's natriuretic effect, unlike in primary hyperaldosteronism, where EABV normalizes once ECF volume expands enough.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the ECF-volume-versus-EABV distinction from Obj 1 is exactly what explains why escape succeeds in one condition and fails in another — a classic integrative board question.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 -> Obj 3 — mineralocorticoid escape, the clinical payoff of Obj 1 and Obj 2 acting together","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q10","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Synthesize the integrated endocrine and neural effector pathways (SNS, RAAS, peritubular Starling forces) that coordinate the renal response to high versus low dietary Na+ intake, and evaluate the clinical distinction between ECF volume and Effective Arterial Blood Volume (EABV) in edematous disease states","batch":2,"stem":"A healthy 25-year-old man switches from a low-Na+ to a high-Na+ diet over several days. Which combination of changes would be expected in his renin and sympathetic activity as his kidneys restore Na+ balance?","choices":{"A":"Both renin release and sympathetic activity rise as the kidneys attempt to conserve the excess Na+","B":"Renin release rises while sympathetic activity falls in an opposing pattern","C":"Renin release stays unchanged while sympathetic activity rises sharply","D":"Neither renin release nor sympathetic activity changes because ANP takes over the regulation of Na+ balance","E":"Both renin release and sympathetic activity fall as rising EABV is sensed by baroreceptors"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A high-Na+ diet expands ECF volume and EABV, which is sensed by baroreceptors and leads to coordinated suppression of both sympathetic outflow and renin release, reducing Na+-retaining signals so that excess Na+ can be excreted.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: renin and sympathetic activity move together (not in opposite directions) in response to volume changes, since both respond to the same baroreceptor-sensed EABV signal.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 1 — this same baroreceptor-driven suppression is the mirror image of the hemorrhage response covered in SDL 8's renal hemodynamics objective","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q11","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Synthesize the integrated endocrine and neural effector pathways (SNS, RAAS, peritubular Starling forces) that coordinate the renal response to high versus low dietary Na+ intake, and evaluate the clinical distinction between ECF volume and Effective Arterial Blood Volume (EABV) in edematous disease states","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man with cirrhosis and ascites has a measured ECF volume far above normal, yet his kidneys avidly retain Na+ as though he were volume-depleted. Which concept best resolves this apparent paradox?","choices":{"A":"His kidneys are responding appropriately to his elevated ECF volume, and the Na+ retention is an error in renal sensing","B":"His EABV is low despite an expanded ECF volume, because fluid has shifted into the peritoneal cavity","C":"ECF volume and EABV are, by definition, identical measurements of the same physiologic space","D":"His arterial pressure is elevated, which would normally suppress Na+ retention","E":"Cirrhosis abolishes baroreceptor sensing, leaving ANP to regulate Na+ retention independently"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In cirrhosis with ascites, portal hypertension and reduced effective circulating volume cause fluid to sequester in the peritoneal cavity, so EABV falls even though total ECF volume rises. The kidneys respond to the low EABV they can sense, not the elevated total ECF volume, explaining the seemingly paradoxical Na+ retention.","boardPrep":"Step 1: cirrhotic ascites is a classic example, alongside nephrotic syndrome and heart failure, of a dissociation between ECF volume and EABV driving inappropriate renal Na+ retention.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 1 — ECF volume and EABV can move in opposite directions in edematous states such as cirrhosis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q12","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Synthesize the integrated endocrine and neural effector pathways (SNS, RAAS, peritubular Starling forces) that coordinate the renal response to high versus low dietary Na+ intake, and evaluate the clinical distinction between ECF volume and Effective Arterial Blood Volume (EABV) in edematous disease states","batch":2,"stem":"A 70-year-old woman with heart failure and reduced cardiac output is found to have elevated renin, elevated sympathetic activity, and significant peripheral edema. Why do the renin and sympathetic responses in this case actually worsen rather than resolve her edema?","choices":{"A":"Renin and sympathetic activity are elevated by direct cardiac injury signals acting on the kidney","B":"Her reduced cardiac output paradoxically produces a high EABV that the kidneys are misinterpreting","C":"Her persistently low EABV keeps triggering Na+ retention even though her ECF volume has expanded","D":"The Na+ retention reflects a primary renal defect in tubular Na+ handling","E":"Elevated renin and sympathetic activity in heart failure occur without any baroreceptor input"},"correct":"C","explanation":"In heart failure, reduced cardiac output lowers EABV even as total ECF volume and venous/interstitial fluid rise from Na+ retention. Baroreceptors continue to sense this low EABV as if the patient were volume-depleted, sustaining renin release and sympathetic activation that drive further Na+ retention and worsen the edema.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: this same low-EABV-despite-high-ECF-volume mechanism links heart failure, cirrhosis, and nephrotic syndrome as the three classic causes of edema driven by inappropriate renal Na+ retention.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 1 — the renin-sympathetic coupling described here connects directly to SDL 8's and SDL 15 Obj 3's hemodynamic feedback mechanisms","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q13","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Deconstruct the molecular and cellular mechanisms of ANP and related vasoactive peptides (BNP, urodilatin), analyzing how their G-protein/cGMP signaling pathway regulates afferent and efferent arteriolar resistance to increase GFR while directly inhibiting distal tubular Na+ reabsorption","batch":2,"stem":"A 61-year-old man with chronic heart failure enrolls in a mechanistic study. Blood pressure is 118/72 mm Hg, and he has 1+ pitting edema of both ankles. Serum sodium is 136 mEq/L (reference range: 136–146 mEq/L), and plasma B-type natriuretic peptide is 640 pg/mL (reference range: less than 100 pg/mL). He receives an investigational agent that selectively blocks the guanylyl cyclase activity of the atrial natriuretic peptide (ANP) receptor without affecting ligand binding to the receptor. Which downstream consequence would be expected?","choices":{"A":"ANP would no longer bind its receptor, since binding would require active guanylyl cyclase","B":"ANP's effects would be rerouted through a compensatory cAMP-based pathway","C":"Aldosterone secretion would increase directly as a result of blocked guanylyl cyclase activity","D":"The afferent arteriole would dilate normally, since that effect is independent of cGMP","E":"ANP would still bind its receptor, but the intracellular cGMP rise would be lost"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Because ANP receptor binding and its intrinsic guanylyl cyclase catalytic function are separable properties, blocking only the catalytic activity leaves ligand binding intact but abolishes the cGMP generation that mediates the principal NPR-A-mediated vascular and tubular effects of ANP.","boardPrep":"Step 1: distinguishing receptor binding from downstream catalytic signaling is a recurring theme across guanylyl cyclase-linked and enzyme-linked receptors on integrated exams.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 2 — ANP signals through a receptor with guanylyl cyclase activity, and cGMP mediates its effects","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q14","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Deconstruct the molecular and cellular mechanisms of ANP and related vasoactive peptides (BNP, urodilatin), analyzing how their G-protein/cGMP signaling pathway regulates afferent and efferent arteriolar resistance to increase GFR while directly inhibiting distal tubular Na+ reabsorption","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old man with acute decompensated heart failure is given a synthetic BNP analog. Within hours, his urine output increases and his GFR rises modestly. Which combined mechanism most directly explains both findings?","choices":{"A":"Efferent dilation raising GFR together with stimulation of collecting duct Na+ reabsorption","B":"Afferent dilation raising GFR together with direct inhibition of collecting duct Na+ reabsorption","C":"A pure mineralocorticoid receptor antagonist effect with no vascular component","D":"Increased proximal tubule Na+-H+ exchange combined with unchanged glomerular hemodynamics","E":"Direct diuretic action at the loop of Henle identical to a loop diuretic's mechanism"},"correct":"B","explanation":"BNP, like ANP, dilates the afferent arteriole (raising GFR) while constricting the efferent arteriole, and directly inhibits Na+ reabsorption in the collecting duct, together producing both the increased GFR and the natriuresis/diuresis seen with BNP analog therapy in heart failure. BNP has a clear vascular effect on both arterioles rather than acting as a pure mineralocorticoid antagonist, and Na+-H+ exchange in the proximal tubule is stimulated by angiotensin II, so raising it would retain sodium rather than cause natriuresis.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: synthetic BNP (nesiritide) exploits the same afferent-dilation/efferent-constriction plus tubular mechanism as endogenous ANP, making this a frequently tested pharmacology-physiology bridge.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 2 — BNP acts like ANP, raising GFR and inhibiting collecting duct Na+ reabsorption","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q15","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Deconstruct the molecular and cellular mechanisms of ANP and related vasoactive peptides (BNP, urodilatin), analyzing how their G-protein/cGMP signaling pathway regulates afferent and efferent arteriolar resistance to increase GFR while directly inhibiting distal tubular Na+ reabsorption","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman undergoes an experimental infusion of urodilatin, a peptide secreted by the kidney itself rather than the atria. Which effect would be expected to differ least from ANP's known actions?","choices":{"A":"Its effects on GFR and Na+ reabsorption, since it shares ANP's receptor family","B":"Its site of synthesis, since urodilatin, like ANP, is produced by atrial myocytes","C":"Its second messenger, since urodilatin signals primarily through cAMP","D":"Its effect on the efferent arteriole, since urodilatin causes efferent dilation","E":"Its overall effect on GFR, since urodilatin decreases glomerular filtration"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Urodilatin belongs to the same natriuretic peptide family as ANP and BNP and signals through the same cGMP-generating receptor mechanism, producing effects similar to ANP, namely increased GFR and decreased Na+ reabsorption in the collecting ducts, even though it is synthesized locally by the kidney rather than the atria.","boardPrep":"Step 1: urodilatin is a useful example of a locally acting (paracrine) member of a hormone family whose downstream signaling mechanism is otherwise identical to its circulating relatives.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 2 — urodilatin and BNP have effects on GFR and Na+ reabsorption similar to ANP","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q16","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Analyze the clinical and physiological compensatory mechanisms of \"mineralocorticoid escape,\" evaluating how hemodynamics, subclinical ECF volume expansion, and ANP release act in concert to prevent progressive hypervolemic edema in primary hyperaldosteronism","batch":2,"stem":"A 50-year-old woman with a confirmed aldosterone-secreting adrenal adenoma has hypertension and hypokalemia but no peripheral edema on examination. Her physician explains that this absence of edema is expected. Which physiologic principle best accounts for it?","choices":{"A":"Aldosterone-secreting adenomas produce no net Na+ retention, since their effects are confined to K+ secretion","B":"Her renal Na+ channels have become desensitized to aldosterone over time","C":"ANP release from ECF expansion has partially offset her aldosterone-driven Na+ retention","D":"Her low serum K+ has independently caused a compensatory diuresis that prevents edema","E":"The adrenal adenoma secretes ANP directly, bypassing the atria"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Primary hyperaldosteronism classically produces hypertension and hypokalemia without significant edema because ANP-mediated mineralocorticoid escape allows Na+ excretion to catch up once ECF volume has expanded enough to normalize EABV, capping further fluid retention well before edema develops. Her principal cells remain responsive to aldosterone, as her ongoing K+ wasting shows, and ANP comes from the cardiac atria, not from the adrenal adenoma.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the classic absence of edema in Conn syndrome (versus its presence in secondary hyperaldosteronism from heart failure or cirrhosis) is explained by this escape mechanism.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 3 — mineralocorticoid escape explains why Conn syndrome does not cause edema","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q17","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Analyze the clinical and physiological compensatory mechanisms of \"mineralocorticoid escape,\" evaluating how hemodynamics, subclinical ECF volume expansion, and ANP release act in concert to prevent progressive hypervolemic edema in primary hyperaldosteronism","batch":2,"stem":"A 62-year-old man with primary hyperaldosteronism is found to have a persistently expanded ECF volume that has plateaued rather than continuing to rise, despite ongoing excess aldosterone secretion. Which sequence of events best explains this plateau?","choices":{"A":"Aldosterone secretion spontaneously declines once the ECF volume reaches a certain fixed threshold level","B":"The adrenal adenoma becomes progressively less sensitive to its own secreted aldosterone over time","C":"Renal Na+ channels are internalized once a fixed cumulative Na+ load has been reached","D":"ECF expansion lowers peritubular oncotic pressure and triggers ANP to reduce Na+ reabsorption","E":"ECF volume expansion directly suppresses adrenal aldosterone synthesis through a local feedback loop"},"correct":"D","explanation":"As aldosterone-driven Na+ retention expands ECF volume, EABV normalizes, peritubular oncotic pressure is diluted so proximal Na+ reabsorption falls, and ANP release inhibits collecting duct Na+ reabsorption. Together these create a new steady state where Na+ intake and excretion are balanced at a higher-than-normal ECF volume, without any change in aldosterone secretion itself. Na+ channels are not internalized at a fixed load; the aldosterone effect on principal cells persists, as the continuing K+ wasting of hyperaldosteronism shows.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the plateau in primary hyperaldosteronism reflects a new equilibrium between unchanged aldosterone secretion and rising ANP-mediated opposition, not any reduction in aldosterone itself.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 3 — escape caps ECF expansion through peritubular Starling forces and ANP","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q18","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Analyze the clinical and physiological compensatory mechanisms of \"mineralocorticoid escape,\" evaluating how hemodynamics, subclinical ECF volume expansion, and ANP release act in concert to prevent progressive hypervolemic edema in primary hyperaldosteronism","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old woman with primary hyperaldosteronism is treated preoperatively with spironolactone before her adrenal tumor is removed. Which change in her mineralocorticoid escape physiology would be expected on this medication?","choices":{"A":"Escape becomes largely unnecessary, since spironolactone blocks the mineralocorticoid receptor directly","B":"Escape becomes more pronounced, since spironolactone directly stimulates additional ANP secretion","C":"Escape is abolished, since spironolactone blocks ANP receptors as well as mineralocorticoid receptors","D":"Escape physiology is unaffected, since spironolactone's relevant action is on potassium handling","E":"Escape reverses direction, causing progressive volume depletion without any plateau"},"correct":"A","explanation":"By blocking the mineralocorticoid receptor directly, spironolactone reduces the excess Na+ reabsorption that made escape physiology necessary in the first place; with aldosterone's effect pharmacologically blunted, ECF volume and EABV move back toward normal without requiring ongoing ANP-mediated opposition.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: spironolactone treats primary hyperaldosteronism at the same receptor step where mineralocorticoid escape naturally intervenes downstream, which is why the two mechanisms are often compared on integrated exams.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 3 — spironolactone's mineralocorticoid receptor mechanism is the same one detailed in SDL 14's K+-sparing diuretic objective","isHighYield":true},{"id":"ex2t_sdl15_q1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Synthesize the integrated endocrine and neural effector pathways (SNS, RAAS, peritubular Starling forces) that coordinate the renal response to high versus low dietary Na+ intake, and evaluate the clinical distinction between ECF volume and Effective Arterial Blood Volume (EABV) in edematous disease states","batch":3,"stem":"A 62-year-old man with ischemic cardiomyopathy has 3+ pitting edema and jugular venous distension. He is hypotensive at 94/60 mm Hg, his serum sodium is 131 mEq/L (reference range: 136–146 mEq/L), and his fractional excretion of sodium is 0.2%. Which of the following best explains his avid renal sodium retention?","choices":{"A":"A low effective arterial blood volume sensed by baroreceptors","B":"Suppression of renin release by atrial stretch receptors","C":"A reduced filtered sodium load from the low plasma sodium","D":"Loss of aldosterone responsiveness in principal cells","E":"High peritubular capillary hydrostatic pressure"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In heart failure, total body sodium and extracellular volume are expanded, but low cardiac output underfills the arterial tree. Carotid sinus, aortic arch, and afferent arteriolar baroreceptors sense this low effective arterial blood volume and activate sympathetic nerves, the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system, and nonosmotic ADH release, so the kidney retains sodium and water and hyponatremia develops. Renin release is stimulated, not suppressed, despite atrial distension. The small fall in plasma sodium changes the filtered load only slightly and does not account for the very low fractional excretion. Aldosterone is elevated and principal cells remain responsive. Angiotensin II and sympathetic tone raise filtration fraction, which lowers peritubular hydrostatic pressure and raises oncotic pressure to favor proximal reabsorption. Sources: Exam 2 lecture notes (SDL 15, effective arterial blood volume and effector pathways); Costanzo Physiology Ch. 6 (effective arterial blood volume and Starling forces in heart failure); Guyton and Hall Ch. 30 (sympathetic and angiotensin II control of sodium excretion).","boardPrep":"Step 1: heart failure, cirrhosis, and nephrotic syndrome share low effective arterial blood volume with expanded total extracellular volume. COMLEX: a low urine sodium in an edematous patient signals a low effective arterial blood volume.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 -> Obj 1 — angiotensin II released in low arterial volume also drives proximal bicarbonate reclamation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"ex2t_sdl15_q2","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Deconstruct the molecular and cellular mechanisms of ANP and related vasoactive peptides (BNP, urodilatin), analyzing how their G-protein/cGMP signaling pathway regulates afferent and efferent arteriolar resistance to increase GFR while directly inhibiting distal tubular Na+ reabsorption","batch":3,"stem":"A 70-year-old woman with acute decompensated heart failure has a plasma BNP of 1450 pg/mL (reference range: 0–100 pg/mL). She receives an infusion of recombinant B-type natriuretic peptide, and her urinary sodium excretion rises. This peptide acts through which second messenger pathway?","choices":{"A":"Cyclic AMP from adenylyl cyclase","B":"Cyclic GMP from guanylyl cyclase","C":"Inositol trisphosphate from phospholipase C","D":"Tyrosine kinase activity from the receptor","E":"Calcium from ryanodine receptors"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Recombinant BNP (nesiritide) binds natriuretic peptide receptor A, whose intracellular domain is a particulate guanylyl cyclase. The rise in cyclic GMP dilates the afferent arteriole and constricts the efferent arteriole to raise GFR, inhibits Na+ reabsorption in the collecting duct, and suppresses renin and aldosterone, producing natriuresis and vasodilation. Cyclic AMP from adenylyl cyclase mediates V2 and beta-1 receptor signaling. Inositol trisphosphate from phospholipase C mediates angiotensin II AT1 and alpha-1 signaling, and receptor tyrosine kinase activity mediates insulin and growth factor signaling. Ryanodine receptors release calcium from the sarcoplasmic reticulum in muscle. Sources: Exam 2 lecture notes (SDL 15, ANP, BNP, and urodilatin mechanisms); Katzung Ch. 17 (natriuretic peptide receptor A and particulate guanylyl cyclase); Costanzo Physiology Ch. 6 (ANP actions on GFR and Na+ reabsorption).","boardPrep":"Step 1: natriuretic peptides use membrane-bound guanylyl cyclase, whereas nitric oxide uses soluble guanylyl cyclase; both raise cGMP. COMLEX: neprilysin inhibitors such as sacubitril raise natriuretic peptide levels in heart failure.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 -> Obj 2 — natriuretic peptides oppose aldosterone-driven ENaC sodium entry in principal cells","isHighYield":true},{"id":"ex2t_sdl15_q3","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Analyze the clinical and physiological compensatory mechanisms of \"mineralocorticoid escape,\" evaluating how hemodynamics, subclinical ECF volume expansion, and ANP release act in concert to prevent progressive hypervolemic edema in primary hyperaldosteronism","batch":3,"stem":"A 46-year-old woman with an aldosterone-producing adrenal adenoma has hypertension at 168/102 mm Hg, serum potassium of 3.0 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L), and bicarbonate of 31 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L). She has no peripheral edema, and her daily urinary sodium excretion matches her intake. Which mechanism best explains the absence of progressive volume overload?","choices":{"A":"Downregulation of mineralocorticoid receptors in principal cells","B":"Suppressed secretion of atrial natriuretic peptide","C":"Increased potassium delivery that blocks apical ENaC","D":"Pressure natriuresis with reduced proximal reabsorption","E":"Renal escape from circulating antidiuretic hormone"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In mineralocorticoid escape, aldosterone-driven sodium retention first expands extracellular volume by roughly 10 to 15% and raises arterial pressure. Higher renal perfusion pressure and suppressed angiotensin II reduce sodium reabsorption in the proximal tubule and loop (pressure natriuresis), and increased atrial natriuretic peptide inhibits collecting duct sodium reabsorption, so excretion again matches intake while hypertension persists. Mineralocorticoid receptors and ENaC in principal cells stay active, which is why hypokalemia and metabolic alkalosis continue. Atrial natriuretic peptide rises rather than falls with volume expansion. Potassium delivery does not block ENaC. Escape from antidiuretic hormone describes water handling in SIADH, not sodium balance in hyperaldosteronism. Sources: Exam 2 lecture notes (SDL 15, mineralocorticoid escape); Guyton and Hall Ch. 30 (aldosterone escape through pressure natriuresis and angiotensin II suppression); Costanzo Physiology Ch. 6 (ANP and the escape phenomenon).","boardPrep":"Step 1: primary hyperaldosteronism causes hypertension, hypokalemia, and metabolic alkalosis without edema because of escape. COMLEX: screen with the plasma aldosterone-to-renin ratio.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 -> Obj 2 — escape restores sodium balance but leaves aldosterone-driven ROMK potassium secretion in place","isHighYield":true},{"id":"grid_sdl15_q01","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Analyze the clinical and physiological compensatory mechanisms of \"mineralocorticoid escape,\" evaluating how hemodynamics, subclinical ECF volume expansion, and ANP release act in concert to prevent progressive hypervolemic edema in primary hyperaldosteronism","batch":4,"stem":"A 42-year-old woman comes to the physician for follow-up of hypertension diagnosed 6 months ago that has remained uncontrolled despite amlodipine. She reports fatigue and nocturia. She does not eat licorice, and neither of her parents had hypertension before age 60. Blood pressure is 168/102 mm Hg in both arms, and pulse is 74/min. No abdominal bruit is heard, and there is no peripheral edema. Serum sodium is 144 mEq/L (reference range: 136–146 mEq/L). CT of the abdomen shows a 1.4-cm left adrenal nodule. Which of the following sets of laboratory findings is most likely in this patient?","grid":{"headers":["Serum K⁺","Serum HCO₃⁻","Renin","Aldos­terone"]},"choices":{"A":["↓","↑","↑","↑"],"B":["↓","↑","↓","↑"],"C":["↓","↑","↓","↓"],"D":["↑","↓","↑","↓"],"E":["↑","↓","↓","↓"]},"correct":"B","explanation":"Resistant hypertension with an adrenal nodule and no edema is primary hyperaldosteronism (Conn syndrome), so the pattern (↓ serum K⁺, ↑ serum HCO₃⁻, ↓ renin, ↑ aldosterone) is correct. Autonomous aldosterone increases Na⁺ reabsorption and K⁺ secretion by principal cells (hypokalemia) and H⁺ secretion by α-intercalated cells (metabolic alkalosis); the expanded ECF volume raises renal perfusion pressure and suppresses renin while aldosterone stays high. The serum sodium is high-normal because the retained Na⁺ is accompanied by water. Mineralocorticoid escape through pressure natriuresis and ANP limits further Na⁺ retention, which explains the absence of edema. The pattern (↓ serum K⁺, ↑ serum HCO₃⁻, ↑ renin, ↑ aldosterone) is secondary hyperaldosteronism, as in renal artery stenosis or a renin-secreting tumor, where renin drives aldosterone. The pattern (↓ serum K⁺, ↑ serum HCO₃⁻, ↓ renin, ↓ aldosterone) is Liddle syndrome or apparent mineralocorticoid excess: the same hypokalemic alkalosis and hypertension, but caused by ENaC gain of function or by cortisol acting on the mineralocorticoid receptor, so both renin and aldosterone are suppressed. The pattern (↑ serum K⁺, ↓ serum HCO₃⁻, ↑ renin, ↓ aldosterone) is primary adrenal insufficiency, with loss of aldosterone and a compensatory rise in renin. The pattern (↑ serum K⁺, ↓ serum HCO₃⁻, ↓ renin, ↓ aldosterone) is hyporeninemic hypoaldosteronism, the usual cause of type 4 renal tubular acidosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: primary hyperaldosteronism = hypertension, hypokalemia, and metabolic alkalosis with high aldosterone and suppressed renin; secondary hyperaldosteronism raises both, and Liddle syndrome or apparent mineralocorticoid excess lowers both. COMLEX: an aldosterone antagonist such as spironolactone controls blood pressure and potassium before removal of a unilateral aldosterone-secreting adenoma.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 2 — aldosterone stimulates the α-intercalated cell H⁺-ATPase, the source of the metabolic alkalosis in hyperaldosteronism","isHighYield":true}],"16":[{"id":"sdl16_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Deconstruct the physiological necessity of the extreme intracellular-to-extracellular K+ concentration gradient maintained by Na+-K+ ATPase, analyzing how shifts in this ratio alter resting membrane potential and tissue excitability, and evaluate the pathological mechanisms (hyperosmolarity, cell lysis, rhabdomyolysis, tumor lysis, strenuous exercise) that trigger cellular K+ efflux","batch":1,"stem":"What maintains the large concentration gradient between intracellular and extracellular K+?","choices":{"A":"Passive diffusion down a gradient established by dietary K+ intake","B":"A dedicated K+ pump expressed selectively in excitable tissues such as nerve and muscle","C":"Active transport by Na+-K+ ATPase present in the membrane of virtually any cell","D":"Facilitated diffusion through K+ channels that require no energy expenditure","E":"Selective renal reabsorption that concentrates K+ inside cells directly"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The Na+-K+ ATPase, present in virtually all cell membranes, actively pumps K+ into cells and Na+ out, maintaining intracellular K+ near 150 mEq/L against extracellular K+ near 4.5 mEq/L despite the constant passive leak that would otherwise dissipate this gradient. There is no separate K+ pump limited to excitable tissues, and K+ channels let K+ leak out down its gradient, which would dissipate the gradient rather than maintain it.","boardPrep":"Step 1: this Na+-K+ ATPase-maintained gradient is the same one that sets resting membrane potential, linking renal/fluid physiology directly to cardiac and neuromuscular excitability questions.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 -> Obj 1 — the K+ gradient that sets resting membrane potential and the pathologic efflux states that disrupt it","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Deconstruct the physiological necessity of the extreme intracellular-to-extracellular K+ concentration gradient maintained by Na+-K+ ATPase, analyzing how shifts in this ratio alter resting membrane potential and tissue excitability, and evaluate the pathological mechanisms (hyperosmolarity, cell lysis, rhabdomyolysis, tumor lysis, strenuous exercise) that trigger cellular K+ efflux","batch":1,"stem":"Why can a relatively small shift of K+ out of cells produce a disproportionately large rise in blood K+ concentration?","choices":{"A":"Because extracellular K+ is normally near zero, so any efflux at any level produces a large percentage change","B":"Because about 98% of body K+ is intracellular, a limited efflux is large relative to the smaller ECF pool","C":"Because the kidneys cannot excrete K+ quickly enough to prevent transient accumulation","D":"Because Na+-K+ ATPase reverses direction during any efflux, actively pumping additional K+ outward","E":"Because plasma K+ is largely bound to albumin, and efflux from cells releases that bound fraction"},"correct":"B","explanation":"With about 98% of total body K+ inside cells and only about 2% in the extracellular fluid, even a modest percentage shift out of the large intracellular pool represents a large absolute amount of K+ added to the comparatively small extracellular volume, producing a disproportionate rise in measured plasma K+. The sharp rise comes from the distribution itself, not from slow renal excretion, which governs longer-term external balance, and the Na+-K+ ATPase does not reverse to pump K+ out; it keeps moving K+ into cells.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: this disproportion is why cell lysis syndromes (rhabdomyolysis, tumor lysis) can cause severe, rapid hyperkalemia even when only a fraction of total body K+ is released.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 -> Obj 1 — the K+ gradient that sets resting membrane potential and the pathologic efflux states that disrupt it","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Deconstruct the physiological necessity of the extreme intracellular-to-extracellular K+ concentration gradient maintained by Na+-K+ ATPase, analyzing how shifts in this ratio alter resting membrane potential and tissue excitability, and evaluate the pathological mechanisms (hyperosmolarity, cell lysis, rhabdomyolysis, tumor lysis, strenuous exercise) that trigger cellular K+ efflux","batch":1,"stem":"How does an acute rise in extracellular K+ concentration affect the resting membrane potential of excitable cells?","choices":{"A":"It depolarizes the resting membrane potential, moving it closer to threshold","B":"It hyperpolarizes the resting membrane potential, moving it further from threshold","C":"It depolarizes the resting membrane potential, but moves it further from threshold","D":"It leaves the resting membrane potential unchanged because Na+-K+ ATPase compensates immediately","E":"It abolishes the resting membrane potential at any degree of hyperkalemia"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Because resting membrane potential is set largely by the K+ concentration gradient, raising extracellular K+ reduces that gradient and depolarizes the resting membrane potential, altering the excitability of nerve, skeletal muscle, and cardiac tissue.","boardPrep":"Step 1: hyperkalemia depolarizes resting membrane potential toward threshold, which paradoxically can inactivate Na+ channels and cause conduction abnormalities at severe levels — a classic ECG/physiology integration point.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 -> Obj 1 — the K+ gradient that sets resting membrane potential and the pathologic efflux states that disrupt it","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Analyze the hormonal and receptor-mediated pathways regulating transcellular K+ distribution, contrasting insulin-stimulated Na+-K+ ATPase upregulation with beta-2 adrenergic agonist and alpha-adrenergic agonist/beta-blocker mediated shifts","batch":1,"stem":"By what mechanism does insulin promote K+ uptake into cells?","choices":{"A":"It opens dedicated K+ channels that allow passive K+ entry down its gradient","B":"It stimulates a separate K+-glucose cotransporter distinct from Na+-K+ ATPase","C":"It increases cellular ATP production, indirectly and nonselectively raising K+ entry","D":"It increases the activity of Na+-K+ ATPase, moving more K+ across the membrane","E":"It inhibits K+ efflux channels without affecting any influx pathway"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Insulin increases Na+-K+ ATPase activity, which actively drives more K+ into cells; this action, distinct from insulin's glucose-uptake effect, is what allows dietary K+ from a meal to be taken up into cells rather than remaining in the ECF and causing hyperkalemia. Insulin's effect is active uptake through greater Na+-K+ ATPase activity, not opening K+ entry channels or blocking K+ efflux channels.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: IV insulin with glucose is an emergency hyperkalemia treatment, reflecting this Na+-K+ ATPase-stimulating effect.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 -> Obj 2 — the hormonal/adrenergic pathways that shift K+ into or out of cells","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Analyze the hormonal and receptor-mediated pathways regulating transcellular K+ distribution, contrasting insulin-stimulated Na+-K+ ATPase upregulation with beta-2 adrenergic agonist and alpha-adrenergic agonist/beta-blocker mediated shifts","batch":1,"stem":"What direction of K+ shift results from beta-2 adrenergic receptor activation?","choices":{"A":"Out of cells, producing a tendency toward hyperkalemia","B":"No net shift, since beta-2 receptors act mainly on vascular smooth muscle","C":"Into cells predominantly in cardiac tissue, with little effect in other organs","D":"Out of cells transiently, followed by a larger rebound shift inward","E":"Into cells, producing a tendency toward hypokalemia"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Beta-2 adrenergic agonists such as albuterol increase Na+-K+ ATPase activity, shifting K+ into cells and lowering plasma K+. Beta-2 agonists are used as an adjunct emergency treatment for hyperkalemia.","boardPrep":"Step 1: beta-2 agonists (K+ into cells) and beta-2 antagonists like propranolol (K+ out of cells) have opposite effects on K+ distribution — a frequently tested pharmacology pairing.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 -> Obj 2 — the hormonal/adrenergic pathways that shift K+ into or out of cells","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Analyze the hormonal and receptor-mediated pathways regulating transcellular K+ distribution, contrasting insulin-stimulated Na+-K+ ATPase upregulation with beta-2 adrenergic agonist and alpha-adrenergic agonist/beta-blocker mediated shifts","batch":1,"stem":"What direction of K+ shift results from alpha-adrenergic receptor activation?","choices":{"A":"Out of cells, producing a tendency toward hyperkalemia","B":"Into cells, producing a tendency toward hypokalemia","C":"No net shift, since alpha receptors regulate mainly vascular tone","D":"Out of cells predominantly in skeletal muscle, sparing cardiac and hepatic tissue","E":"Into cells transiently, followed by a larger rebound shift outward"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Alpha-adrenergic receptor activation causes K+ to shift out of cells and can produce hyperkalemia, the opposite direction from beta-2 receptor activation, giving catecholamines a dual and receptor-specific effect on K+ distribution.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: alpha-adrenergic agonists shift K+ out of cells while alpha-adrenergic antagonists shift K+ into cells — the mirror image of the beta-2 agonist/antagonist pairing.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 -> Obj 2 — the hormonal/adrenergic pathways that shift K+ into or out of cells","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Critically evaluate transcellular H+-K+ exchange during systemic pH disturbances, contrasting the predictable hypokalemia of alkalemia and hyperkalemia of inorganic mineral acidemia with the clinical exceptions of organic metabolic acidoses and respiratory acid-base disorders","batch":1,"stem":"What is the physiologic rationale for H+-K+ exchange across cell membranes during acid-base disturbances?","choices":{"A":"It allows K+ to buffer H+ directly through a simple chemical acid-base reaction in the cytosol","B":"It is a byproduct of renal ammonia synthesis that does not contribute to cellular buffering","C":"It occurs to protect Na+-K+ ATPase from being inhibited by pH changes","D":"It lets H+ enter or leave cells to use buffers while preserving electroneutrality","E":"It is driven primarily by changes in cell volume, independent of charge balance"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Cells contain considerable buffering capacity for H+, so H+ must enter or leave them to use it; to preserve electroneutrality, H+ cannot move alone and must either be accompanied by an anion or be exchanged for another cation, which is K+. This holds in both acidemia (H+ in, K+ out) and alkalemia (H+ out, K+ in). The exchange serves electroneutrality during buffering, not protection of the Na+-K+ ATPase from pH or cell volume regulation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: this electroneutrality requirement is the mechanistic reason acidemia and alkalemia predictably shift K+, and understanding it predicts which acid-base disorders will and will not cause a K+ shift.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 -> Obj 3 — H+-K+ exchange during acid-base disturbances, including which disorders spare the shift","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Critically evaluate transcellular H+-K+ exchange during systemic pH disturbances, contrasting the predictable hypokalemia of alkalemia and hyperkalemia of inorganic mineral acidemia with the clinical exceptions of organic metabolic acidoses and respiratory acid-base disorders","batch":1,"stem":"What K+ shift is expected in acidemia caused by excess inorganic mineral acid?","choices":{"A":"H+ leaves cells and K+ enters cells, producing hypokalemia","B":"H+ and K+ both leave cells simultaneously, producing hyperkalemia through two independent mechanisms","C":"H+ enters cells and K+ leaves cells, producing hyperkalemia","D":"Neither ion shifts, since inorganic acids do not require cellular buffering","E":"H+ enters cells while Na+, not K+, leaves to preserve electroneutrality"},"correct":"C","explanation":"In acidemia from excess inorganic acid, elevated blood H+ drives H+ into cells in exchange for K+ leaving, producing hyperkalemia; this is the classic, predictable direction of the H+-K+ exchange during mineral (inorganic) acidosis.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: this predictable hyperkalemia is specifically for inorganic/mineral acidosis — recognizing when this rule does NOT apply (organic acidoses, respiratory disorders) is the higher-yield distinction tested on boards.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 -> Obj 3 — H+-K+ exchange during acid-base disturbances, including which disorders spare the shift","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Critically evaluate transcellular H+-K+ exchange during systemic pH disturbances, contrasting the predictable hypokalemia of alkalemia and hyperkalemia of inorganic mineral acidemia with the clinical exceptions of organic metabolic acidoses and respiratory acid-base disorders","batch":1,"stem":"Why does respiratory acidosis typically fail to produce the same K+ shift seen in mineral metabolic acidosis?","choices":{"A":"Respiratory acidosis does not meaningfully lower intracellular pH, so no buffering is required","B":"CO2 is lipid-soluble and crosses membranes freely, needing no exchange for K+","C":"The lungs compensate for respiratory acidosis too quickly for any K+ shift to develop","D":"CO2 is charged and requires an exchange with Na+ across the cell membrane","E":"Respiratory acidosis selectively affects extracellular buffering systems"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Because CO2 is lipid-soluble, it diffuses freely across cell membranes without carrying a charge that would require a compensatory K+ exchange, so respiratory acid-base disturbances typically do not shift K+ across cell membranes the way mineral acid-driven metabolic disturbances do.","boardPrep":"Step 1: this CO2-permeability exception, along with the organic-acid exception (lactic acidosis, DKA, salicylate poisoning), is exactly the kind of \"which acid-base disorder does NOT shift K+\" question boards favor.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 -> Obj 3 — H+-K+ exchange during acid-base disturbances, including which disorders spare the shift","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q10","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Deconstruct the physiological necessity of the extreme intracellular-to-extracellular K+ concentration gradient maintained by Na+-K+ ATPase, analyzing how shifts in this ratio alter resting membrane potential and tissue excitability, and evaluate the pathological mechanisms (hyperosmolarity, cell lysis, rhabdomyolysis, tumor lysis, strenuous exercise) that trigger cellular K+ efflux","batch":2,"stem":"A 24-year-old man is extracted from a collapsed building after being trapped for 18 hours and is found to have dark urine and markedly elevated creatine kinase. Shortly after extrication, his serum K+ rises sharply. Which mechanism most directly explains this finding?","choices":{"A":"The prolonged immobilization by itself raised his insulin sensitivity, paradoxically shifting K+ out of cells","B":"His injury triggered a beta-2 adrenergic surge that shifted K+ out of cells over several hours","C":"Dehydration from the entrapment concentrated his existing extracellular K+ without any new release","D":"Crush injury caused widespread muscle cell lysis, releasing large amounts of K+ into the extracellular fluid","E":"His kidneys became acutely unable to excrete K+, causing accumulation from ordinary dietary intake"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Crush injury causes rhabdomyolysis, in which extensive skeletal muscle cell membrane breakdown releases the large intracellular K+ pool directly into the extracellular fluid, producing rapid and often severe hyperkalemia independent of any change in renal excretion or hormonal signaling. Insulin drives K+ into cells, so greater insulin sensitivity would lower serum K+, not raise it.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: crush syndrome hyperkalemia is a classic cell-lysis mechanism; the source lists burns, rhabdomyolysis, and malignant cells destroyed by chemotherapy as other examples of cell lysis releasing K+.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 1 — cell lysis (rhabdomyolysis, burns, chemotherapy-induced cell death) releases the large intracellular K+ pool","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q11","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Deconstruct the physiological necessity of the extreme intracellular-to-extracellular K+ concentration gradient maintained by Na+-K+ ATPase, analyzing how shifts in this ratio alter resting membrane potential and tissue excitability, and evaluate the pathological mechanisms (hyperosmolarity, cell lysis, rhabdomyolysis, tumor lysis, strenuous exercise) that trigger cellular K+ efflux","batch":2,"stem":"A 19-year-old previously healthy man completes an extremely intense sprint workout and has a mildly elevated serum K+ immediately afterward, which resolves within 15 minutes of rest. Which mechanism accounts for this transient rise?","choices":{"A":"Exercise directly inhibits Na+-K+ ATPase throughout the entire body for the duration of activity","B":"Lactic acid from the sprint caused a sustained organic acidosis that shifted K+ out of cells","C":"His skeletal muscle underwent mild rhabdomyolysis, releasing a small amount of intracellular K+","D":"Sympathetic alpha-adrenergic activation during exercise persists for hours after the activity ends","E":"ATP depletion in exercising muscle opens K+ channels, letting K+ move down its gradient out of the cell"},"correct":"E","explanation":"During intense exercise, cellular ATP depletion opens K+ channels in the muscle membrane, allowing K+ to leak out down its electrochemical gradient; this shift is usually small and self-limited, reversing quickly once ATP stores are replenished during rest, and does not require any cell injury or acid-base disturbance.","boardPrep":"Step 1: exercise-induced hyperkalemia is typically mild and rapidly reversible, unlike the exercise-associated hyperkalemia seen in patients on beta-blockers or with renal impairment, where the shift can become dangerous.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 1 — exercise-induced K+ efflux, which also acts as a local vasodilator in exercising muscle","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q12","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Deconstruct the physiological necessity of the extreme intracellular-to-extracellular K+ concentration gradient maintained by Na+-K+ ATPase, analyzing how shifts in this ratio alter resting membrane potential and tissue excitability, and evaluate the pathological mechanisms (hyperosmolarity, cell lysis, rhabdomyolysis, tumor lysis, strenuous exercise) that trigger cellular K+ efflux","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old man with poorly controlled diabetes presents with severe hyperglycemia and a measured serum osmolarity well above normal, along with a serum K+ at the upper limit of normal despite clinically significant total-body K+ depletion from osmotic diuresis. Which mechanism explains why his measured K+ does not yet reflect his true deficit?","choices":{"A":"His pancreatic beta cells are secreting excess insulin, which is independently raising his measured K+","B":"His kidneys are actively secreting K+ into the tubular lumen faster than it can be measured in the blood","C":"Hyperosmolarity pulls water out of cells, dragging intracellular K+ out into the extracellular fluid with it","D":"The osmotic diuresis has concentrated his extracellular fluid without any actual K+ movement across cell membranes","E":"His acid-base status has shifted toward alkalemia, a change that would be expected to raise his measured K+"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Severe hyperosmolarity causes water to move from the intracellular to extracellular compartment along the osmotic gradient; as water leaves cells, intracellular K+ concentration rises and K+ is \"dragged\" out with the water flow into the ECF, temporarily masking an underlying total-body K+ deficit that will become apparent once the hyperosmolar state and osmotic diuresis are corrected. Renal K+ secretion removes K+ from the body and lowers plasma K+ over time rather than raising it, and alkalemia shifts K+ into cells and lowers serum K+.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: this osmotic K+-shift phenomenon is why a \"normal\" or even high measured K+ in severe hyperglycemia/DKA can be dangerously misleading — total body K+ is typically depleted and will fall further with insulin treatment.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 1 — hyperosmolarity moves water out of cells and drags K+ with it","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q13","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Analyze the hormonal and receptor-mediated pathways regulating transcellular K+ distribution, contrasting insulin-stimulated Na+-K+ ATPase upregulation with beta-2 adrenergic agonist and alpha-adrenergic agonist/beta-blocker mediated shifts","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old man with type 1 diabetes mellitus presents in diabetic ketoacidosis with a serum K+ of 6.2 mEq/L. His physician begins an insulin infusion. Which mechanism explains why his serum K+ is expected to fall over the following hours, even before his total body K+ deficit is corrected?","choices":{"A":"Insulin resolves his underlying organic acidosis via the H+-K+ exchange system","B":"Insulin stimulates Na+-K+ ATPase and moves K+ into cells independent of total body K+","C":"Insulin acutely increases renal K+ excretion and rapidly lowers total body K+","D":"Insulin stimulates aldosterone release and rapidly increases distal K+ secretion","E":"Insulin acutely expands plasma volume and dilutes his extracellular K+ concentration"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Insulin's Na+-K+ ATPase-stimulating effect shifts K+ from the extracellular into the intracellular compartment directly, independent of any change in total body K+ content, renal excretion, or acid-base status, which is why serum K+ falls rapidly with insulin therapy even though the patient's underlying total body K+ deficit (from osmotic diuresis) has not yet been replaced.","boardPrep":"Step 1: this is why insulin-treated DKA patients require careful K+ monitoring and supplementation — the transcellular shift can produce dangerous hypokalemia once the initially elevated (but often falsely reassuring) serum K+ falls.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 2 — insulin-stimulated Na+-K+ ATPase uptake of K+ into cells","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q14","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Analyze the hormonal and receptor-mediated pathways regulating transcellular K+ distribution, contrasting insulin-stimulated Na+-K+ ATPase upregulation with beta-2 adrenergic agonist and alpha-adrenergic agonist/beta-blocker mediated shifts","batch":2,"stem":"A 60-year-old man on chronic propranolol for migraine prophylaxis performs unusually strenuous yard work on a hot afternoon and develops muscle weakness with a serum K+ of 6.8 mEq/L. Which mechanism best explains why his hyperkalemia is more severe than would be expected from exercise alone?","choices":{"A":"Propranolol directly damages skeletal muscle cell membranes, independent of any receptor-level effect","B":"Propranolol increases renal K+ reabsorption acutely, adding to the exercise-induced efflux","C":"Propranolol blocks alpha-adrenergic receptors, which would otherwise have shifted K+ into cells","D":"Beta-2 blockade independently shifts K+ out of cells, adding to the exercise efflux","E":"Propranolol stimulates aldosterone release, further impairing renal K+ excretion during exercise"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Strenuous exercise shifts K+ out of cells as ATP depletion opens muscle K+ channels, which is usually small and reversible. A beta-2 antagonist such as propranolol independently shifts K+ out of cells, so its effect adds to the exercise efflux and can produce clinically significant hyperkalemia.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: nonselective beta-blockers plus strenuous exercise (or renal impairment) is a classic combination for clinically significant hyperkalemia, distinct from the usual mild, self-limited exercise-induced K+ rise.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 2 — beta-2 antagonists such as propranolol shift K+ out of cells","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q15","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Analyze the hormonal and receptor-mediated pathways regulating transcellular K+ distribution, contrasting insulin-stimulated Na+-K+ ATPase upregulation with beta-2 adrenergic agonist and alpha-adrenergic agonist/beta-blocker mediated shifts","batch":2,"stem":"A 50-year-old man in hemorrhagic shock has markedly elevated circulating catecholamines, with predominant alpha-adrenergic activation as part of the compensatory response. Which K+ change would be expected from this adrenergic state alone, independent of any acid-base or renal effect?","choices":{"A":"A shift of K+ out of cells, tending to raise serum K+","B":"A shift of K+ into cells, tending to lower serum K+","C":"No K+ shift, since alpha-adrenergic receptors do not influence Na+-K+ ATPase directly","D":"A biphasic shift, first into cells and then out, canceling to a net-zero change","E":"A shift restricted mainly to cardiac myocytes, sparing skeletal muscle and hepatocytes"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Predominant alpha-adrenergic activation, as occurs during the catecholamine surge of hemorrhagic shock, shifts K+ out of cells and tends to raise serum K+, an effect that can add to any concurrent hyperkalemia from tissue hypoperfusion or acidosis in severe shock states. The alpha-adrenergic K+ shift out of cells is described as a general cellular effect, not one confined to cardiac myocytes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: this alpha-adrenergic K+-out effect is worth pairing mentally with beta-2's opposite K+-in effect, since catecholamine states often activate both receptor types simultaneously with competing net effects.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 2 — alpha-adrenergic activation shifts K+ out of cells","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q16","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Critically evaluate transcellular H+-K+ exchange during systemic pH disturbances, contrasting the predictable hypokalemia of alkalemia and hyperkalemia of inorganic mineral acidemia with the clinical exceptions of organic metabolic acidoses and respiratory acid-base disorders","batch":2,"stem":"A 30-year-old woman with severe salicylate poisoning has a significant metabolic acidosis but her serum K+ remains within the normal range. Which explanation best accounts for the absence of the hyperkalemia that might otherwise be expected with this degree of acidemia?","choices":{"A":"Salicylate poisoning does not lower blood pH despite laboratory measurements suggesting acidosis","B":"Her kidneys are excreting K+ at an accelerated rate that exactly offsets any cellular efflux","C":"Salicylate is an organic anion that enters cells with H+, preserving electroneutrality without K+ efflux","D":"Salicylate directly stimulates Na+-K+ ATPase, actively counteracting the expected K+ shift","E":"Organic acidoses typically produce hypokalemia through a different, non-electroneutrality mechanism"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Because salicylate is an organic acid whose anion can accompany H+ into cells, electroneutrality is preserved without an exchange for K+, which is why organic metabolic acidoses like salicylate poisoning, lactic acidosis, and diabetic ketoacidosis often do not produce the hyperkalemia expected from an inorganic mineral acidosis of similar severity. Faster renal K+ excretion is not the reason, since the absence of a shift is explained by the organic anion entering cells with H+.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: recognizing organic acidoses as an exception to the \"acidosis causes hyperkalemia\" rule is one of the most frequently tested nuances in acid-base/potassium physiology.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 3 — organic acidoses (lactic acid, ketoacids, salicylic acid) spare the K+ shift because the organic anion enters cells with H+","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q17","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Critically evaluate transcellular H+-K+ exchange during systemic pH disturbances, contrasting the predictable hypokalemia of alkalemia and hyperkalemia of inorganic mineral acidemia with the clinical exceptions of organic metabolic acidoses and respiratory acid-base disorders","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old man receives a rapid intravenous infusion of sodium bicarbonate during treatment of a drug overdose, and his arterial pH rises from 7.40 to 7.55 over 1 hour. During the same hour, his serum K+ falls from 4.3 to 3.6 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L) with no change in his urinary K+ excretion. Which mechanism best explains the early fall in serum K+?","choices":{"A":"Bicarbonate directly stimulating the muscle Na+-K+ ATPase","B":"H+ leaving cells for buffering while K+ moves into them","C":"Hyposmolar ECF drawing water, and with it K+, into cells","D":"Bicarbonate raising aldosterone and principal cell secretion","E":"Lipid-soluble CO2 carrying K+ into cells as it diffuses"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In alkalemia, the H+ concentration in blood is decreased, so H+ leaves cells to help buffer the ECF and, to preserve electroneutrality, K+ enters cells in exchange, producing hypokalemia; this internal shift happens within the hour, before any renal change. Stimulation of the Na+-K+ ATPase is the mechanism of insulin and beta-2 agonists, not of alkalemia. A sodium bicarbonate load raises rather than lowers ECF osmolarity, so a hyposmolar shift of water into cells does not apply. A rise in aldosterone and principal cell secretion would increase urinary K+ excretion, which did not change. CO2 crosses membranes freely without exchanging for K+, which is why respiratory acid-base disorders typically cause no K+ shift.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Shifts into cells (hypokalemia): insulin, beta-2 agonists, alpha antagonists, alkalosis, hyposmolarity; shifts out of cells (hyperkalemia): insulin deficiency, beta-2 antagonists, alpha agonists, acidosis, hyperosmolarity, cell lysis, exercise. COMLEX: Recheck K+ when giving bicarbonate, because alkalinization moves K+ into cells.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 2 — alkalemia also raises principal cell K+ secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q18","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Critically evaluate transcellular H+-K+ exchange during systemic pH disturbances, contrasting the predictable hypokalemia of alkalemia and hyperkalemia of inorganic mineral acidemia with the clinical exceptions of organic metabolic acidoses and respiratory acid-base disorders","batch":2,"stem":"A 65-year-old man with chronic kidney disease develops a mineral (non-organic) metabolic acidosis from progressive renal failure, with a serum K+ that rises in parallel with his falling serum bicarbonate. Which sequence of events best explains this parallel change?","choices":{"A":"Rising H+ enters cells in exchange for K+ leaving, raising extracellular K+ as bicarbonate falls","B":"Falling bicarbonate directly stimulates renal K+ secretion, which then raises his serum K+","C":"His failing kidneys are unable to reabsorb K+, so excess dietary K+ remains unabsorbed in the gut","D":"Rising H+ stimulates aldosterone release, which is the primary driver of his observed hyperkalemia","E":"His acidosis has increased his glomerular filtration rate, paradoxically increasing K+ retention"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In mineral (inorganic) metabolic acidosis, such as that from advanced renal failure with reduced acid excretion, rising extracellular H+ enters cells in direct exchange for K+, which leaves cells to preserve electroneutrality; this transcellular shift raises extracellular K+ in parallel with the falling bicarbonate, compounding whatever reduction in renal K+ excretion is also present from the kidney disease itself.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: chronic kidney disease patients are especially vulnerable to acidosis-driven hyperkalemia because both the transcellular H+-K+ shift and reduced renal K+ excretion push serum K+ upward simultaneously.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 3 — acidemia drives H+ into cells and K+ out, producing hyperkalemia","isHighYield":true},{"id":"ex2t_sdl16_q1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Deconstruct the physiological necessity of the extreme intracellular-to-extracellular K+ concentration gradient maintained by Na+-K+ ATPase, analyzing how shifts in this ratio alter resting membrane potential and tissue excitability, and evaluate the pathological mechanisms (hyperosmolarity, cell lysis, rhabdomyolysis, tumor lysis, strenuous exercise) that trigger cellular K+ efflux","batch":3,"stem":"A 19-year-old man is brought in after a crush injury, and his serum potassium is 7.2 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L) with a creatinine of 2.4 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL). His electrocardiogram shows peaked T waves and a widened QRS complex. Which change in his cardiac myocytes best explains the widened QRS?","choices":{"A":"Hyperpolarization that moves the resting membrane away from threshold","B":"Blockade of L-type calcium channels during the plateau","C":"Sustained depolarization that inactivates fast sodium channels","D":"Inhibition of the Na+-K+-ATPase by extracellular potassium","E":"Slowed repolarization through delayed rectifier channels"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The resting membrane potential is set mainly by the K+ concentration gradient. A high extracellular K+ shrinks that gradient and depolarizes the cell, and sustained depolarization closes the inactivation gates of fast voltage-gated Na+ channels, so fewer channels are available, the phase 0 upstroke slows, and the QRS widens. Hyperpolarization away from threshold is the effect of hypokalemia. L-type calcium channels shape the plateau and are the target of verapamil-type drugs, not of hyperkalemia. Extracellular potassium stimulates rather than inhibits the Na+-K+-ATPase. Hyperkalemia raises K+ conductance and speeds repolarization, which is why T waves become peaked rather than delayed. Sources: Exam 2 lecture notes (SDL 16, the K+ gradient and membrane excitability); Costanzo Physiology Ch. 1 (hyperkalemia depolarizes the membrane and closes Na+ inactivation gates).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hyperkalemia ECG sequence is peaked T waves, PR prolongation, QRS widening, a sine-wave pattern, and then ventricular fibrillation. COMLEX: give intravenous calcium first to stabilize the membrane, then shift potassium into cells.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 -> Obj 2 — principal-cell ROMK secretion is the route that finally clears potassium released from injured muscle","isHighYield":true},{"id":"ex2t_sdl16_q2","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Analyze the hormonal and receptor-mediated pathways regulating transcellular K+ distribution, contrasting insulin-stimulated Na+-K+ ATPase upregulation with beta-2 adrenergic agonist and alpha-adrenergic agonist/beta-blocker mediated shifts","batch":3,"stem":"A 24-year-old woman with an asthma exacerbation receives repeated nebulized albuterol treatments. Her serum potassium falls from 4.2 to 3.0 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L) over 2 hours. Which mechanism best explains this fall?","choices":{"A":"Alpha-adrenergic stimulation of potassium exit from cells","B":"Increased potassium secretion by collecting duct ROMK channels","C":"Insulin deficiency that limits cellular potassium uptake","D":"Aldosterone-driven potassium loss in the distal nephron","E":"Beta-2 stimulation of the muscle Na+-K+-ATPase pump"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Albuterol stimulates beta-2 receptors, raising cAMP and Na+-K+-ATPase activity in skeletal muscle and liver, which moves K+ into cells within minutes; the effect adds to that of insulin and is used to treat hyperkalemia. A fall this large within 2 hours reflects a transcellular shift rather than renal loss. Alpha-adrenergic stimulation moves K+ out of cells and raises serum potassium. ROMK secretion and aldosterone-driven distal loss are slower renal mechanisms and are not stimulated by albuterol. Insulin deficiency would raise serum potassium. Sources: Exam 2 lecture notes (SDL 16, hormonal regulation of transcellular K+ distribution); Costanzo Physiology Ch. 6 (beta-adrenergic and alpha-adrenergic effects on K+ shifts); Guyton and Hall Ch. 30 (beta-adrenergic stimulation and cellular K+ uptake).","boardPrep":"Step 1: insulin, beta-2 agonists, and alkalosis shift potassium into cells; beta-blockers, alpha agonists, hyperosmolarity, and cell lysis shift it out. COMLEX: shifting agents buy time in hyperkalemia, but removal requires binders, diuretics, or dialysis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 -> Obj 1 — renal excretion rather than cellular shifting sets long-term potassium balance","isHighYield":true},{"id":"ex2t_sdl16_q3","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Critically evaluate transcellular H+-K+ exchange during systemic pH disturbances, contrasting the predictable hypokalemia of alkalemia and hyperkalemia of inorganic mineral acidemia with the clinical exceptions of organic metabolic acidoses and respiratory acid-base disorders","batch":3,"stem":"A 17-year-old boy with type 1 diabetes has Kussmaul respirations. Serum glucose is 620 mg/dL (reference range: 70–100 mg/dL), potassium is 5.7 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L), bicarbonate is 7 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L), and arterial pH is 7.20 (reference range: 7.35–7.45). Which mechanism best accounts for his hyperkalemia?","choices":{"A":"Hydrogen ion entry into cells exchanged for potassium","B":"Insulin deficiency with hyperosmolar water exit from cells","C":"Beta-2 receptor blockade of cellular potassium uptake","D":"Respiratory acidosis shifting potassium out of cells","E":"Reduced glomerular potassium filtration into the tubule"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In diabetic ketoacidosis, the lack of insulin removes the main stimulus for Na+-K+-ATPase–mediated K+ uptake, and hyperglycemia raises plasma osmolality so water leaves cells and carries K+ with it by solvent drag. Organic acidoses such as ketoacidosis and lactic acidosis cause little transcellular H+-K+ exchange, because the organic anion enters cells along with H+, so the classic mineral-acidosis shift does not explain the hyperkalemia. Beta-2 blockade is not part of this presentation. Kussmaul breathing lowers PaCO2 as respiratory compensation, which argues against a respiratory acidosis. Osmotic diuresis increases renal K+ loss, which is why total body potassium is depleted despite the high serum level. Sources: Exam 2 lecture notes (SDL 16, transcellular H+-K+ exchange and its exceptions); Costanzo Physiology Ch. 6 (organic acidosis and hyperosmolarity in K+ shifts); Costanzo Physiology Ch. 1 (hyperkalemia in uncontrolled diabetes).","boardPrep":"Step 1: patients with DKA have total body potassium depletion despite hyperkalemia, and serum potassium falls once insulin is given. COMLEX: replace potassium before or with insulin when the serum level is low or normal.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 -> Obj 1 — ketoacidosis is a classic high-anion-gap metabolic acidosis","isHighYield":true}],"17":[{"id":"sdl17_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze K+ filtration and segmental handling along the nephron, contrasting fixed reabsorption in the PCT and thick ascending limb with variable distal transport, and deconstructing alpha-intercalated cell H+-K+ ATPase-mediated reabsorption in K+ depletion states","batch":1,"stem":"What fraction of the filtered K+ load is reabsorbed by the proximal convoluted tubule?","choices":{"A":"About 20%, reabsorbed actively via the Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransporter","B":"About 90%, reabsorbed through a dedicated apical K+-H+ exchanger","C":"About 67%, reabsorbed as a part of isosmotic fluid reabsorption","D":"About 40%, reabsorbed by facilitated diffusion driven by insulin","E":"About 10%, reabsorbed passively behind an osmotic urea gradient"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The proximal convoluted tubule reabsorbs roughly 67% of the filtered K+ load as an incidental part of isosmotic fluid and solute reabsorption, not through a K+-specific regulated transporter; the thick ascending limb accounts for another 20% via the Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransporter, leaving the distal nephron to fine-tune the remainder.","boardPrep":"Step 1: memorize the K+ handling percentages (67% PCT, 20% TAL, then variable distal reabsorption or secretion) the same way you memorize Na+ and water handling by segment.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 -> Obj 1 — fixed proximal/TAL K+ reabsorption versus the variable distal segments that fine-tune K+ balance","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze K+ filtration and segmental handling along the nephron, contrasting fixed reabsorption in the PCT and thick ascending limb with variable distal transport, and deconstructing alpha-intercalated cell H+-K+ ATPase-mediated reabsorption in K+ depletion states","batch":1,"stem":"By what mechanism does the thick ascending limb reabsorb roughly 20% of the filtered K+ load?","choices":{"A":"K+ enters via a basolateral H+-K+ ATPase and exits through apical anion channels into the lumen","B":"K+ enters via the apical Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransporter and exits basolateral K+ channels","C":"K+ enters via paracellular diffusion driven by a lumen-negative transepithelial potential","D":"K+ enters via facilitated diffusion through channels gated by ambient tubular flow rate","E":"K+ enters via active cotransport with glucose across the apical brush border membrane"},"correct":"B","explanation":"K+ enters thick ascending limb cells across the apical membrane via the Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransporter (NKCC2) and can then either recycle back into the lumen through apical K+ channels, which generates the lumen-positive potential that drives paracellular cation reabsorption, or exit across the basolateral membrane through K+ channels to be reabsorbed into blood.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: this same NKCC2 transporter is the target of loop diuretics, which is why loop diuretics block TAL K+ reabsorption in addition to increasing distal K+ secretion.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 -> Obj 1 — fixed proximal/TAL K+ reabsorption versus the variable distal segments that fine-tune K+ balance","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze K+ filtration and segmental handling along the nephron, contrasting fixed reabsorption in the PCT and thick ascending limb with variable distal transport, and deconstructing alpha-intercalated cell H+-K+ ATPase-mediated reabsorption in K+ depletion states","batch":1,"stem":"Under a chronic low-K+ diet, which mechanism allows the kidney to further conserve K+ beyond fixed proximal and TAL reabsorption?","choices":{"A":"Principal cells switch from secreting to reabsorbing K+ using the same apical ROMK channels","B":"The thick ascending limb doubles its Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransport activity to reabsorb more K+","C":"The proximal tubule upregulates a dedicated basolateral K+ reabsorptive channel","D":"Alpha-intercalated cells reabsorb K+ via an apical H+-K+ ATPase pumping K+ into the cell","E":"The collecting duct principal cells reverse their basolateral Na+-K+ ATPase to pull K+ inward"},"correct":"D","explanation":"On a low-K+ diet, alpha-intercalated cells in the late distal tubule and collecting duct express an apical H+-K+ ATPase, similar to the one in gastric parietal cells, that actively pumps H+ into the lumen while pumping K+ from the lumen into the cell; the K+ then diffuses into blood, allowing urinary K+ excretion to fall as low as 1% of the filtered load. K+ reabsorption in the thick ascending limb is constant under most conditions, so it does not double on a low-K+ diet.","boardPrep":"Step 1: alpha-intercalated cells reabsorb K+ and secrete H+ using the same H+-K+ ATPase, making this a favorite integration point between K+ balance and acid-base physiology.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 -> Obj 1 — fixed proximal/TAL K+ reabsorption versus the variable distal segments that fine-tune K+ balance","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Deconstruct principal cell electrogenic K+ secretion mechanics (basolateral Na+-K+ ATPase, apical ROMK/BK channels), evaluating how aldosterone, dietary K+, alkalosis, high tubular flow, and luminal anions regulate secretion","batch":1,"stem":"What is the immediate source of the K+ that principal cells secrete into the tubular lumen?","choices":{"A":"K+ filtered directly across the apical membrane from the tubular lumen","B":"K+ released from intracellular buffering stores during acidosis","C":"K+ absorbed from dietary intake through a dedicated luminal transporter","D":"K+ generated locally by cellular metabolism of ATP within the cell","E":"K+ pumped in from peritubular blood by the basolateral Na+-K+ ATPase"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Principal cells secrete K+ that was first pumped in from the blood by the basolateral Na+-K+ ATPase, which maintains a high intracellular K+ concentration; this K+ then diffuses down its electrochemical gradient into the lumen through apical K+ channels rather than being recycled back across the basolateral membrane. K+ is not produced by ATP breakdown in the cell; intracellular K+ comes from uptake from the blood by the Na+-K+ ATPase.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: this is the same basolateral Na+-K+ ATPase used for Na+ reabsorption in principal cells, tying K+ secretion mechanistically to Na+ reabsorption and aldosterone action.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 -> Obj 2 — the electrochemical-gradient mechanics that principal cells use to secrete K+ into the lumen","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Deconstruct principal cell electrogenic K+ secretion mechanics (basolateral Na+-K+ ATPase, apical ROMK/BK channels), evaluating how aldosterone, dietary K+, alkalosis, high tubular flow, and luminal anions regulate secretion","batch":1,"stem":"What quantity most directly determines the magnitude of K+ secretion by principal cells at any given moment?","choices":{"A":"The K+ electrochemical gradient across the cell's apical membrane","B":"The absolute plasma aldosterone concentration measured at that moment","C":"The total number of basolateral Na+-K+ ATPase pumps expressed by the cell","D":"The rate of glomerular filtration delivered to the distal nephron","E":"The plasma pH measured simultaneously in the systemic circulation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Aldosterone, dietary K+, acid-base status, and tubular flow rate largely regulate K+ secretion by changing the electrochemical gradient for K+ across the apical membrane (together with the availability of apical K+ channels); that gradient, not any single hormone or channel count in isolation, is the most direct determinant of secretion rate. The number of basolateral pumps matters only insofar as it raises intracellular K+ and so the luminal gradient, as aldosterone does by inducing more pumps.","boardPrep":"Step 1: frame every K+ secretion question around this one governing principle, then work out whether a given factor raises or lowers the apical K+ gradient.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 -> Obj 2 — the electrochemical-gradient mechanics that principal cells use to secrete K+ into the lumen","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Deconstruct principal cell electrogenic K+ secretion mechanics (basolateral Na+-K+ ATPase, apical ROMK/BK channels), evaluating how aldosterone, dietary K+, alkalosis, high tubular flow, and luminal anions regulate secretion","batch":1,"stem":"How does an increase in tubular flow rate through the collecting duct raise K+ secretion?","choices":{"A":"Faster flow directly stimulates aldosterone release from the adrenal cortex","B":"Faster flow keeps luminal K+ dilute, steepening the apical K+ exit gradient","C":"Faster flow increases basolateral Na+-K+ ATPase expression within minutes","D":"Faster flow raises intracellular pH, indirectly opening more ROMK channels","E":"Faster flow increases paracellular Na+ reabsorption in the collecting duct"},"correct":"B","explanation":"When tubular flow rate increases, the secreted K+ is washed downstream faster, keeping luminal K+ concentration low; this widens the electrochemical gradient between the K+-rich cell and the diluted lumen, increasing the driving force for K+ exit through apical channels, which is why diuretics that raise distal flow also raise K+ excretion.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: this flow-dependence explains why any diuretic that raises distal flow (loop, thiazide) tends to increase kaliuresis through this shared flow effect, whereas K+-sparing diuretics do not.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 -> Obj 2 — the electrochemical-gradient mechanics that principal cells use to secrete K+ into the lumen","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Analyze the molecular biophysics of intracellular magnesium gating on luminal ROMK channels, formulating a clinical rationale for why magnesium replacement is mandatory to correct refractory hypokalemia","batch":1,"stem":"How does intracellular magnesium regulate luminal ROMK channels in principal cells?","choices":{"A":"Intracellular Mg2+ blocks ROMK, so Mg2+ depletion opens the channel","B":"Intracellular Mg2+ activates ROMK, so Mg2+ depletion closes the channel","C":"Intracellular Mg2+ regulates ROMK gene transcription over several days","D":"Intracellular Mg2+ raises ROMK open probability by binding luminal K+","E":"Intracellular Mg2+ has no direct effect on ROMK, acting through aldosterone"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The luminal K+ channels (ROMK) of principal cells are gated by intracellular Mg2+: Mg2+ depletion and decreased intracellular Mg2+ open the channels, so K+ secretion rises and hypokalemia results, while normal intracellular Mg2+ keeps the channels from being held open.","boardPrep":"Step 1: this direct channel-gating mechanism, not a genomic or hormonal one, is the specific detail boards test to distinguish Mg2+ from aldosterone-type regulators of K+ secretion.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 -> Obj 3 — how intracellular Mg2+ gates ROMK channels and why hypomagnesemia causes refractory hypokalemia","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Analyze the molecular biophysics of intracellular magnesium gating on luminal ROMK channels, formulating a clinical rationale for why magnesium replacement is mandatory to correct refractory hypokalemia","batch":1,"stem":"Aldosterone increases K+ secretion by principal cells partly by increasing Na+ entry through ENaC and the number of basolateral Na+-K+ ATPase pumps, which together raise intracellular K+. Which separate action of aldosterone adds to this effect?","choices":{"A":"It adds H+-K+ ATPase pumps to the luminal membrane","B":"It raises intracellular Mg2+ that holds ROMK open","C":"It inserts AQP2 channels that speed luminal flow","D":"It adds more K+ channels to the luminal membrane","E":"It blocks luminal ENaC so the lumen is less negative"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Costanzo lists, as a separate effect, that aldosterone increases the number of K+ channels in the luminal membrane, which coordinates with the larger driving force created by greater Na+ entry and more Na+-K+ ATPase pumps. Luminal H+-K+ ATPase belongs to alpha-intercalated cells and reabsorbs K+, mainly on a low-K+ diet. Intracellular Mg2+ blocks ROMK, so more Mg2+ would reduce rather than increase secretion; it is Mg2+ depletion that opens the channels. AQP2 insertion is the action of ADH on water permeability, not of aldosterone on K+ channels. Blocking ENaC is the action of amiloride, which makes the lumen less negative and reduces K+ secretion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Aldosterone raises K+ secretion three ways: more ENaC (more Na+ entry and pumping), more Na+-K+ ATPase, and more luminal K+ channels. COMLEX: Hyperaldosteronism causes hypokalemia, and spironolactone or amiloride reverses it by removing these effects.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 2 — the same aldosterone-induced proteins drive principal cell Na+ reabsorption","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Analyze the molecular biophysics of intracellular magnesium gating on luminal ROMK channels, formulating a clinical rationale for why magnesium replacement is mandatory to correct refractory hypokalemia","batch":1,"stem":"Why is magnesium repletion often necessary to correct hypokalemia caused by concurrent hypomagnesemia?","choices":{"A":"Oral potassium is poorly absorbed from the gut whenever magnesium is deficient","B":"Magnesium deficiency accelerates renal clearance of supplemented potassium directly","C":"Continued Mg2+ deficiency keeps ROMK unblocked despite ongoing K+ supplementation","D":"Magnesium deficiency shifts supplemented potassium into cells before it can act","E":"Magnesium deficiency blunts the intestinal signal that normally triggers insulin release"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Supplemented K+ raises plasma K+ only briefly if the underlying ROMK channel block from Mg2+ remains lost; without that block restored, principal cells continue secreting K+ at an elevated rate, so plasma K+ falls again unless the Mg2+ deficiency itself is corrected.","boardPrep":"Step 1: this is the mechanistic basis for the classic teaching that refractory hypokalemia should prompt a serum magnesium check before simply increasing the potassium dose.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 -> Obj 3 — how intracellular Mg2+ gates ROMK channels and why hypomagnesemia causes refractory hypokalemia","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q10","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze K+ filtration and segmental handling along the nephron, contrasting fixed reabsorption in the PCT and thick ascending limb with variable distal transport, and deconstructing alpha-intercalated cell H+-K+ ATPase-mediated reabsorption in K+ depletion states","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman adheres to a strict low-potassium renal diet for months. Her 24-hour urine K+ excretion is measured at only 1% of her filtered K+ load. Which additional reabsorptive mechanism accounts for this conservation beyond the fixed proximal and loop reabsorption?","choices":{"A":"Principal cells in the late distal tubule, via reversal of their basolateral Na+-K+ ATPase","B":"The proximal convoluted tubule, via an inducible basolateral K+ reabsorptive channel","C":"The thick ascending limb, via a threefold increase in Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransporter density","D":"Alpha-intercalated cells in the late distal segments, via an apical H+-K+ ATPase","E":"The macula densa, via tubuloglomerular feedback that reduces filtered K+ delivery"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Proximal and thick ascending limb K+ reabsorption stay essentially fixed regardless of dietary intake, so the additional conservation seen on a chronic low-K+ diet comes from the late distal tubule and collecting duct, where alpha-intercalated cells reabsorb K+ through an apical H+-K+ ATPase, allowing urinary excretion to fall to as little as 1% of the filtered load.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: this vignette applies the alpha-intercalated cell H+-K+ ATPase mechanism to an extreme dietary K+ conservation scenario.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 1 — alpha-intercalated cell H+-K+ ATPase reabsorption in K+ depletion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q11","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze K+ filtration and segmental handling along the nephron, contrasting fixed reabsorption in the PCT and thick ascending limb with variable distal transport, and deconstructing alpha-intercalated cell H+-K+ ATPase-mediated reabsorption in K+ depletion states","batch":2,"stem":"A 28-year-old man doubles his dietary potassium intake for two weeks as part of a nutrition experiment. Which statement correctly describes how his proximal tubule and thick ascending limb respond to this change?","choices":{"A":"Their fractional K+ reabsorption stays fixed while the distal nephron excretes the extra load","B":"Their fractional K+ reabsorption falls sharply to compensate for the added dietary K+ load","C":"Their fractional K+ reabsorption rises sharply to buffer the added dietary K+ load","D":"The thick ascending limb switches from reabsorbing to secreting K+ during high intake","E":"The proximal tubule begins actively secreting K+ once dietary intake exceeds baseline"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The proximal tubule and thick ascending limb reabsorb a relatively constant fraction of filtered K+ regardless of dietary intake, since their K+ handling is incidental to bulk fluid and NaCl transport rather than being independently regulated; all of the physiological adjustment to a higher K+ diet occurs in the distal tubule and collecting duct through increased principal cell secretion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: this fixed-versus-variable segmental split is why dietary K+ adaptation is attributed to the distal nephron rather than the proximal tubule or loop.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 1 — fixed proximal and thick ascending limb K+ reabsorption versus variable distal handling","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q12","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze K+ filtration and segmental handling along the nephron, contrasting fixed reabsorption in the PCT and thick ascending limb with variable distal transport, and deconstructing alpha-intercalated cell H+-K+ ATPase-mediated reabsorption in K+ depletion states","batch":2,"stem":"A 60-year-old man with heart failure is started on a loop diuretic and subsequently develops hypokalemia. Beyond the increased distal Na+ delivery that raises principal cell K+ secretion, which additional mechanism specific to the thick ascending limb contributes to his K+ loss?","choices":{"A":"The loop diuretic stimulates the alpha-intercalated cell H+-K+ ATPase to secrete additional K+","B":"The loop diuretic increases proximal tubular K+ secretion through an osmotic diuresis effect","C":"The loop diuretic directly opens ROMK channels in the thick ascending limb basolateral membrane","D":"The loop diuretic raises circulating insulin, shifting K+ from the ECF into distal tubular cells","E":"The loop diuretic blocks the Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransporter, inhibiting the K+ reabsorption there"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Loop diuretics inhibit the apical Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransporter of the thick ascending limb, which both blocks the roughly 20% of filtered K+ that this segment normally reabsorbs and increases distal Na+ delivery to principal cells; both effects add together to produce the pronounced kaliuresis and hypokalemia seen with loop diuretic therapy.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: contrast this dual mechanism with K+-sparing diuretics, which act only on principal cells and therefore do not cause kaliuresis at all.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 1 — loop diuretics inhibit NKCC2 and thick ascending limb K+ reabsorption","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q13","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Deconstruct principal cell electrogenic K+ secretion mechanics (basolateral Na+-K+ ATPase, apical ROMK/BK channels), evaluating how aldosterone, dietary K+, alkalosis, high tubular flow, and luminal anions regulate secretion","batch":2,"stem":"A 50-year-old man with an aldosterone-secreting adrenal adenoma has hypertension, plasma K+ of 2.1 mEq/L, and inappropriately high urinary K+ excretion. Which mechanism best explains why elevated aldosterone lowers his plasma K+?","choices":{"A":"Aldosterone acts on alpha-intercalated cells to secrete K+ while leaving principal cells unchanged","B":"Aldosterone increases glomerular filtration of K+, raising the amount delivered to the distal nephron","C":"Aldosterone increases Na+-K+ ATPase activity, raising intracellular K+ and thus its secretion","D":"Aldosterone inhibits the alpha-intercalated cell H+-K+ ATPase, blocking compensatory K+ reabsorption","E":"Aldosterone lowers tubular flow rate, which paradoxically increases the luminal K+ gradient"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Aldosterone induces synthesis of luminal Na+ channels and basolateral Na+-K+ ATPase in principal cells; increased Na+ entry and pumping raises intracellular K+ concentration, which increases the driving force for K+ secretion into the lumen, while aldosterone also separately increases the number of luminal K+ channels available to carry that secreted K+.","boardPrep":"Step 1: primary hyperaldosteronism (Conn syndrome) is a classic vignette pairing hypertension from Na+ retention with hypokalemia from this same principal cell mechanism.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 2 — aldosterone-driven Na+ entry and Na+-K+ ATPase activity raise principal cell K+ secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q14","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Deconstruct principal cell electrogenic K+ secretion mechanics (basolateral Na+-K+ ATPase, apical ROMK/BK channels), evaluating how aldosterone, dietary K+, alkalosis, high tubular flow, and luminal anions regulate secretion","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old woman on chronic thiazide therapy for hypertension develops a plasma K+ of 3.0 mEq/L. Which mechanism most directly explains how a diuretic acting on the distal convoluted tubule produces this hypokalemia?","choices":{"A":"Blocking NCC directly increases the number of luminal ROMK channels in principal cells","B":"Blocking NCC delivers more Na+ and fluid to principal cells, raising K+ secretion","C":"Blocking NCC raises plasma aldosterone independent of any change in Na+ delivery","D":"Blocking NCC reduces proximal tubular K+ reabsorption through a compensatory reflex","E":"Blocking NCC increases plasma pH, indirectly opening more luminal K+ channels"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Thiazides inhibit the Na+-Cl- cotransporter (NCC) in the early distal tubule, which is upstream of the principal cells; the resulting increase in Na+ delivery to principal cells raises Na+ entry and basolateral Na+-K+ ATPase activity, increasing intracellular K+ and the driving force for its secretion, producing the same downstream effect as the increased Na+ delivery seen with loop diuretics. Proximal tubule K+ reabsorption stays constant under most conditions, so no compensatory proximal change accounts for the K+ loss.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: loop and thiazide diuretics share this increased distal Na+ delivery mechanism, a common cause of diuretic-induced hypokalemia.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 2 — thiazides deliver more Na+ to principal cells, raising K+ secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q15","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Deconstruct principal cell electrogenic K+ secretion mechanics (basolateral Na+-K+ ATPase, apical ROMK/BK channels), evaluating how aldosterone, dietary K+, alkalosis, high tubular flow, and luminal anions regulate secretion","batch":2,"stem":"A 35-year-old woman with protracted vomiting develops metabolic alkalosis and a plasma K+ of 2.8 mEq/L. Which mechanism links her alkalemia to this degree of hypokalemia?","choices":{"A":"Falling extracellular H+ increases intercalated cell K+ reabsorption, raising plasma K+","B":"Falling extracellular H+ increases K+ filtration by dilating afferent arterioles","C":"Falling extracellular H+ suppresses aldosterone, paradoxically increasing K+ secretion","D":"Falling extracellular H+ drives H+ out of cells in exchange for K+, raising K+ secretion","E":"Falling extracellular H+ raises tubular flow rate independent of any volume contraction"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In alkalemia, the deficit of extracellular H+ drives H+ out of cells to help buffer the blood, and K+ enters cells in exchange to preserve electroneutrality; this raises intracellular K+ concentration in principal cells specifically, increasing the driving force for K+ secretion into the lumen and producing hypokalemia.","boardPrep":"Step 1: alkalemia from vomiting increases principal cell K+ secretion through the H+-K+ exchange across the basolateral membrane, which is the link between alkalosis and hypokalemia.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 2 — alkalosis raises intracellular K+ and increases principal cell K+ secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q16","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Analyze the molecular biophysics of intracellular magnesium gating on luminal ROMK channels, formulating a clinical rationale for why magnesium replacement is mandatory to correct refractory hypokalemia","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man on chronic loop diuretic therapy has persistent hypokalemia that fails to improve despite two weeks of oral potassium supplementation. His plasma magnesium is found to be low. Which mechanism best explains why his hypokalemia has been refractory to potassium alone?","choices":{"A":"Depleted intracellular Mg2+ increases intestinal potassium excretion, offsetting his oral supplementation","B":"Depleted intracellular Mg2+ leaves ROMK channels unblocked, so K+ secretion continues unabated","C":"Depleted intracellular Mg2+ directly raises plasma aldosterone, worsening his ongoing K+ loss","D":"Depleted intracellular Mg2+ increases renal clearance of the supplemented potassium within hours","E":"Depleted intracellular Mg2+ shifts the supplemented potassium into cells before it reaches the plasma"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Because intracellular Mg2+ normally blocks luminal ROMK channels, his ongoing Mg2+ deficiency leaves these channels open regardless of how much potassium he ingests, so principal cells keep secreting K+ at an elevated rate; his hypokalemia will remain refractory until his magnesium deficit is corrected alongside continued potassium supplementation.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: when hypokalemia does not correct with K+ repletion, check Mg2+; intracellular Mg2+ depletion opens luminal K+ channels, so Mg2+ must be repleted to correct the hypokalemia.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 3 — Mg2+ depletion opens luminal ROMK channels, so Mg2+ must be repleted to correct hypokalemia","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q17","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Analyze the molecular biophysics of intracellular magnesium gating on luminal ROMK channels, formulating a clinical rationale for why magnesium replacement is mandatory to correct refractory hypokalemia","batch":2,"stem":"A 62-year-old man has persistent hypokalemia despite escalating doses of oral potassium chloride tablets, and his plasma magnesium is low. Which single intervention is most likely to correct his plasma potassium?","choices":{"A":"Increasing his oral potassium chloride dose substantially","B":"Repleting his Mg2+ deficit so the luminal ROMK channels close","C":"Starting intravenous aldosterone-receptor antagonism","D":"Alkalinizing his urine with intravenous sodium bicarbonate","E":"Giving intravenous insulin together with dextrose"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Intracellular Mg2+ depletion opens the luminal ROMK channels of principal cells, driving ongoing K+ secretion regardless of oral potassium dosing; only repleting the magnesium deficit closes these channels and allows plasma potassium to normalize and stay normal. Aldosterone blockade does not close the Mg2+-gated channels, bicarbonate adds luminal anion and alkalosis, both of which increase K+ secretion, and insulin with dextrose shifts K+ into cells, lowering plasma K+ further.","boardPrep":"Step 1: refractory hypokalemia with low Mg2+ requires Mg2+ repletion, because potassium dosing, aldosterone antagonism, or luminal anions do not correct the channel gating.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 3 — Mg2+ repletion is required to correct hypokalemia from Mg2+ depletion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q18","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Analyze the molecular biophysics of intracellular magnesium gating on luminal ROMK channels, formulating a clinical rationale for why magnesium replacement is mandatory to correct refractory hypokalemia","batch":2,"stem":"A 40-year-old man with poor oral intake presents with muscle weakness and a plasma K+ of 2.7 mEq/L that improves only transiently with intravenous potassium. His plasma magnesium is also low. Which explanation accounts for the transient response to potassium alone?","choices":{"A":"Persistent Mg2+ deficiency keeps ROMK channels open, so the kidney keeps secreting the infused potassium","B":"Persistent Mg2+ deficiency accelerates gastrointestinal loss of the infused potassium directly","C":"Persistent Mg2+ deficiency increases insulin secretion, shifting the infused potassium into cells","D":"Persistent Mg2+ deficiency raises plasma aldosterone, which worsens his ongoing potassium loss","E":"Persistent Mg2+ deficiency reduces hepatic potassium storage, releasing it slowly back into plasma"},"correct":"A","explanation":"As long as intracellular Mg2+ remains low, the luminal ROMK channels stay open and principal cells continue secreting whatever potassium is infused, so plasma K+ rises only briefly unless magnesium is repleted alongside the potassium.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: combined hypomagnesemia and hypokalemia with only a transient response to K+ repletion tests whether the ROMK-gating mechanism is understood, not just memorized as a lab association.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 3 — Mg2+ depletion opens luminal ROMK channels and sustains K+ secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"ex2t_sdl17_q1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze K+ filtration and segmental handling along the nephron, contrasting fixed reabsorption in the PCT and thick ascending limb with variable distal transport, and deconstructing alpha-intercalated cell H+-K+ ATPase-mediated reabsorption in K+ depletion states","batch":3,"stem":"A 33-year-old woman with anorexia nervosa and very low food intake denies vomiting or laxative use. Her serum potassium is 2.8 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L), and her 24-hour urine potassium is 11 mEq/24 h (reference range: 25–125 mEq/24 h). Upregulation of which transport process allows net potassium reabsorption in her distal nephron?","choices":{"A":"Apical ROMK channel activity in principal cells","B":"Solvent drag reabsorption in the proximal convoluted tubule","C":"Apical H+-K+-ATPase activity in alpha-intercalated cells","D":"NKCC2-mediated uptake in the thick ascending limb","E":"Basolateral Na+-K+-ATPase activity in principal cells"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The proximal tubule and thick ascending limb reabsorb a roughly constant fraction of filtered K+ (about 67% and 20%), so adaptation to intake happens in the distal nephron. On a low-K+ intake, principal-cell secretion falls and alpha-intercalated cells upregulate the apical H+-K+-ATPase, which reabsorbs K+ in exchange for secreted H+ and allows urinary potassium to fall to about 1% of the filtered load (under 10 mEq/day in the course notes). ROMK activity mediates secretion and is downregulated in potassium depletion. Proximal solvent drag and thick ascending limb NKCC2 uptake are fixed fractions that do not adjust to intake. Basolateral Na+-K+-ATPase activity in principal cells loads K+ for secretion rather than conserving it. Sources: Exam 2 lecture notes (SDL 17, K+ filtration and segmental handling); Costanzo Physiology Ch. 6 (alpha-intercalated cell H+-K+-ATPase on a low-K+ diet).","boardPrep":"Step 1: a low urine potassium in hypokalemia points to poor intake or lower gastrointestinal loss, such as diarrhea or laxatives, rather than renal wasting. COMLEX: in eating disorders, check for occult vomiting or laxative use when urine findings do not fit.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 -> Obj 2 — the same alpha-intercalated cell secretes H+ and generates new bicarbonate","isHighYield":true},{"id":"ex2t_sdl17_q2","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Deconstruct principal cell electrogenic K+ secretion mechanics (basolateral Na+-K+ ATPase, apical ROMK/BK channels), evaluating how aldosterone, dietary K+, alkalosis, high tubular flow, and luminal anions regulate secretion","batch":3,"stem":"A 60-year-old man with heart failure is treated with a drug that blocks Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransport in the thick ascending limb. Two weeks later his serum potassium is 2.9 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L) and bicarbonate is 32 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L). Which factor most directly increases potassium secretion by his principal cells?","choices":{"A":"High tubular flow that keeps luminal potassium low","B":"Reduced sodium delivery to the distal nephron","C":"Acidemia that inhibits the basolateral sodium pump","D":"Lower plasma aldosterone acting on principal cells","E":"Intracellular magnesium plugging apical potassium channels"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Loop diuretics deliver more fluid and Na+ to the late distal tubule and collecting duct. High tubular flow carries away secreted K+, keeping the luminal K+ concentration low and the gradient for ROMK and flow-activated BK channel secretion steep; the added Na+ entry through ENaC makes the lumen more negative, and volume contraction raises aldosterone. Sodium delivery to the distal nephron increases rather than decreases with a loop diuretic. The high bicarbonate points to a metabolic alkalosis, and acidemia would reduce rather than promote K+ secretion. Aldosterone rises with diuretic-induced volume contraction. Intracellular magnesium blocks ROMK and limits secretion, so its loss in hypomagnesemia, not its presence, raises K+ excretion. Sources: Exam 2 lecture notes (SDL 17, principal cell K+ secretion and its regulators); Costanzo Physiology Ch. 6 (flow rate, aldosterone, and acid-base effects on distal K+ secretion).","boardPrep":"Step 1: loop and thiazide diuretics cause hypokalemia through increased distal flow and Na+ delivery plus secondary hyperaldosteronism. COMLEX: pair them with a potassium-sparing agent or supplement when hypokalemia develops.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 -> Obj 2 — diuretic-induced volume contraction also maintains a metabolic alkalosis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"ex2t_sdl17_q3","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Analyze the molecular biophysics of intracellular magnesium gating on luminal ROMK channels, formulating a clinical rationale for why magnesium replacement is mandatory to correct refractory hypokalemia","batch":3,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman who drinks alcohol daily and takes a thiazide has a serum potassium of 2.7 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L) despite 3 days of oral potassium chloride. Her serum Mg2+ is 1.1 mg/dL (reference range: 1.5–2.0 mg/dL). Which mechanism best explains the refractory hypokalemia?","choices":{"A":"Impaired intestinal absorption of oral potassium salts","B":"Increased H+-K+-ATPase reabsorption in intercalated cells","C":"Reduced basolateral uptake of potassium into principal cells","D":"Aldosterone deficiency limiting distal sodium reabsorption","E":"Loss of cytosolic magnesium block of apical ROMK channels"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Cytosolic Mg2+ binds the inner mouth of ROMK and limits outward K+ flow from principal cells. In hypomagnesemia this block is lost, so K+ secretion continues despite supplementation, and the hypokalemia corrects only after magnesium is repleted. Oral potassium chloride is well absorbed. Increased H+-K+-ATPase activity would reabsorb K+ and raise serum potassium. Reduced basolateral uptake would lower secretion. Aldosterone deficiency causes hyperkalemia, and thiazide-induced volume contraction raises aldosterone. Sources: Exam 2 lecture notes (SDL 17, magnesium gating of ROMK); Costanzo Physiology Ch. 6 (hypomagnesemia, ROMK, and the need to replete Mg2+ before K+ corrects).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hypokalemia that resists potassium supplementation suggests coexisting hypomagnesemia, common with alcohol use, diuretics, and proton pump inhibitors. COMLEX: check and replace magnesium first.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 -> Obj 3 — loop diuretics abolish the thick ascending limb voltage that drives paracellular magnesium reabsorption","isHighYield":true}],"18":[{"id":"sdl18_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze renal calcium handling, contrasting passive paracellular reabsorption in the PCT and thick ascending limb (driven by the lumen-positive potential) with active transcellular reabsorption in the distal tubule regulated by parathyroid hormone (PTH)","batch":1,"stem":"What fraction of the filtered Ca2+ load is reabsorbed in the proximal tubule?","choices":{"A":"About 25%, matching the fraction reabsorbed in the thick ascending limb","B":"About 8%, matching the fraction reabsorbed in the distal tubule","C":"About 99%, matching the fraction reabsorbed across the whole nephron","D":"About 40%, matching the fraction of Ca2+ that is protein-bound","E":"About 67%, matching the fraction of Na+ reabsorbed there"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Ca2+ reabsorption in the proximal tubule parallels Na+ reabsorption almost exactly, with about 67% of the filtered load reabsorbed passively, tightly coupled to Na+ transport; the thick ascending limb accounts for another 25%, and the distal tubule, where regulation actually occurs, reabsorbs only about 8%.","boardPrep":"Step 1: memorize Ca2+ handling by segment the same way as Na+ handling, since the two ions are coupled everywhere except the distal tubule.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 -> Obj 1 — passive paracellular Ca2+ reabsorption versus the PTH-regulated active transport in the distal tubule","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze renal calcium handling, contrasting passive paracellular reabsorption in the PCT and thick ascending limb (driven by the lumen-positive potential) with active transcellular reabsorption in the distal tubule regulated by parathyroid hormone (PTH)","batch":1,"stem":"What drives paracellular Ca2+ reabsorption in the thick ascending limb?","choices":{"A":"A dedicated basolateral Ca2+-ATPase pump unique to the thick ascending limb","B":"Direct cotransport of Ca2+ with Na+ through the apical NKCC2 transporter","C":"A steep transcellular Ca2+ concentration gradient maintained by calbindin","D":"The lumen-positive potential generated by the Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransporter","E":"PTH-stimulated active transport identical to that of the distal tubule"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransporter in the thick ascending limb generates a lumen-positive transepithelial potential; because positive charge repels positive charge, this potential drives divalent cations such as Ca2+ (and Mg2+) through the paracellular pathway, not through any dedicated transcellular pump. PTH-stimulated, cAMP-mediated Ca2+ reabsorption is the distal tubule mechanism; reabsorption in the thick ascending limb is passive and paracellular.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: this shared lumen-positive driving force is exactly why loop diuretics, which abolish this potential, increase both Ca2+ and Mg2+ excretion together.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 -> Obj 1 — passive paracellular Ca2+ reabsorption versus the PTH-regulated active transport in the distal tubule","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze renal calcium handling, contrasting passive paracellular reabsorption in the PCT and thick ascending limb (driven by the lumen-positive potential) with active transcellular reabsorption in the distal tubule regulated by parathyroid hormone (PTH)","batch":1,"stem":"What makes Ca2+ reabsorption in the distal tubule different from the proximal tubule and thick ascending limb?","choices":{"A":"It becomes paracellular and remains coupled to Na+ reabsorption","B":"It nearly stops, since the distal tubule reabsorbs very little Ca2+","C":"It becomes actively transcellular and uncoupled from Na+ reabsorption","D":"It becomes coupled to phosphate reabsorption in the same segment","E":"It becomes regulated primarily by aldosterone"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Unlike the proximal tubule and thick ascending limb, where Ca2+ reabsorption is passive and parallels Na+ reabsorption, the distal tubule reabsorbs Ca2+ through an active, transcellular, PTH-regulated pathway that is uncoupled from Na+ handling; this uncoupling is why thiazide diuretics increase Ca2+ reabsorption while decreasing Na+ reabsorption in the same segment.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the distal tubule is the one nephron segment where Ca2+ and Na+ reabsorption move in opposite directions under thiazide therapy — a frequently tested exception.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 -> Obj 1 — passive paracellular Ca2+ reabsorption versus the PTH-regulated active transport in the distal tubule","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Deconstruct proximal tubular phosphate reabsorption via Na+-phosphate cotransport, analyzing the cellular mechanism of PTH-mediated inhibition (receptor binding, cAMP signaling, and transporter inhibition) causing phosphaturia","batch":1,"stem":"Where does the majority of filtered phosphate get reabsorbed?","choices":{"A":"The thick ascending limb, via a basolateral phosphate-chloride exchanger","B":"The distal tubule, via a PTH-independent active transport mechanism","C":"The collecting duct, via facilitated diffusion driven by ADH","D":"The proximal tubule, via an apical Na+-phosphate cotransporter","E":"The loop of Henle, via the same NKCC2 transporter used for Na+ reabsorption"},"correct":"D","explanation":"About 85% of the filtered phosphate load is reabsorbed in the proximal tubule (convoluted and straight segments combined) via an apical Na+-phosphate cotransporter; little to no reabsorption occurs beyond the proximal tubule, which is why PTH acting there is sufficient to control urinary phosphate excretion. The collecting duct is not a phosphate reabsorbing site, and ADH regulates water and urea permeability there, not phosphate transport.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: because reabsorption beyond the proximal tubule is negligible, any phosphate PTH prevents from being reabsorbed there is excreted, with no downstream segment able to compensate.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 -> Obj 2 — proximal Na+-phosphate cotransport and the cAMP cascade PTH uses to shut it off","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Deconstruct proximal tubular phosphate reabsorption via Na+-phosphate cotransport, analyzing the cellular mechanism of PTH-mediated inhibition (receptor binding, cAMP signaling, and transporter inhibition) causing phosphaturia","batch":1,"stem":"Why does proximal tubular phosphate reabsorption show a Tm-limited pattern, similar to glucose?","choices":{"A":"Because it is filtered at a fixed rate that does not change with plasma phosphate concentration","B":"Because it is carried by a saturable Na+-phosphate cotransporter with finite capacity","C":"Because its rate is set by the glomerular filtration rate with no carrier involved","D":"Because it is reabsorbed once plasma phosphate exceeds a threshold value","E":"Because it is cotransported with glucose through the same luminal carrier"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Like glucose, phosphate reabsorption depends on a specific carrier protein, the Na+-phosphate cotransporter, whose finite number of binding sites can become saturated; once transport maximum is reached, additional filtered phosphate cannot be reabsorbed and is excreted, producing the same Tm-limited kinetics seen with glucose.","boardPrep":"Step 1: recognizing shared Tm-limited kinetics between phosphate and glucose is a useful shortcut for predicting how any saturable proximal transporter will behave.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 -> Obj 2 — proximal Na+-phosphate cotransport and the cAMP cascade PTH uses to shut it off","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Deconstruct proximal tubular phosphate reabsorption via Na+-phosphate cotransport, analyzing the cellular mechanism of PTH-mediated inhibition (receptor binding, cAMP signaling, and transporter inhibition) causing phosphaturia","batch":1,"stem":"By what intracellular pathway does PTH inhibit proximal tubular phosphate reabsorption?","choices":{"A":"PTH binds a basolateral receptor and activates adenylyl cyclase to generate cAMP","B":"PTH binds a luminal receptor and blocks the Na+-phosphate cotransporter pore","C":"PTH binds a basolateral receptor and couples to Gi to lower cAMP","D":"PTH binds a luminal receptor and triggers internalization of aquaporin channels","E":"PTH binds a basolateral receptor and directly opens apical calcium channels"},"correct":"A","explanation":"PTH binds a basolateral receptor coupled via a Gs protein to adenylyl cyclase; the resulting cAMP activates protein kinases that phosphorylate luminal membrane components, ultimately inhibiting the Na+-phosphate cotransporter and producing phosphaturia, with excess cAMP itself excreted into the urine as a marker of PTH action.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: urinary cAMP and phosphate excretion are the classic laboratory hallmarks of PTH bioactivity, both driven by this same Gs-adenylyl cyclase-cAMP cascade.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 -> Obj 2 — proximal Na+-phosphate cotransport and the cAMP cascade PTH uses to shut it off","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Evaluate renal magnesium handling, analyzing why the thick ascending limb is the primary site of reabsorption (paracellular pathway driven by the lumen-positive potential) and predicting the hypomagnesemic effect of loop diuretics","batch":1,"stem":"What fraction of filtered magnesium is reabsorbed by the thick ascending limb?","choices":{"A":"About 30%, making the proximal tubule the primary reabsorptive site","B":"About 95%, accounting for nearly complete filtered magnesium reabsorption","C":"About 60%, making it the primary site of Mg2+ reabsorption","D":"About 5%, a minor contribution compared with the proximal tubule","E":"About 80%, smaller than the distal tubule's larger contribution"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Unlike Na+ and Ca2+, whose primary reabsorptive site is the proximal tubule, Mg2+ handling is dominated by the thick ascending limb, which reabsorbs about 60% of the filtered load; the proximal tubule reabsorbs a comparatively small 30%, and the distal tubule reabsorbs only about 5%, together leaving roughly 5% for excretion (Costanzo; Guyton gives about 10% to 15% of filtered Mg2+ excreted).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the thick ascending limb being the primary Mg2+ reabsorptive site, unlike its secondary role for Na+ and Ca2+, is the single most tested fact about renal Mg2+ handling.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 -> Obj 3 — why the thick ascending limb dominates Mg2+ reabsorption and why loop diuretics waste it","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Evaluate renal magnesium handling, analyzing why the thick ascending limb is the primary site of reabsorption (paracellular pathway driven by the lumen-positive potential) and predicting the hypomagnesemic effect of loop diuretics","batch":1,"stem":"In a micropuncture study, about one-third of the filtered Na+ and of the filtered Ca2+ remain in tubular fluid at the end of the proximal tubule. If proximal Mg2+ handling follows its usual pattern, about what fraction of the filtered Mg2+ remains in tubular fluid at that point?","choices":{"A":"About 70%","B":"About 33%","C":"About 5%","D":"About 10%","E":"About 90%"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Costanzo notes that the proximal tubule reabsorbs only 30% of the filtered Mg2+, a small share compared with the 67% reabsorbed for Na+ and Ca2+, so about 70% remains at the end of the proximal tubule; the thick ascending limb then reabsorbs 60%, the distal tubule 5%, and 5% is excreted. 33% would apply if Mg2+ tracked Na+ and Ca2+ proximally. 10% is what remains after the thick ascending limb, and 5% is the fraction finally excreted. 90% is the cumulative fraction reabsorbed by the end of the thick ascending limb, not what remains after the proximal tubule.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Mg2+: about 80% filterable; proximal tubule 30%, thick ascending limb 60% (paracellular, lumen-positive potential), distal tubule 5%, about 5% excreted. COMLEX: Because the thick ascending limb is the main site, loop diuretics are a common cause of renal Mg2+ wasting.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 1 — Ca2+ instead follows Na+, with 67% reabsorbed proximally","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Evaluate renal magnesium handling, analyzing why the thick ascending limb is the primary site of reabsorption (paracellular pathway driven by the lumen-positive potential) and predicting the hypomagnesemic effect of loop diuretics","batch":1,"stem":"Why may loop diuretics lead to hypomagnesemia?","choices":{"A":"They directly inhibit a basolateral Mg2+-ATPase pump in the thick ascending limb","B":"They block NKCC2, removing the potential that drives Mg2+ reabsorption","C":"They increase PTH secretion, which paradoxically suppresses Mg2+ reabsorption","D":"They stimulate a luminal Mg2+ secretory channel in the collecting duct","E":"They inhibit Mg2+ absorption directly at the level of the intestine"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Loop diuretics inhibit the apical Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransporter of the thick ascending limb, which eliminates the lumen-positive potential that normally drives paracellular Mg2+ (and Ca2+) reabsorption in that segment; because the thick ascending limb is the primary site of Mg2+ reabsorption, this loss may lead to hypomagnesemia. Mg2+ is handled by reabsorption, about 60% of it in the thick ascending limb, and no collecting duct Mg2+ secretory channel is involved.","boardPrep":"Step 1: pair this with the parallel loop-diuretic effect on Ca2+ excretion, since both ions depend on the same lumen-positive potential in the same segment.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 -> Obj 3 — why the thick ascending limb dominates Mg2+ reabsorption and why loop diuretics waste it","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q10","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze renal calcium handling, contrasting passive paracellular reabsorption in the PCT and thick ascending limb (driven by the lumen-positive potential) with active transcellular reabsorption in the distal tubule regulated by parathyroid hormone (PTH)","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman receives a large IV saline bolus for volume resuscitation. Which additional urinary finding would be expected from this fluid load, beyond the increase in Na+ excretion?","choices":{"A":"Increased Ca2+ excretion, since proximal Ca2+ reabsorption tracks Na+ closely","B":"Decreased Ca2+ excretion, since ECF expansion independently stimulates PTH release","C":"No change in Ca2+ excretion, since Ca2+ handling is independent of Na+ handling","D":"Increased phosphate reabsorption, since PTH secretion falls in response to volume expansion","E":"Decreased Mg2+ excretion, since thick ascending limb reabsorption rises with ECF expansion"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Because proximal tubular Ca2+ reabsorption is tightly coupled to Na+ reabsorption, any maneuver that inhibits proximal Na+ reabsorption, such as ECF volume expansion from a saline bolus, simultaneously inhibits Ca2+ reabsorption, producing parallel increases in both Na+ and Ca2+ excretion.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: this coupling explains why volume-expanded states (and volume-contracted states in the opposite direction) move urinary Ca2+ excretion in the same direction as Na+ excretion, outside of the distal tubule.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 Obj 1 — proximal Ca2+ reabsorption is coupled to Na+ reabsorption","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q11","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze renal calcium handling, contrasting passive paracellular reabsorption in the PCT and thick ascending limb (driven by the lumen-positive potential) with active transcellular reabsorption in the distal tubule regulated by parathyroid hormone (PTH)","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old man has a markedly elevated serum Ca2+ from bony metastases. His urinary Ca2+ excretion is appropriately increased. Which receptor-mediated mechanism in the thick ascending limb explains this response?","choices":{"A":"A Ca2+-sensing receptor detects hypercalcemia and directly opens paracellular Ca2+ channels","B":"A Ca2+-sensing receptor detects hypercalcemia and inhibits NKCC2 directly","C":"A Ca2+-sensing receptor detects hypercalcemia and stimulates PTH release from the parathyroid","D":"A Ca2+-sensing receptor detects hypercalcemia and increases distal tubule Na+ reabsorption","E":"A Ca2+-sensing receptor detects hypercalcemia and blocks the apical H+-K+ ATPase"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A Ca2+-sensing receptor in the thick ascending limb detects elevated plasma Ca2+ and inhibits the Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransporter; this eliminates the lumen-positive potential that normally drives paracellular Ca2+ reabsorption, decreasing thick ascending limb Ca2+ reabsorption and appropriately increasing urinary Ca2+ excretion as a protective response to hypercalcemia.","boardPrep":"Step 1: this is the same Ca2+-sensing receptor that is defective in familial hypocalciuric hypercalcemia, where the protective increase in Ca2+ excretion fails to occur.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 Obj 1 — the thick ascending limb Ca2+-sensing receptor inhibits NKCC2 in hypercalcemia","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q12","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze renal calcium handling, contrasting passive paracellular reabsorption in the PCT and thick ascending limb (driven by the lumen-positive potential) with active transcellular reabsorption in the distal tubule regulated by parathyroid hormone (PTH)","batch":2,"stem":"A 30-year-old asymptomatic woman is found to have mild hypercalcemia and inappropriately low urinary Ca2+ excretion, a pattern present in several first-degree relatives. Genetic testing reveals a loss-of-function mutation in the thick ascending limb calcium-sensing receptor. Which mechanism explains her hypocalciuria despite hypercalcemia?","choices":{"A":"Her defective receptor over-senses hypercalcemia, paradoxically increasing Ca2+ excretion further","B":"Her defective receptor suppresses PTH secretion, which independently lowers Ca2+ reabsorption","C":"Her defective receptor fails to sense hypercalcemia, so Ca2+ reabsorption continues unchecked","D":"Her defective receptor blocks distal tubule Ca2+ reabsorption, redirecting it to the proximal tubule","E":"Her defective receptor stimulates additional Na+-phosphate cotransport in the proximal tubule"},"correct":"C","explanation":"This is familial hypocalciuric hypercalcemia: a loss-of-function mutation in the thick ascending limb Ca2+-sensing receptor prevents hypercalcemia from inhibiting NKCC2, so the lumen-positive potential and paracellular Ca2+ reabsorption persist despite an elevated serum Ca2+, producing hypocalciuria (low urinary Ca2+) together with hypercalcemia, the two findings that give the syndrome its name.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: the combination of hypercalcemia with inappropriately low urinary Ca2+ excretion should immediately suggest FHH rather than primary hyperparathyroidism.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 Obj 1 — defective Ca2+-sensing receptor in familial hypocalciuric hypercalcemia","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q13","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Deconstruct proximal tubular phosphate reabsorption via Na+-phosphate cotransport, analyzing the cellular mechanism of PTH-mediated inhibition (receptor binding, cAMP signaling, and transporter inhibition) causing phosphaturia","batch":2,"stem":"A 12-year-old girl has hypocalcemia with markedly elevated serum PTH. Urinary cAMP and phosphate excretion are both low despite the elevated PTH. Which defect best explains this combination?","choices":{"A":"A defect in PTH secretion that leaves her circulating PTH low despite hypocalcemia","B":"A defect in the renal PTH receptor-Gs protein-adenylyl cyclase complex causing PTH resistance","C":"A defect in the Na+-phosphate cotransporter making it resistant to inhibition","D":"A defect in renal 1-alpha-hydroxylase that lowers active vitamin D production selectively","E":"A defect in the parathyroid calcium-sensing receptor that raises the PTH secretion setpoint"},"correct":"B","explanation":"This is pseudohypoparathyroidism: a defect in the renal PTH receptor, Gs protein, or adenylyl cyclase complex makes the kidney resistant to PTH despite elevated circulating hormone; because PTH signaling through this cAMP cascade never occurs, urinary cAMP and phosphate excretion remain low even though PTH itself is appropriately elevated in response to hypocalcemia.","boardPrep":"Step 1: low urinary cAMP despite high PTH is the key laboratory clue that localizes the defect to the renal receptor-signaling complex, not to PTH synthesis or secretion.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 Obj 2 — PTH receptor, Gs protein, or adenylyl cyclase defect in pseudohypoparathyroidism","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q14","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Deconstruct proximal tubular phosphate reabsorption via Na+-phosphate cotransport, analyzing the cellular mechanism of PTH-mediated inhibition (receptor binding, cAMP signaling, and transporter inhibition) causing phosphaturia","batch":2,"stem":"A 65-year-old man with stage 4 chronic kidney disease has elevated PTH and elevated serum phosphate despite the expected phosphaturic action of PTH. Which explanation accounts for this apparent mismatch?","choices":{"A":"His elevated PTH has become biologically inactive due to prolonged parathyroid gland stimulation","B":"His remaining nephrons have become resistant to the PTH-mediated phosphaturic effect","C":"His reduced nephron mass severely limits how much phosphate the kidneys can filter and excrete","D":"His elevated phosphate mainly reflects increased phosphate release from bone","E":"His Na+-phosphate cotransporter density has paradoxically increased in response to elevated PTH"},"correct":"C","explanation":"PTH inhibits the Na+-phosphate cotransporter in proximal tubule cells and causes phosphaturia. With far fewer functioning nephrons in advanced chronic kidney disease, the filtered phosphate that can be excreted is limited, so phosphate rises and secondary hyperparathyroidism develops; reduced nephron mass, not receptor resistance, is the limiting factor. The hormone is not inactive: in chronic kidney disease the high PTH still acts on bone, causing renal osteodystrophy, and it rises in response to phosphate retention and hypocalcemia.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: secondary hyperparathyroidism in chronic kidney disease reflects an appropriate hormonal response overwhelmed by reduced nephron number, distinct from the receptor-level resistance of pseudohypoparathyroidism.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 Obj 2 — PTH inhibition of Na+-phosphate cotransport causes phosphaturia","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q15","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Deconstruct proximal tubular phosphate reabsorption via Na+-phosphate cotransport, analyzing the cellular mechanism of PTH-mediated inhibition (receptor binding, cAMP signaling, and transporter inhibition) causing phosphaturia","batch":2,"stem":"A 42-year-old woman comes to the clinic because of 2 months of tingling around her mouth and muscle cramps in her hands. She has no history of neck surgery or radiation. Tapping over the facial nerve produces twitching of the upper lip. Serum calcium is 6.9 mg/dL (reference range: 8.4–10.2 mg/dL), phosphorus is 5.8 mg/dL (reference range: 2.5–4.5 mg/dL), magnesium is 2.0 mg/dL (reference range: 1.7–2.2 mg/dL), and parathyroid hormone is 5 pg/mL (reference range: 10–65 pg/mL). She is given an infusion of exogenous parathyroid hormone, after which her urinary cAMP excretion rises appropriately. Which conclusion about her renal parathyroid hormone signaling is best supported by this response?","choices":{"A":"Her basolateral PTH receptor is defective, but a separate luminal pathway compensates for it","B":"Her Na+-phosphate cotransporter is already maximally inhibited, independent of any cAMP signal","C":"Her renal tubules are unresponsive to PTH, similar to pseudohypoparathyroidism","D":"Her basolateral PTH receptor and adenylyl cyclase pathway remain intact, functioning normally","E":"Her low baseline PTH mainly reflects ongoing bone-level hormone resistance"},"correct":"D","explanation":"A normal rise in urinary cAMP after exogenous PTH administration demonstrates that the renal basolateral receptor, its coupled Gs protein, and adenylyl cyclase are all functioning normally; this response argues against pseudohypoparathyroidism and points instead toward deficient PTH secretion, that is hypoparathyroidism, as the cause of her hypocalcemia with low PTH. Hormone resistance, as in pseudohypoparathyroidism, raises PTH rather than lowering it.","boardPrep":"Step 1: urinary cAMP is a hallmark of PTH action that is decreased in pseudohypoparathyroidism; using an exogenous PTH challenge to separate hypoparathyroidism from renal PTH resistance is a standard clinical approach.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 Obj 2 — PTH receptor, Gs protein, adenylyl cyclase, and urinary cAMP as a marker of PTH action","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q16","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Evaluate renal magnesium handling, analyzing why the thick ascending limb is the primary site of reabsorption (paracellular pathway driven by the lumen-positive potential) and predicting the hypomagnesemic effect of loop diuretics","batch":2,"stem":"A 50-year-old man has a GFR of 100 mL/min and a plasma Mg2+ concentration of 2.0 mg/dL (reference range: 1.5–2.0 mg/dL); 20% of his plasma Mg2+ is bound to proteins. Assuming normal overall tubular reabsorption of Mg2+, what is his approximate urinary Mg2+ excretion rate?","choices":{"A":"0.08 mg/min","B":"0.10 mg/min","C":"1.52 mg/min","D":"0.48 mg/min","E":"1.12 mg/min"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Only the unbound 80% of plasma Mg2+ is filterable, so the filtered load is 100 mL/min × 0.016 mg/mL = 1.6 mg/min; overall reabsorption of Mg2+ by the nephron is 95%, leaving 5% excreted, or 0.08 mg/min. 0.10 mg/min takes 5% of 2.0 mg/min and ignores protein binding. 1.52 mg/min is the amount reabsorbed rather than excreted. 0.48 mg/min is the 30% reabsorbed by the proximal tubule, and 1.12 mg/min is the 70% still in the lumen after the proximal tubule, before the thick ascending limb reabsorbs its 60%.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Filtered load = GFR × filterable plasma concentration; Mg2+ is 80% filterable, and the nephron reabsorbs about 95%, mostly in the thick ascending limb (60%). COMLEX: When the thick ascending limb is blocked by a loop diuretic, much of that 60% is lost, raising Mg2+ excretion well above this baseline.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 1 — filtered load and excretion rate follow the same clearance arithmetic","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q17","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Evaluate renal magnesium handling, analyzing why the thick ascending limb is the primary site of reabsorption (paracellular pathway driven by the lumen-positive potential) and predicting the hypomagnesemic effect of loop diuretics","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man has persistent renal magnesium wasting after nephrotoxic injury to his renal tubules. Which loss of function accounts for the largest share of his Mg2+ wasting?","choices":{"A":"Loss of proximal tubule Mg2+ reabsorption, which normally handles the majority","B":"Loss of distal tubule Mg2+ reabsorption, the main regulated site for Mg2+","C":"Excessive Mg2+ secretion by the collecting duct into the tubular lumen","D":"Loss of collecting duct Mg2+ reabsorption, the major site for fine regulation","E":"Loss of thick ascending limb Mg2+ reabsorption, the major site at about 60%"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The thick ascending limb is the major site of Mg2+ reabsorption (about 60% of the filtered load, driven by the lumen-positive potential), compared with about 30% in the proximal tubule and about 5% in the distal tubule. Loss of reabsorption in the thick ascending limb therefore accounts for the largest share of urinary Mg2+ wasting.","boardPrep":"Step 1: because the thick ascending limb is the major Mg2+ reabsorptive site, anything that impairs it, as loop diuretics do by eliminating the lumen-positive potential, wastes Mg2+.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 Obj 3 — the thick ascending limb is the major site (about 60%) of Mg2+ reabsorption","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q18","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Evaluate renal magnesium handling, analyzing why the thick ascending limb is the primary site of reabsorption (paracellular pathway driven by the lumen-positive potential) and predicting the hypomagnesemic effect of loop diuretics","batch":2,"stem":"A 50-year-old woman is switched from hydrochlorothiazide to furosemide for refractory edema. Which combined change in her urinary calcium and magnesium excretion would be expected after this switch?","choices":{"A":"Decreased Ca2+ excretion and increased Mg2+ excretion, since furosemide would affect the distal tubule directly","B":"Increased Ca2+ excretion and decreased Mg2+ excretion, since furosemide selectively spares magnesium reabsorption","C":"No change in Ca2+ excretion and increased Mg2+ excretion, since calcium handling is unaffected by loop diuretics","D":"Increased Ca2+ excretion and increased Mg2+ excretion, since furosemide removes their shared driving force","E":"Decreased Ca2+ excretion and decreased Mg2+ excretion, since furosemide mimics thiazide's distal action"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Thiazides act on the distal tubule and increase Ca2+ reabsorption while sparing the thick ascending limb, but furosemide acts on the thick ascending limb itself, eliminating the lumen-positive potential that drives both Ca2+ and Mg2+ reabsorption there; switching from a thiazide to a loop diuretic therefore reverses the calcium effect (increasing rather than decreasing excretion) while also increasing magnesium excretion.","boardPrep":"COMLEX: this opposite effect on urinary Ca2+ is exactly why thiazides are used for idiopathic hypercalciuria while loop diuretics are used to treat hypercalcemia.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 Obj 3 — loop diuretics abolish the lumen-positive potential that drives Ca2+ and Mg2+ reabsorption, unlike thiazides","isHighYield":true},{"id":"ex2t_sdl18_q1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze renal calcium handling, contrasting passive paracellular reabsorption in the PCT and thick ascending limb (driven by the lumen-positive potential) with active transcellular reabsorption in the distal tubule regulated by parathyroid hormone (PTH)","batch":3,"stem":"A 36-year-old woman with a parathyroid adenoma has a serum calcium of 11.6 mg/dL (reference range: 8.4–10.2 mg/dL) and phosphate of 2.1 mg/dL (reference range: 3.0–4.5 mg/dL). Her parathyroid hormone level is 145 pg/mL (reference range: 10–65 pg/mL). Hormone-stimulated calcium reabsorption occurs mainly in which nephron segment?","choices":{"A":"The proximal convoluted tubule","B":"The distal convoluted tubule","C":"The thin descending limb of Henle","D":"The cortical collecting duct","E":"The inner medullary collecting duct"},"correct":"B","explanation":"PTH acts on the distal convoluted tubule through PTH1 receptors and cAMP to increase transcellular Ca2+ reabsorption: apical TRPV5 entry, calbindin buffering, and basolateral Na+-Ca2+ exchange and Ca2+-ATPase. This segment reabsorbs a small fraction of filtered calcium (about 8%) but is the main site of hormonal fine-tuning. The proximal convoluted tubule reabsorbs about 67% of filtered calcium passively, following sodium and water. The thin descending limb is permeable to water but transports little calcium. The cortical and inner medullary collecting ducts reabsorb little calcium. Sources: Exam 2 lecture notes (SDL 18, renal calcium handling); Costanzo Physiology Ch. 6 (segmental Ca2+ reabsorption and PTH action in the distal tubule).","boardPrep":"Step 1: PTH raises distal calcium reabsorption, lowers proximal phosphate reabsorption, and stimulates renal 1-alpha-hydroxylase. COMLEX: in primary hyperparathyroidism, hypercalciuria can still occur because the filtered calcium load rises.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 -> Obj 1 — thiazides also enhance calcium reabsorption in the distal convoluted tubule","isHighYield":true},{"id":"ex2t_sdl18_q2","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Deconstruct proximal tubular phosphate reabsorption via Na+-phosphate cotransport, analyzing the cellular mechanism of PTH-mediated inhibition (receptor binding, cAMP signaling, and transporter inhibition) causing phosphaturia","batch":3,"stem":"A 52-year-old man with primary hyperparathyroidism has bone pain, a serum calcium of 11.2 mg/dL (reference range: 8.4–10.2 mg/dL), and a serum phosphate of 2.0 mg/dL (reference range: 3.0–4.5 mg/dL). His urinary cyclic AMP excretion is increased. Which proximal tubular event best explains his phosphate wasting?","choices":{"A":"Inhibition of basolateral Na+-K+-ATPase pumps","B":"Blockade of apical Na+-H+ exchange by cyclic AMP","C":"Reduced filtration of phosphate at the glomerulus","D":"Secretion of phosphate by the distal convoluted tubule","E":"Retrieval of apical Na+-phosphate cotransporters"},"correct":"E","explanation":"About 85% of filtered phosphate is reabsorbed in the proximal tubule by apical Na+-phosphate cotransporters. PTH binds proximal receptors, raises cAMP (which spills into the urine as nephrogenous cAMP), and triggers endocytic retrieval of these cotransporters from the brush border, lowering the transport maximum and causing phosphaturia. PTH's phosphaturic effect is not mediated by the Na+-K+-ATPase. PTH does inhibit Na+-H+ exchange, but that effect lowers bicarbonate reabsorption rather than phosphate reabsorption. Low plasma phosphate reduces the filtered load, which would lower rather than raise urinary loss. The distal nephron does not secrete phosphate. Sources: Exam 2 lecture notes (SDL 18, proximal phosphate reabsorption and PTH); Costanzo Physiology Ch. 6 (PTH, cAMP, and inhibition of Na+-phosphate cotransport).","boardPrep":"Step 1: PTH lowers serum phosphate through phosphaturia, and FGF23 does the same by acting on the same proximal cotransporters. COMLEX: urinary cAMP rises with PTH action and is used to distinguish PTH resistance in pseudohypoparathyroidism.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 -> Obj 3 — filtered phosphate becomes the main titratable acid buffer in the urine","isHighYield":true},{"id":"ex2t_sdl18_q3","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Evaluate renal magnesium handling, analyzing why the thick ascending limb is the primary site of reabsorption (paracellular pathway driven by the lumen-positive potential) and predicting the hypomagnesemic effect of loop diuretics","batch":3,"stem":"A 70-year-old man with pulmonary edema receives several days of an intravenous drug that blocks Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransport. His serum magnesium falls to 1.1 mg/dL (reference range: 1.5–2.0 mg/dL) while his urinary magnesium excretion is high. Loss of which driving force best explains the magnesium wasting?","choices":{"A":"The lumen-negative voltage of the collecting duct","B":"The sodium gradient that powers apical TRPM6 entry","C":"The parathyroid hormone signal acting on distal cells","D":"The lumen-positive voltage that repels divalent cations","E":"The osmotic gradient driving paracellular water flow"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The thick ascending limb reabsorbs about 60% of filtered Mg2+, the largest share of any segment, through paracellular claudin-16 and claudin-19 pathways. The driving force is the lumen-positive voltage generated by NKCC2 transport and ROMK K+ recycling, which pushes divalent cations out of the lumen. A loop diuretic stops NKCC2, the voltage collapses, and Mg2+ and Ca2+ are lost in the urine. The collecting duct reabsorbs little magnesium. TRPM6 in the distal convoluted tubule handles a smaller fraction and depends on membrane voltage rather than a sodium gradient. PTH signaling is not blocked by the drug. The thick ascending limb is water impermeable, so solvent drag does not carry Mg2+ there. Sources: Exam 2 lecture notes (SDL 18, renal magnesium handling and loop diuretics); Costanzo Physiology Ch. 6 (thick ascending limb lumen-positive potential and Mg2+ reabsorption).","boardPrep":"Step 1: loop diuretics cause hypomagnesemia and hypercalciuria through loss of the thick ascending limb voltage, while thiazides cause hypomagnesemia but hypocalciuria. COMLEX: hypomagnesemia can cause refractory hypokalemia and hypocalcemia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 -> Obj 3 — loop diuretics bind the Cl- site of NKCC2 and abolish the lumen-positive potential","isHighYield":true},{"id":"grid_sdl18_q01","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Deconstruct proximal tubular phosphate reabsorption via Na+-phosphate cotransport, analyzing the cellular mechanism of PTH-mediated inhibition (receptor binding, cAMP signaling, and transporter inhibition) causing phosphaturia","batch":4,"stem":"A 14-year-old girl is brought to the physician because of 2 months of muscle cramps and tingling around her mouth and in her fingers. She has never had neck surgery or radiation, eats a varied diet that includes dairy products, and takes no medications. She is 142 cm (4 ft 8 in) tall (below the 3rd percentile) and has a short neck; her fourth metacarpals are short. Tapping anterior to the ear causes twitching of the facial muscles, and inflating a blood pressure cuff on her arm produces carpal spasm. Serum magnesium and creatinine concentrations are within the reference ranges. Which of the following sets of laboratory findings is most likely in this patient?","grid":{"headers":["Serum Ca²⁺","Serum phosphate","PTH","Urinary cAMP"]},"choices":{"A":["↓","↑","↓","↓"],"B":["↓","↓","↑","↑"],"C":["↑","↓","↑","↑"],"D":["↑","↓","↓","↑"],"E":["↓","↑","↑","↓"]},"correct":"E","explanation":"Hypocalcemic tetany in a short girl with a short neck and short fourth metacarpals (Albright hereditary osteodystrophy) is pseudohypoparathyroidism, so the pattern (↓ serum Ca²⁺, ↑ serum phosphate, ↑ PTH, ↓ urinary cAMP) is correct. A defective Gs protein leaves kidney and bone resistant to PTH: the proximal tubule does not reduce phosphate reabsorption, Ca²⁺ is not mobilized, and 1α-hydroxylation of vitamin D is not stimulated, so Ca²⁺ falls and phosphate rises. Low Ca²⁺ drives PTH secretion, but without adenylyl cyclase activation the tubule generates little cAMP, so urinary cAMP is low. The pattern (↓ serum Ca²⁺, ↑ serum phosphate, ↓ PTH, ↓ urinary cAMP) is hypoparathyroidism, usually after thyroid or parathyroid surgery: the same Ca²⁺ and phosphate, but PTH is low and the skeletal phenotype is absent. The pattern (↓ serum Ca²⁺, ↓ serum phosphate, ↑ PTH, ↑ urinary cAMP) is vitamin D deficiency, in which secondary hyperparathyroidism acts on a normal kidney, raising urinary cAMP and phosphate excretion. The pattern (↑ serum Ca²⁺, ↓ serum phosphate, ↑ PTH, ↑ urinary cAMP) is primary hyperparathyroidism. The pattern (↑ serum Ca²⁺, ↓ serum phosphate, ↓ PTH, ↑ urinary cAMP) is humoral hypercalcemia of malignancy, in which PTH-related peptide acts on the PTH receptor and raises urinary cAMP while suppressing PTH.","boardPrep":"Step 1: pseudohypoparathyroidism has the chemistry of hypoparathyroidism (low Ca²⁺, high phosphate) with a high PTH, and exogenous PTH produces no phosphaturia and no rise in urinary cAMP. COMLEX: in a hypocalcemic patient, check magnesium first, because hypomagnesemia impairs PTH secretion and action.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 Obj 1 — PTH stimulates active transcellular Ca²⁺ reabsorption in the distal tubule, a step lost when the kidney is PTH-resistant","isHighYield":true}]};
